/* THE STUDY OF EVERY BEDTIME STORY. Built from the 147 picture-book pages in
   www/stories by tools/build-story-study.js: the story itself, the Traveler of
   the night, the Feeling Moment and its Five Stones, Remember This, the
   questions for the child and the prompts for the parent. The timeline's Study
   Library loads this only when someone opens an In-Depth Study. Keyed by the
   library's story id (b8-w3-n1). Rebuild it when a story page changes. */
window.LST_STUDY={"b8-w1-n1":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father’s people lived far away from home in a land called Babylon. Their beautiful city, Jerusalem, was empty, and Heavenly Father’s temple was gone. The people missed home so very much.","One day, a mighty king named Cyrus stood up and made a wonderful announcement. “Heavenly Father has helped me,” he said. “His people may go home and build Him a temple again!”","The people could hardly believe their happy ears. King Cyrus even gave back the special gold and silver bowls that belonged in Heavenly Father’s temple. Families packed their bags with big smiles and started the long journey home. Heavenly Father had promised long ago that they could return — and He kept His promise. When Heavenly Father makes a promise, He always keeps it."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because going home took trust that Heavenly Father would lead the way. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s family just moved to a brand-new house, and the new room felt strange and a little scary. But Scout took a big breath, remembered Heavenly Father leads His children, and went exploring — finding a friendly neighbor’s dog and the coziest reading corner. “Heavenly Father helped me find good things here!” Just like the people trusted Him on the road home, Scout trusted Him in a brand-new place.","feel":"Let’s pack a pretend bag! Reach out and “grab” your favorite things and tuck them inside. Now sling it over your shoulder and take three big marching steps — we’re going home! Can you feel how happy a big journey feels?","stones":[["SPOT","Cyrus and the people felt hopeful and excited to go home."],["NAME","That happy, can’t-wait feeling is called excited — and a little bit nervous, too."],["FIND","When have you felt excited and nervous at the very same time — like a first day somewhere new?"],["FORK","A nervous feeling can make us want to hide — or we can hold Heavenly Father’s hand and go bravely. The people chose to trust Him and go."],["CALM","Take one slow breath together and let it out slow. Say, “Heavenly Father is with me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father keeps His promises to YOU, just like He did for His people going home. When He asks you to do something good, you can trust Him with your whole heart. What is one thing you can trust Heavenly Father to help you with tomorrow?","ask":["“Who let Heavenly Father’s people go home?”","“What did the people go home to build?”"],"share":["When has Heavenly Father kept a promise to you, even after a long wait? Tell your child how it felt to finally see it come true.","Describe a time He softened someone’s heart to help your family in a way you didn’t expect.","Did a grandparent or ancestor make a long journey or a big move because they trusted Heavenly Father? Share where they were going and why."]},"b8-w1-n2":{"story":["When the people reached home, the temple ground was just dirt and rubble. But they were not sad for long — they were ready to build Heavenly Father’s house!","First, they set up an altar so they could pray and worship together. Then the builders came with big, heavy stones to lay the very first layer, called the foundation.","When the foundation was finished, the people had a giant celebration! Trumpets played and cymbals crashed. Everyone sang, “Heavenly Father is so good; His love lasts forever!”","Something tender happened too. The oldest people remembered the very first temple, long ago, and happy tears rolled down their cheeks. So there was singing AND crying, all mixed together, and Heavenly Father saw every bit of it. Big feelings are welcome when we worship Him."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she notices every kind of feeling. In Willow’s world today: at Grandma’s birthday, Willow saw Grandma cry — but they were happy tears! Instead of feeling worried, Willow climbed up for a hug and said, “Your happy tears are okay, Grandma.” Just like the people who laughed and cried while they praised Heavenly Father, Willow learned that joy and tears can live in the very same heart.","feel":"Let’s make a joyful noise! Clap your hands like clanging cymbals — crash, crash! Now hum a happy little “la-la-la.” Some feelings are so big they bring happy tears. Can you show me a happy face… and a teary face?","stones":[["SPOT","The people felt joyful, and some felt tender and teary too."],["NAME","That full-up, tender-happy feeling is called joy."],["FIND","When did something make you SO happy you almost cried, or felt full inside?"],["FORK","A big feeling can bubble over into a fuss — or we can let it out gently in a song or a hug. The people let theirs out in praise to Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Put a hand on your heart and hum one slow, happy note together."]],"remember":"Your feelings are a gift from Heavenly Father — even the big ones! It’s okay to feel happy and cry at the very same time. He loves your whole heart. When was a time you felt a really big feeling today?","ask":["“What did the people build first?”","“Why did some of the older people cry?”"],"share":["When has worship or a sacred moment brought happy tears to your eyes? Share what you were feeling.","Tell your child about a “foundation” moment in your family — a small beginning that grew into something you treasure.","Did a grandparent or ancestor help build or begin something for Heavenly Father — a chapel, a home, a family tradition? Describe it."]},"b8-w1-n3":{"story":["Building the temple was not always easy. Some unkind neighbors tried to make the people stop, and for a while the work grew quiet and slow.","But two of Heavenly Father’s prophets, Haggai and Zechariah, cheered the people on. “Keep going!” they said. “Heavenly Father is with you!” So the people picked up their tools again.","A new king named Darius found King Cyrus’s old promise in the royal books. “Let them build!” Darius said. “And give them whatever they need.” What wonderful news!","Tap, tap, tap — the hammers sang, and higher and higher the temple rose, until at last it was finished! The people threw a joyful party to give the whole temple to Heavenly Father. He had helped them finish what they started, even when it was hard."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he keeps going when things get hard. In Beau’s world today: Beau was building a tall block tower and it kept toppling — crash! Beau wanted to quit and cry. But he took a breath, said a little prayer, and tried again, stacking slower and steadier, until the tower stood tall. “I didn’t give up!” Just like the people who finished Heavenly Father’s temple, Beau finished the hard thing with Heavenly Father’s help.","feel":"Let’s build together! Stack your hands one on top of the other, higher and higher — tap, tap, tap. Now throw your arms up high: “It’s finished!” Doesn’t it feel good to finish something you worked hard on?","stones":[["SPOT","At first the builders felt discouraged when others tried to stop them."],["NAME","That heavy, want-to-quit feeling is called discouraged."],["FIND","When have you felt like giving up because something was too hard?"],["FORK","Feeling discouraged can make us stop — or we can take a breath, ask for help, and try again. The builders kept going and trusted Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Breathe in slow, then say together, “I can try again.”"]],"remember":"When something good is hard, Heavenly Father helps you keep going! Maybe you’re learning to tie your shoes or clean your whole room. Don’t give up — He is right there with you. What is something hard you can keep trying, even when it’s tricky?","ask":["“Who told the people to keep building?”","“How did everyone feel when the temple was finished?”"],"share":["When did you finish something hard because you trusted Heavenly Father to help? Tell the story.","Who were the “Haggai and Zechariah” voices that cheered you on when you wanted to quit?","Did a grandparent or ancestor persevere through hard times to finish something important? Share their grit and faith."]},"b8-w1-n4":{"story":["There was a good man named Ezra who loved Heavenly Father with all his heart. More than almost anything, Ezra loved to read Heavenly Father’s holy words, the scriptures.","Ezra didn’t just read the scriptures once and forget them. He studied them carefully. He set his heart to learn them, to live them, and to teach them to others.","The king trusted Ezra so much that he sent him on a long journey to help the people back home. “Teach them Heavenly Father’s ways,” the king said.","So Ezra traveled far, carrying the scriptures like a treasure. Heavenly Father watched over him the whole way. When we love the scriptures like Ezra, our hearts grow wise and strong."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she slows down to let good things sink in. In Wren’s world today: at story time, everyone was rushing to the next page, but Wren wanted to stop and think. “Wait — what did that part mean?” she wondered. She read it again and found something wonderful. Just like Ezra, who set his heart to study Heavenly Father’s words, Wren learned that pondering slowly helps the good things stay in your heart.","feel":"Let’s open a pretend book! Hold out both hands like pages and “read” with your finger, left to right. Now hug the book close to your heart. Heavenly Father’s words are a treasure. Can you show me how much you love them with a great big squeeze?","stones":[["SPOT","Ezra felt eager and full of love for Heavenly Father’s words."],["NAME","That warm, want-to-learn feeling is called eager."],["FIND","When have you felt eager to hear a favorite story again and again?"],["FORK","An eager heart can rush past the good part — or slow down and treasure it. Ezra slowed down to study and truly learn."],["CALM","Snuggle in, take one calm breath, and think of your favorite scripture story."]],"remember":"You can love the scriptures like Ezra did — even now! When you listen to a bedtime scripture story, you are filling your heart with Heavenly Father’s words. What is your favorite scripture story so far?","ask":["“What did Ezra love more than almost anything?”","“What did the king send Ezra to do?”"],"share":["When did a scripture settle into your heart and change how you lived? Share that verse and moment.","How have you tried to “set your heart” to study Heavenly Father’s word? Tell your child about a habit you love.","Did a grandparent or ancestor treasure the scriptures? Describe how their love of Heavenly Father’s word touched your family."]},"b8-w1-n5":{"story":["Far away in the king’s palace, a man named Nehemiah heard sad news. The walls around Jerusalem were broken down, and his people were not safe.","Nehemiah’s heart felt so heavy. But he knew just what to do — he prayed and prayed to Heavenly Father for many days.","Then Nehemiah felt brave. He asked the king, “May I please go home and rebuild the walls?” And the king said yes! He even sent wood to help.","When Nehemiah reached Jerusalem, he quietly rode all around the broken walls at night to see what needed fixing. Then he told the people, “Come, let us rise up and build!” Heavenly Father had turned his heavy heart into a brave one."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because a prayer helped him feel brave. In Beau’s world today: Beau had a big pile of toys to clean up and it felt too big to start. His tummy felt worried. So Beau said a quick prayer — “Please help me” — took a deep breath, and picked up just one toy, then another. Soon the whole room was tidy! Just like Nehemiah prayed and then began the big work, Beau prayed and started, one little step at a time.","feel":"Let’s be brave like Nehemiah! First, fold your arms and bow your head to pray — soft and quiet. Now stand up tall, put your hands on your hips, and say, “Let us rise up and build!” Can you feel your prayer turning into brave?","stones":[["SPOT","Nehemiah felt sad and worried when he heard the bad news."],["NAME","That heavy, worried feeling is called worried."],["FIND","When has your heart felt heavy or worried about something?"],["FORK","A worried feeling can freeze us — or we can pray, then begin one small step. Nehemiah prayed first, and then he started to build."],["CALM","Fold your arms, bow your head, and take one slow breath with Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"When your heart feels heavy or scared, you can pray — and Heavenly Father will help you be brave! Then you can start your job one little step at a time, just like Nehemiah. What is something brave you’d like Heavenly Father to help you do?","ask":["“What did Nehemiah do first when his heart felt heavy?”","“What did he want to rebuild?”"],"share":["When did prayer turn your heavy heart into a brave one? Share the before and the after.","Tell your child about a big, scary task you began “one step at a time” with Heavenly Father’s help.","Did a grandparent or ancestor pray for courage before a hard task, then step forward in faith? Describe their bravery."]},"b8-w1-n6":{"story":["As the people built the walls, some grumpy men tried to stop them. They laughed and teased. “You can’t do it!” they said. But the people just kept building.","The workers were wise. They held a building tool in one hand and stayed close together, trusting Heavenly Father to keep them safe. “Heavenly Father will fight for us,” they said.","The grumpy men tried a sneaky trick. “Come down and talk to us,” they called, again and again, hoping Nehemiah would stop his important work.","But Nehemiah stood tall on the wall and called back, “I am doing a great work, so I cannot come down!” He would not let anything pull him away from Heavenly Father’s job. And in just fifty-two days, the wall was finished!"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she stops to think about what matters most. In Wren’s world today: Wren was cleaning up like Mom asked when a friend called, “Come play right now!” Wren paused and wondered, “What’s most important first?” She finished her job, then went to play with a happy heart. Just like Nehemiah wouldn’t come down from his great work, Wren knew the difference between a silly distraction and an important job.","feel":"Let’s stay strong on the wall! Plant your feet, stand up tall, and shake your head “no, no, no” to the silly distractions. Now say it big: “I am doing a great work!” Can you keep building with your hands while you say it?","stones":[["SPOT","The grumpy men’s teasing might have made Nehemiah feel annoyed or pulled-away."],["NAME","That tugged-in-two-directions feeling is called distracted."],["FIND","When have you felt pulled away from something important by something silly?"],["FORK","A distraction can pull us off our good work — or we can say, “Not now,” and stay on task. Nehemiah stayed on the wall and finished."],["CALM","Stand tall, take one strong breath, and say, “I can finish my job.”"]],"remember":"You can keep doing good, even when others try to pull you away! Like Nehemiah, you can say, “I have important work to do.” Finishing your good jobs makes Heavenly Father happy. What is a “great work” you can finish this week — a chore, or a kind deed?","ask":["“What did the grumpy men want Nehemiah to do?”","“What did Nehemiah say back to them?”"],"share":["When did you stay focused on something good while others tried to pull you away? Share how you kept going.","How do you decide what matters most when life gets noisy and distracting? Tell your child your secret.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay true to an important work despite discouragement? Describe their steadiness."]},"b8-w1-n7":{"story":["When the walls were finished, all the people gathered together in one big place. They wanted to hear Heavenly Father’s holy words.","Good Ezra stood on a tall wooden platform and opened the scriptures. He read out loud from early morning, and everyone listened carefully with all their hearts.","As they listened, some of the people began to cry. They were sorry for the times they had forgotten Heavenly Father. But the leaders said something kind: “Don’t be sad today! This is a happy day.”","Then came the best part: “The joy of the Lord is your strength!” So the people wiped their tears, shared yummy food with everyone, and celebrated. It was one of the happiest days ever — all because of Heavenly Father’s words."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to discover treasure and share it. In Scout’s world today: at preschool, Scout learned a happy new song about Heavenly Father and could hardly wait to share it. Scout ran home, calling, “Listen! I learned something wonderful!” and taught it to the whole family. Just like the people who found joy in Heavenly Father’s words, Scout discovered that the best things are even better when we share them.","feel":"Let’s celebrate the scriptures! Cup your hands around your mouth and cheer, “Hooray for Heavenly Father’s word!” Now rub your tummy and share a pretend treat with someone you love. Can you turn a sad face into a great big joyful smile?","stones":[["SPOT","The people felt sorry at first, then Heavenly Father’s words made them glad."],["NAME","That turned-around, better-now feeling is called comforted."],["FIND","When has a hug or a kind word turned your sad feeling into a happy one?"],["FORK","A sad feeling can stay stuck — or Heavenly Father’s words and love can turn it to joy. The people let His word cheer their hearts."],["CALM","Take a breath, then trade a great big smile with someone you love."]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s words can make your heart glad and strong, even on a hard day! When you listen to scripture stories, joy sneaks right into your heart. How do you feel inside when you hear a story about Heavenly Father?","ask":["“What did Ezra read to all the people?”","“What did the leaders say would be the people’s strength?”"],"share":["When have Heavenly Father’s words turned your sorrow into joy or strength? Share that tender experience.","How does “the joy of the Lord” strengthen you on a hard day? Tell your child what that joy feels like.","Did a grandparent or ancestor find strength and joy in the scriptures during a difficult season? Describe their example."]},"b8-w2-n1":{"story":["In a faraway kingdom lived a kind young woman named Esther. She had no mommy or daddy, so her good cousin Mordecai took care of her and loved her like his own.","One day, the king was looking for a new queen. Many young women came to the palace, but Esther’s gentle, kind heart shone the brightest of all.","The king chose Esther to be the new queen! She wore a beautiful crown, but she stayed just as humble and kind as before.","Mordecai told Esther to keep something special a secret for now — that she was one of Heavenly Father’s chosen people. Esther listened wisely. Heavenly Father had a special plan for this kind, humble girl."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because a kind heart is her superpower. In Willow’s world today: a new child stood alone at the playground, looking shy. Willow could have kept playing, but instead she walked over and said, “Want to play with me?” The new friend beamed. Just like Esther’s kindness shone in the palace, Willow’s kindness helped someone feel welcome and loved.","feel":"Let’s put on a pretend crown! Place your hands gently on your head like a sparkly crown, then take a little bow. A crown is nice — but a kind heart is even better. Can you show me your very kindest smile?","stones":[["SPOT","Esther may have felt shy and unsure in the big new palace."],["NAME","That small, unsure feeling is called shy."],["FIND","When have you felt shy somewhere new, like a new class or a big party?"],["FORK","A shy feeling can make us hide — or we can be brave and share a kind smile. Esther stayed kind and gentle even when everything was new."],["CALM","Take a slow breath and picture Heavenly Father right beside you."]],"remember":"Your kind heart is beautiful to Heavenly Father — more than any crown! You can be gentle and kind wherever you go, just like Esther. Who is someone you can be kind to tomorrow?","ask":["“Who took care of Esther and loved her?”","“What did the king choose Esther to be?”"],"share":["When has a quiet act of kindness mattered more than anything fancy? Share that memory.","Tell your child about a time Heavenly Father placed you somewhere for a reason you only understood later.","Did a grandparent or ancestor become known for gentle kindness? Describe how their warmth blessed the family."]},"b8-w2-n2":{"story":["There was a very proud man named Haman who worked for the king. Haman wanted everyone to bow down low to him whenever he walked by.","Everybody bowed — everybody except Mordecai. Mordecai was kind and respectful, but he only bowed down to worship Heavenly Father.","This made proud Haman very angry. But Mordecai stood quietly and firmly. He knew that worship belongs only to Heavenly Father.","Mordecai was not being unkind — he was being faithful. It can take courage to do what’s right when everyone else is doing something different. Heavenly Father is pleased when we stand tall for Him."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he does what’s right even when it’s hard. In Beau’s world today: some kids were teasing another child, and they wanted Beau to join in. Beau felt his tummy flip, but he shook his head and said, “That’s not kind.” Then he went and sat by the child who was sad. Just like Mordecai stood tall for Heavenly Father, Beau stood tall for what was right, even when it wasn’t easy.","feel":"Let’s stand tall like Mordecai! Put your feet strong on the floor, straighten your back, and stand as tall as you can. Now put your hand on your heart. Can you stand tall and say, “I love Heavenly Father”?","stones":[["SPOT","Standing alone, Mordecai might have felt nervous but also sure inside."],["NAME","That firm, do-the-right-thing feeling is called brave."],["FIND","When have you had to do the right thing even though it felt a little scary?"],["FORK","A nervous feeling can make us follow the crowd — or we can stand tall and choose right. Mordecai chose to be faithful to Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Plant your feet, take one strong breath, and stand tall together."]],"remember":"You can stand tall for Heavenly Father, even when others don’t! You can be kind and respectful and still choose what’s right. When might it be brave to do the right thing this week?","ask":["“Who did Mordecai bow down to worship?”","“Why did Haman get angry?”"],"share":["When did doing right require quiet courage while others went a different way? Share it.","How do you teach your child to be respectful and firm at the same time? Tell about a moment it mattered.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stand firm in their faith when it was unpopular? Describe their courage."]},"b8-w2-n3":{"story":["Proud Haman made a wicked plan to hurt Heavenly Father’s people. Mordecai was very worried. He sent a message to Queen Esther, asking her to help. Esther felt afraid. Going to the king without being called could be dangerous. What should she do?","Then Mordecai said something wise: “Maybe Heavenly Father made you queen for this very reason — for such a time as this.”","Esther grew quiet and thought about her special purpose. Perhaps Heavenly Father had placed her right where she was to help save her people. Heavenly Father gives each of us a purpose, right where we are."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"","feel":"Let’s think quietly, like Esther. Put your finger to your chin and make a big “hmm, I wonder” face. Now nod your head like you’ve decided something brave. What do you think Esther will do?","stones":[["SPOT","Esther felt scared, and also thoughtful about what to do."],["NAME","That mixed-up, thinking-hard feeling is called uncertain."],["FIND","When have you felt unsure about doing something big and important?"],["FORK","An uncertain feeling can make us freeze — or we can pause, pray, and find our brave. Esther took time to think and chose to help."],["CALM","Sit still, put a hand on your chin, and take one slow, thinking breath."]],"remember":"Heavenly Father gives YOU a purpose, right where you are — in your family, your class, your home! You can look for ways to help, just like Esther. How might Heavenly Father want to use your helping hands?","ask":["“What wise thing did Mordecai tell Esther?”","“What did “for such a time as this”"],"share":["When did you sense you were placed somewhere “for such a time as this”? Share the experience.","How do you help your child see they have a purpose from Heavenly Father? Tell about a time you felt yours.","Did a grandparent or ancestor step up at just the right moment to help others? Describe their courage of timing."]},"b8-w2-n4":{"story":["Esther decided to be brave and help her people. But first, she wanted Heavenly Father’s help.","She asked everyone to fast and pray with her for three whole days. “Then I will go to the king,” she said, “and if I perish, I perish.” That means she would be brave no matter what.","After praying, Esther put on her royal robe and walked bravely to the king. Her heart was probably beating fast, but she kept going.","The king smiled and welcomed her! He held out his golden scepter, which meant, “Come closer, I’m happy to see you.” Heavenly Father had helped brave Esther every step of the way."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he asks Heavenly Father for help before doing hard things. In Beau’s world today: Beau had to get a shot at the doctor, and he felt scared. He squeezed Mom’s hand, took a big brave breath, and whispered a little prayer. It still pinched, but Beau did it! Just like Esther prayed and then bravely went to the king, Beau prayed and then did the hard, brave thing.","feel":"","stones":[["SPOT","Esther felt afraid before going to the king."],["NAME","That heart-pounding feeling is called afraid."],["FIND","When have you felt afraid to do something brave, like a shot or a new activity?"],["FORK","Being afraid can make us run away — or we can pray, breathe, and go bravely anyway. Esther prayed and then walked forward."],["CALM","Take one big “brave breath” in and out with Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"When something feels scary, you can pray and ask Heavenly Father for a brave heart — then do the hard thing anyway, like Esther! What is something brave Heavenly Father can help you do?","ask":["“What did Esther ask everyone to do for three days?”","“How did the king welcome Esther?”"],"share":["When did prayer and fasting give you courage to face something hard? Share it simply.","Tell your child about a time you felt afraid but did the right thing anyway with Heavenly Father’s help.","Did a grandparent or ancestor show great courage in a frightening moment? Describe how faith carried them."]},"b8-w2-n5":{"story":["Esther was brave, and she was also wise. She did not rush. She invited the king and Haman to a special dinner she had prepared.","Everyone ate the yummy food, and the king asked, “Esther, what would you like? I will give it to you.” But Esther waited for just the right moment.","“Please come to one more dinner tomorrow,” she said kindly. She was being patient and wise, waiting for the best time to speak.","The next night, when the moment was just right, Esther would tell the king the truth. Heavenly Father helped her know when to wait and when to speak. Being wise means choosing the right time."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she knows the wisdom of waiting for the right moment. In Wren’s world today: Wren wanted to tell Dad her big news, but he was on an important phone call. She really, really wanted to interrupt! Instead, she waited patiently, and when he finished, she shared her news — and he could truly listen. Just like Esther waited for the right time, Wren learned that patience makes our words land better.","feel":"Let’s practice waiting wisely. Fold your hands in your lap and sit as still as a statue... wait... wait... now gently smile. Waiting can be hard! Can you show me your calmest, most patient face?","stones":[["SPOT","Esther felt eager to speak, but she waited patiently."],["NAME","That hard-to-wait feeling is called impatient."],["FIND","When have you felt like you just couldn’t wait for something?"],["FORK","An impatient feeling can make us blurt out or fuss — or we can breathe and wait for the right time. Esther wisely chose to wait."],["CALM","Fold your hands and take three slow, patient breaths together."]],"remember":"Heavenly Father can help you be patient and wait for the right time, just like wise Esther! Waiting is hard, but it helps good things go well. When is a time you can practice patient waiting?","ask":["“What did Esther invite the king and Haman to?”","“What did wise Esther choose to do before speaking?”"],"share":["When has patience — waiting for the right moment — made all the difference? Share that story.","How do you model wise timing and self-control for your child? Tell about a time it paid off.","Did a grandparent or ancestor show wisdom in knowing when to wait and when to act? Describe their good judgment."]},"b8-w2-n6":{"story":["At the second dinner, the moment was finally right. Brave Queen Esther spoke the truth to the king. “Please save me and my people,” she said. “A wicked plan has been made to hurt us.” The king asked, “Who would do such a thing?” Esther answered, “It is Haman.”","The king was very upset with proud Haman. He stopped Haman’s wicked plan right away and kept Esther and her people safe.","Because Esther was brave and told the truth, Heavenly Father’s people were saved! Speaking the truth, even when it’s hard, can help and protect the people we love."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout is honest and speaks up. In Scout’s world today: Scout saw a smaller child’s tower get knocked down by accident, and the child was blamed. Scout spoke up: “It was a bump — nobody meant to.” Telling the truth made everything fair again. Just like brave Esther told the truth to save her people, Scout learned that honest words can protect and help others.","feel":"Let’s use a brave, truthful voice! Stand tall, put your hand on your heart, and say clearly, “I will tell the truth.” A truthful voice is a strong voice. Can you say it big and brave?","stones":[["SPOT","Esther felt nervous but determined as she told the truth."],["NAME","That strong, do-it-anyway feeling is called courageous."],["FIND","When have you needed courage to tell the truth, even though it was hard?"],["FORK","Fear can make us stay quiet — or we can speak the truth kindly and bravely. Esther bravely told the king the truth."],["CALM","Hand on your heart, take one strong breath, and say, “Truth is brave.”"]],"remember":"Telling the truth, even when it’s hard, is brave and good — and it can help the people you love! You can be honest like Esther. When might it take courage to tell the truth?","ask":["“What truth did Esther bravely tell the king?”","“What did the king do to keep the people safe?”"],"share":["When did telling the truth — even at a cost — protect or bless your family? Share it.","How do you nurture honest courage in your child? Tell about a time truth mattered most.","Did a grandparent or ancestor speak a hard truth that helped others? Describe their integrity."]},"b8-w2-n7":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people were safe, and their sadness turned into great joy! They wanted to remember this wonderful day forever.","So they made a special holiday called Purim. Every year, families would celebrate that Heavenly Father had saved them through brave Queen Esther.","On this happy day, people shared yummy treats and gave gifts to one another. They even gave presents to those who had little, so everyone could feel the joy. Remembering how Heavenly Father helps us fills our hearts with thankfulness. And sharing our joy with others — especially by giving — makes the happiness even bigger."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she loves to share joy and give to others. In Willow’s world today: Willow had two cookies and saw a friend who had none. She thought about keeping both… but instead she said, “Here — let’s share!” Both girls giggled with happy, full hearts. Just like the people shared gifts at their celebration, Willow learned that joy grows bigger the moment we share it.","feel":"Let’s celebrate! Clap your hands three times — clap, clap, clap! Now pretend to hand a gift to someone you love: “This is for you!” Sharing makes joy grow. Can you show me your happiest celebration dance?","stones":[["SPOT","The people felt relieved and thankful, and full of joy."],["NAME","That happy — and-thankful feeling is called grateful."],["FIND","What is something that makes YOUR heart feel grateful and glad?"],["FORK","Joy can be kept all to ourselves — or we can share it and make it grow. The people shared gifts and gladness with everyone."],["CALM","Put a hand on your heart and whisper, “Thank you, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"When Heavenly Father helps us, we can be thankful and share our joy with others! Giving a gift or a smile makes happiness grow. Who could you share a little joy with tomorrow?","ask":["“What special holiday did the people make to remember?”","“What did people share with each other on that happy day?”"],"share":["When has remembering Heavenly Father’s help filled you with gratitude? Share that feeling.","How does your family turn blessings into generosity? Tell about a meaningful time of giving.","Did a grandparent or ancestor celebrate Heavenly Father’s goodness by giving to others? Describe their generous joy."]},"b8-w3-n1":{"story":["Long ago there lived a good man named Job. He loved Heavenly Father with all his heart, and he was kind to everyone around him.","Job had a happy family and many animals to care for. Every day he thanked Heavenly Father for his blessings.","Job wasn’t only good when things were easy. Loving Heavenly Father was simply who he was, deep down inside.","Each morning, Job would pray for his family, asking Heavenly Father to watch over them. His heart was full of love and thankfulness. Heavenly Father was very pleased with faithful Job."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"","feel":"Let’s thank Heavenly Father like Job! Put your hands together and count three blessings on your fingers — one, two, three. Now give a happy sigh: “Thank you, Heavenly Father.” What are you thankful for tonight?","stones":[["SPOT","Job felt happy and thankful for all his blessings."],["NAME","That warm, lucky-inside feeling is called thankful."],["FIND","What is something in your life that makes you feel thankful?"],["FORK","A thankful heart can be quiet — or we can share it out loud with Heavenly Father. Job thanked Him every single day."],["CALM","Put your hands together and whisper one “thank you” to Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"You can love Heavenly Father and thank Him every day, just like Job — not only when things are easy, but all the time! What is one blessing you can thank Heavenly Father for right now?","ask":["“Who did Job love with all his heart?”","“What did Job do every morning for his family?”"],"share":["How do you cultivate gratitude in the good seasons, so it’s ready in the hard ones? Share your practice.","Tell your child about a daily habit of thanks or prayer that steadies your family.","Did a grandparent or ancestor live with deep gratitude to Heavenly Father? Describe their thankful heart."]},"b8-w3-n2":{"story":["One day, hard things began to happen to Job. He lost his animals, and then his big house was gone too. Soon after, Job did not feel well at all.","Job was very sad. So many good things had gone away, and he didn’t understand why.","But even with his heavy heart, Job did something wonderful. He bowed down and said, “I will still trust Heavenly Father.”","Job didn’t stop loving Heavenly Father, even when life was hard. He held onto his faith like a strong little anchor. Trusting Heavenly Father in hard times is one of the bravest things we can do."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he trusts Heavenly Father even when things are hard. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s favorite toy broke, and he felt so sad — he even cried a little. But after his tears, Beau took a breath and remembered that Heavenly Father still loved him and would help him feel better. Just like Job kept trusting through his hard day, Beau learned that we can be sad AND still trust Heavenly Father at the same time.","feel":"When things are hard, we can hold on tight. Give yourself a big squeezy hug and hold on… hold on… now take a slow breath out. Heavenly Father holds us close. Can you give a strong “holding-on” hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Job felt very sad when hard things happened."],["NAME","That deep, heavy feeling is called sad."],["FIND","When have you felt really sad about something you lost or that didn’t go your way?"],["FORK","A sad feeling can make us stop trusting — or we can hold onto Heavenly Father right through it. Job stayed sad but kept trusting Him."],["CALM","Give yourself a hug and breathe out slowly, saying, “Heavenly Father is with me.”"]],"remember":"Even when something makes you sad, you can still trust Heavenly Father — He never leaves you! It’s okay to feel sad and hold onto Him at the same time. Who can you talk to when you feel sad?","ask":["“What happened to Job that made him sad?”","“What did Job say he would still do?”"],"share":["When did you choose to trust Heavenly Father in the middle of loss or pain? Share it gently.","How do you show your child that sadness and faith can live together? Tell about a hard season He carried you through.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep their faith through great hardship? Describe how they held on."]},"b8-w3-n3":{"story":["When Job was sad and not feeling well, his friends heard about it. They wanted to help, so they came to be with him.","At first, Job’s friends didn’t say a single word. They just sat quietly beside him for a long, long time.","Sometimes when someone is sad, the kindest thing is simply to be near them. Job’s friends showed love just by staying close.","Being there for a sad friend is a beautiful gift. A quiet hug or a hand to hold can say, “I love you, and you’re not alone.” Heavenly Father loves it when we comfort one another."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she notices who is hurting and stays close. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend fell down and scraped her knee and started to cry. Willow didn’t know how to fix it, so she just sat beside her friend and held her hand until she felt better. Just like Job’s friends sat quietly with him, Willow learned that being near someone who is sad is a gift of love.","feel":"Let’s show quiet kindness. Sit close to someone you love and give a gentle pat on the back — pat, pat, pat. No words needed! Can you be a kind, quiet friend right now?","stones":[["SPOT","Job felt comforted when his friends came and stayed with him."],["NAME","That not — so-alone feeling is called comforted."],["FIND","When has someone sitting close made you feel better when you were sad?"],["FORK","When a friend is sad, we can walk away — or we can stay close and comfort them. Job’s friends chose to sit right beside him."],["CALM","Sit close to someone you love and take one calm breath together."]],"remember":"When someone is sad, you can be a kind friend just by being near them — a hug or a hand to hold means so much! Who could you sit with and comfort when they feel sad?","ask":["“What did Job’s friends do when they first came?”","“How can we help a friend who feels sad?”"],"share":["When did someone’s quiet presence comfort you more than any words? Share that memory.","How do you teach your child the ministry of simply “being with” someone? Tell about a time you offered it.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a gift for comforting others? Describe their tender presence."]},"b8-w3-n4":{"story":["Job had so many big feelings inside. He felt sad and confused, and he had lots of questions.","So Job talked to Heavenly Father about all of it. He didn’t hide his feelings — he shared them honestly, just like talking to a loving Father.","Even in his sadness, Job remembered something hopeful. “I know that my Redeemer lives,” he said. That means he trusted that Heavenly Father would make everything right someday.","It’s good to tell Heavenly Father exactly how we feel. He is never upset by our honest hearts. And He always gives us hope, even on our hardest days."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she thinks deeply and holds onto hope. In Wren’s world today: Wren had a hard day and felt grumpy inside. At bedtime, instead of keeping it in, she told Heavenly Father all about it in her prayer. Afterward, she felt lighter, and she remembered that tomorrow was a fresh new day. Just like Job shared his honest feelings and kept his hope, Wren learned that telling Heavenly Father everything brings peace.","feel":"Let’s tell Heavenly Father our feelings. Put your hand on your heart and take a deep breath. Now whisper, “Heavenly Father, this is how I feel.” He always listens. Can you take a big feelings-breath with me?","stones":[["SPOT","Job felt sad and confused, but also hopeful deep down."],["NAME","That wondering, mixed-up feeling is called confused."],["FIND","When have you felt confused or full of questions about something hard?"],["FORK","A confused feeling can bottle up inside — or we can pour it out to Heavenly Father in prayer. Job told Him everything, and hope stayed alive."],["CALM","Hand on your heart, breathe in and out, and tell Heavenly Father one true feeling."]],"remember":"You can tell Heavenly Father exactly how you feel — even the sad or confused feelings! He always listens and always gives hope. What feeling would you like to share with Heavenly Father tonight?","ask":["“Who did Job talk to about his big feelings?”","“What hopeful thing did Job remember?”"],"share":["When did honest prayer — even complaint — bring you closer to Heavenly Father? Share it.","How do you invite your child to bring real feelings to Heavenly Father? Tell about a prayer that helped you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor cling to hope through sorrow? Describe the hope that held them."]},"b8-w3-n5":{"story":["After a long, hard time, Heavenly Father spoke to Job. But He didn’t scold him. Instead, He showed Job all the amazing things He had made!","“Job, did you hang the twinkling stars? Did you make the great big sea? Do you know where the snow is kept?” Heavenly Father asked.","Job looked at the wide sky, the wild animals, and the deep ocean, and his eyes grew big with wonder. Heavenly Father was so much bigger and wiser than Job had ever imagined.","When we look at the stars, the mountains, and the tiny ladybugs, we can see how great Heavenly Father is. He made it all, and He is wise enough to take care of everything — including us."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to notice the wonders of Heavenly Father’s world. In Scout’s world today: on a walk, Scout stopped to watch a tiny ant carry a crumb ten times its size. “Look and see — how did Heavenly Father make something so little so strong?” Scout wondered. Just like Job’s eyes grew big at Heavenly Father’s creations, Scout discovered that the whole world is full of Heavenly Father’s amazing work.","feel":"Let’s look at the wonders! Point up high like you’re touching the stars, then sweep your arms wide like the big ocean. Wow — Heavenly Father made it all! Can you show me your most amazed “wow” face?","stones":[["SPOT","Looking at creation, Job felt small — and full of wonder."],["NAME","That big-eyed, whoa feeling is called amazed."],["FIND","What is something in nature — stars, bugs, oceans — that fills you with wonder?"],["FORK","Feeling small can make us feel scared — or it can help us trust Heavenly Father, who is so big and wise. Job’s wonder helped him trust."],["CALM","Look up, take a slow breath, and whisper, “Heavenly Father, You are so great.”"]],"remember":"When you look at the stars, the animals, and the tiny bugs, you can see how great and wise Heavenly Father is — big enough to care for you! What is your favorite thing Heavenly Father made?","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father show Job?”","“What can we learn about Heavenly Father from the stars and the sea?”"],"share":["When did the wonder of creation restore your trust in Heavenly Father’s wisdom? Share that moment.","How do you use nature to teach your child about Heavenly Father’s greatness? Tell about a favorite spot.","Did a grandparent or ancestor find Heavenly Father in the natural world? Describe their sense of wonder."]},"b8-w3-n6":{"story":["After seeing all of Heavenly Father’s wonderful works, Job understood something important. Heavenly Father knows and sees everything — even the things Job could not understand.","“I trust You,” Job said humbly. “You are wise, and You know what is best. I don’t need to understand everything.”","Job felt peaceful again. He didn’t have all the answers, but he had something even better: he had trust in Heavenly Father.","Sometimes we won’t understand why hard things happen, and that’s okay. We can still trust that Heavenly Father is wise and loving. His plan is always good, even when we can’t see the whole picture."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she’s learning that we don’t have to understand everything. In Wren’s world today: Wren asked why she couldn’t have a treat before dinner, and Mom said, “Trust me — it’s so you’ll feel good later.” Wren didn’t fully understand, but she trusted Mom’s love. Just like Job trusted Heavenly Father’s wisdom without knowing everything, Wren learned that trust means believing in someone’s love and wisdom.","feel":"Let’s show trusting hearts. Cross your arms gently over your heart and give a little squeeze. Now nod your head “yes” to Heavenly Father. Can you whisper, “I trust You, Heavenly Father”?","stones":[["SPOT","Job felt peaceful once he chose to trust Heavenly Father."],["NAME","That calm, settled feeling is called peaceful."],["FIND","When has trusting a grown-up who loves you helped your worried heart feel calm?"],["FORK","Not understanding can make us upset — or we can rest in trusting Heavenly Father’s wisdom. Job chose to trust, and peace came."],["CALM","Cross your arms over your heart and take three slow, peaceful breaths."]],"remember":"Sometimes we won’t understand why things happen — and that’s okay! We can still trust that Heavenly Father is wise and loves us. What is something you can trust Heavenly Father with, even if you don’t understand it?","ask":["“What did Job say he would do, even without understanding everything?”","“How did Job feel once he trusted Heavenly Father?”"],"share":["When did you find peace by trusting Heavenly Father’s wisdom over your own understanding? Share it.","How do you help your child rest in trust when answers don’t come? Tell about a time you had to.","Did a grandparent or ancestor trust Heavenly Father through mystery and find peace? Describe their quiet faith."]},"b8-w3-n7":{"story":["Job’s hard time didn’t last forever. After all his trusting and waiting, Heavenly Father blessed Job again — even more than before!","Job had a happy home once more, with animals to care for and family all around. His heart was filled up with joy and thankfulness.","Job had learned something he would never forget: Heavenly Father is always good, in the hard times and the happy times.","Even on our very hardest days, morning always comes again. Heavenly Father loves us through the sad parts and brings joy back around. He is always, always faithful."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because his faithfulness carried him all the way through to joy. In Beau’s world today: Beau was sad when it rained all day and playtime outside was canceled. But he trusted it would pass — and the next morning, the sun came out and the puddles were perfect for splashing! Just like Job trusted Heavenly Father through the hard time and joy came back, Beau learned that after the rain, the sunshine always returns.","feel":"Let’s celebrate the sunshine after the rain! Wiggle your fingers down like falling rain, then throw your arms up like a bright, warm sun. Joy comes back! Can you show me rain… and then a big, happy sun?","stones":[["SPOT","After his hard time, Job felt joyful and thankful again."],["NAME","That bright, happy-again feeling is called joyful."],["FIND","When has a sad time gotten better and turned into a happy one?"],["FORK","When we’re in a hard time, we can believe joy will never come — or we can trust that Heavenly Father brings the morning. Job trusted, and joy returned."],["CALM","Throw your arms up like sunshine and take one big, happy breath."]],"remember":"Even on your hardest days, morning always comes again — Heavenly Father brings joy back around! He loves you through the sad parts. What is something happy you can look forward to tomorrow?","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father do for Job after his hard time?”","“What did Job learn about Heavenly Father?”"],"share":["When did joy return after a long, hard season, reminding you of Heavenly Father’s faithfulness? Share it.","How do you help your child hold onto hope that morning comes again? Tell about your own “sunrise” after sorrow.","Did a grandparent or ancestor see Heavenly Father restore joy after great trial? Describe their story of hope renewed."]},"b8-w4-n1":{"story":["A very long time ago, people wrote beautiful songs and poems to Heavenly Father. We call them Psalms. One of them tells about a happy little tree. The song says a person who loves Heavenly Father’s words is like a tree planted right beside a cool, flowing river.","Because the tree drinks from the water every day, its roots grow deep and strong. Its leaves stay green, and it grows sweet, healthy fruit.","When we listen to Heavenly Father’s words each day, we’re like that happy tree drinking from the river. Our hearts grow strong and good, and we bloom with kindness and love."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she loves to soak in Heavenly Father’s words. In Wren’s world today: every night, Wren asks for one more scripture story before bed. Little by little, those stories are growing in her heart like water helps a tree. When a friend was sad, Wren remembered a story about kindness and knew just what to do. Just like the happy tree by the river, Wren is growing strong and good from Heavenly Father’s words.","feel":"Let’s be a strong little tree! Stand tall, stretch your arms up like branches, and wiggle your fingers like leaves in the breeze. Now stand firm and strong. Can you be a happy tree, rooted deep?","stones":[["SPOT","The happy tree feels strong, steady, and full of life."],["NAME","That rooted, sturdy feeling is called strong."],["FIND","What helps you feel strong and steady inside?"],["FORK","We can skip the things that help us grow — or drink them in like the tree drinks water. Loving Heavenly Father’s words each day keeps our hearts strong."],["CALM","Stand tall, breathe in slow, and imagine your roots growing deep and strong."]],"remember":"When you listen to Heavenly Father’s words each day, you grow strong and good, just like the happy little tree by the river! What good thing can you do each day to help your heart grow?","ask":["“What is the person who loves Heavenly Father’s words like?”","“What helps the little tree grow strong?”"],"share":["How has daily time in Heavenly Father’s word kept you rooted through storms? Share it.","What small, daily spiritual habit do you hope takes root in your child? Tell why it matters to you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay “planted by the water” through steady devotion? Describe their rootedness."]},"b8-w4-n2":{"story":["One night, a person looked up at the wide, dark sky and saw thousands of twinkling stars. It filled his heart with wonder, so he sang a song to Heavenly Father.","“Heavenly Father, You are so wonderful! You made the moon and the shining stars and put them in their places.”","Then he thought about something amazing. With all the great big sky and stars, Heavenly Father still knows and loves each one of us.","Even the littlest children are precious to Heavenly Father. He made the whole universe, and He still has room in His heart for you. How wonderful is that!"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"","feel":"Let’s reach for the stars! Open and close your hands high in the sky like twinkling stars — twinkle, twinkle. Now point to yourself: Heavenly Father loves ME! Can you make your hands twinkle like little stars?","stones":[["SPOT","Looking at the stars, the person felt wonder and loved."],["NAME","That small-but-special feeling is called loved."],["FIND","When do you feel most loved and special?"],["FORK","A big sky could make us feel tiny and forgotten — or remind us that Heavenly Father made it all and still loves us. The person felt cherished."],["CALM","Look up, breathe slow, and whisper, “Heavenly Father knows my name.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father made the whole sky full of stars — and He still knows and loves YOU! You are precious to Him. How does it feel to know Heavenly Father loves you so much?","ask":["“What did the person see in the night sky?”","“Even with all the stars, who does Heavenly Father still love?”"],"share":["When did creation’s vastness make Heavenly Father’s personal love feel more real, not less? Share it.","How do you help your child feel seen and treasured by Heavenly Father? Tell about a moment you felt His notice.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a deep sense of being known by Heavenly Father? Describe their assurance of His love."]},"b8-w4-n3":{"story":["A shepherd boy named David wrote one of the most beautiful songs of all. He knew all about sheep and how a good shepherd takes care of them.","“The Lord is my shepherd,” David sang. “He leads me to soft green grass and cool, quiet water. He helps me rest.”","David knew that Heavenly Father cares for us just like a shepherd cares for his little lambs. He leads us, feeds us, and keeps us safe.","“Even in the dark, I will not be afraid,” David sang, “because Heavenly Father is with me.” When we feel scared, we can remember: our good Shepherd is always right beside us."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"","feel":"Let’s be cozy little lambs! Curl up small and soft, then give a gentle “baa.” Now feel safe, because the Good Shepherd is near. Can you snuggle up like a sleepy little lamb?","stones":[["SPOT","With the Good Shepherd near, David felt safe, even in the dark."],["NAME","That warm, safe-and-sound feeling is called safe."],["FIND","When do you feel most safe and cozy?"],["FORK","The dark can make us feel afraid — or we can remember the Good Shepherd is right beside us. David chose to feel safe with Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Snuggle in, breathe slow, and whisper, “Heavenly Father is my shepherd.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is your Good Shepherd — He leads you, keeps you safe, and is with you even in the dark! When you feel scared, what could you remember about your Shepherd?","ask":["“What did David call Heavenly Father in his song?”","“Why didn’t David need to be afraid, even in the dark?”"],"share":["When did the image of Heavenly Father as your Shepherd carry you through a “dark valley”? Share it.","How do you comfort your child’s nighttime fears with Heavenly Father’s nearness? Tell about your own comfort.","Did a grandparent or ancestor lean on the Shepherd’s care in frightening times? Describe their trust."]},"b8-w4-n4":{"story":["Another song says, “Heavenly Father is my light. Whom shall I fear?” A light helps us see and chases the scary dark away.","When it’s nighttime and dark, a little light makes everything feel better. We can see where we’re going, and we don’t feel so afraid.","Heavenly Father is like that light for our hearts. When we feel worried or scared, He shines warm and bright inside us.","With Heavenly Father as our light, we don’t need to be afraid of the dark or of hard things. His love shines brightly, and it never, ever goes out."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because Heavenly Father’s light makes him brave. In Beau’s world today: the power went out and the whole house went dark, and Beau felt his heart jump. Then he remembered that Heavenly Father was with him, bright as any light. He took a brave breath, helped find the flashlight, and even comforted his little sister. Just like the psalm-writer said Heavenly Father is his light, Beau’s fear turned to brave.","feel":"Let’s be a little light! Make a fist, then pop your fingers open wide like a bright light turning on — shine! Now hold your light up high. Heavenly Father lights our hearts. Can you shine your bright light?","stones":[["SPOT","In the dark, the person chose not to be afraid because of Heavenly Father."],["NAME","That no-need-to-fear feeling is called brave."],["FIND","When has a little light helped you feel brave in the dark?"],["FORK","The dark can grow our fear — or we can let Heavenly Father’s light make us brave. The person chose to shine with Heavenly Father’s light."],["CALM","Take a brave breath and picture Heavenly Father’s warm light inside your heart."]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is your light! When you feel worried or scared, He shines warm and bright inside you and helps you be brave. What makes you feel brave when you’re afraid of the dark?","ask":["“What did the song say Heavenly Father is?”","“How does a light help us when it’s dark?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father become “light” to you in a frightening or uncertain time? Share it.","How do you point your child to Heavenly Father when fear comes? Tell about a fear His light dispelled.","Did a grandparent or ancestor walk by faith’s light through dark days? Describe how it guided them."]},"b8-w4-n5":{"story":["Sometimes life feels loud and busy and fast. There’s a special song for those times that says, “Be still, and know that Heavenly Father is with you.”","Being still means slowing our bodies down and making our hearts quiet and calm.","When we get very still, we can feel Heavenly Father close, like a warm, peaceful hug for our hearts. Heavenly Father is our safe place. When everything feels too fast or too big, we can be still, take a breath, and remember He is right here with us. In the quiet, we find His peace."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she knows the gift of being still. In Wren’s world today: the day was noisy and Wren felt wiggly and grumpy all over. Mom said, “Let’s be still for a minute.” Wren sat quietly, took a few slow breaths, and felt the grumpies melt away. Just like the psalm says to be still and know Heavenly Father is near, Wren discovered that quiet moments bring His peace.","feel":"Let’s be still together. Sit very quietly and put your hands softly in your lap. Take one slow breath in... and out. Shhh — can you feel how peaceful still can be? Can you be as still as a sleeping kitten?","stones":[["SPOT","When things felt loud and busy, the psalm invites a still, calm feeling."],["NAME","That slowed-down, quiet feeling is called calm."],["FIND","When do you feel wiggly or busy inside, and need to get calm?"],["FORK","Busy feelings can make us fussy and fast — or we can be still and find Heavenly Father’s peace. The psalm shows us to be still and know He is near."],["CALM","Sit still, hands in your lap, and take three slow, quiet breaths together."]],"remember":"When everything feels too fast or too big, you can be still, take a breath, and remember Heavenly Father is right here with you! When could you practice being still and calm?","ask":["“What does the song say to do when life feels busy?”","“What can we feel when we get very still?”"],"share":["How do you create stillness so you can sense Heavenly Father in a noisy life? Share your practice.","How do you teach your child to self — soothe and find calm with Heavenly Father? Tell about a still moment together.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a quiet, grounded peace in Heavenly Father? Describe their stillness."]},"b8-w4-n6":{"story":["One song-writer remembered a hard time when he felt stuck, like he was down in a deep, muddy pit and couldn’t get out.","So he called out to Heavenly Father for help. And Heavenly Father heard him! He gently lifted him up and set his feet on solid, strong rock.","The person was so thankful and happy that Heavenly Father even put a brand-new song in his heart — a joyful song of thanks.","When we feel stuck or sad, we can call out to Heavenly Father, and He will help lift us up. And when He does, our hearts fill up with a happy new song of thankfulness."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she knows the joy of being lifted up. In Willow’s world today: Willow felt stuck and grumpy because she couldn’t build her puzzle. She was about to cry when Grandpa gently helped her find the right piece. Suddenly her heart felt light and happy, and she hummed a little tune. Just like Heavenly Father lifted the psalm-writer from the pit and gave him a new song, kind help turned Willow’s stuck feeling into joy.","feel":"Let’s climb up out of the pit! Reach your hands down low, then slowly lift up, up, up onto strong rock — you made it! Now sing a happy “la-la- la.” Can you climb up and sing your new song?","stones":[["SPOT","The person felt stuck and low, then lifted-up and joyful."],["NAME","That stuck, can’t-get-out feeling is called frustrated."],["FIND","When have you felt stuck or frustrated because something wouldn’t work?"],["FORK","A stuck feeling can make us give up — or we can call out for help and be lifted up.He called to Heavenly Father, who lifted him and gave him a new song."],["CALM","Take a breath, then reach your arms up high like you’re being lifted onto strong rock."]],"remember":"When you feel stuck or sad, you can call out to Heavenly Father — He will help lift you up and put a happy song in your heart! Who can you call on for help when you feel stuck?","ask":["“How did the person feel before Heavenly Father helped him?”","“What did Heavenly Father put in his heart?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father lift you from a “pit” and give you a new song? Share that rescue.","How do you help your child ask for help instead of staying stuck? Tell about a time He lifted you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor testify of being rescued by Heavenly Father? Describe their “new song.”"]},"b8-w4-n7":{"story":["The Psalms are full of happy songs, and one of them says, “Sing to Heavenly Father a joyful song! Play music and praise Him!”","Long ago, people praised Heavenly Father with harps and horns and all kinds of joyful sounds. They sang because their hearts were so full of love for Him.","The song reminds us that Heavenly Father made the whole big world, and His love fills the earth from top to bottom.","We can praise Heavenly Father too — by singing, humming, clapping, or just saying “thank You” with a happy heart. He loves to hear our joyful praise!"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to share joyful praise. In Scout’s world today: Scout woke up to a bright, sunny morning and felt so happy that a little song just bubbled right out. Scout marched around singing, “Thank you, Heavenly Father, for this beautiful day!” and soon the whole family was singing along. Just like the psalm invites us to sing joyfully, Scout discovered that praising Heavenly Father makes joy spread everywhere.","feel":"Let’s make joyful music! Clap a happy beat — clap, clap, clap — then pretend to strum a harp or toot a horn: “toot-toot!” Now sing “I love Heavenly Father!” Can you make a joyful, praising sound?","stones":[["SPOT","The people felt so full of love that they had to sing and praise."],["NAME","That bursting, happy-hearted feeling is called joyful."],["FIND","When do you feel so happy you just want to sing or dance?"],["FORK","Big joy can stay quiet inside — or bubble out in praise to Heavenly Father. The people let their joy sing right out loud."],["CALM","Take a happy breath and hum one joyful note to Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"You can praise Heavenly Father with singing, clapping, or a happy “thank You” — He loves your joyful praise! What is a happy way you like to praise Heavenly Father?","ask":["“How did people praise Heavenly Father in the song?”","“What fills the whole earth, according to the song?”"],"share":["When has worshipful music lifted your heart to Heavenly Father? Share a song that does it for you.","How does your family praise Heavenly Father with joy? Tell about a meaningful time of singing together.","Did a grandparent or ancestor love to praise Heavenly Father in song? Describe the music of their faith."]},"b8-w5-n1":{"story":["Sometimes we make mistakes or do things we know are wrong, and our hearts feel yucky and sad inside. One of the Psalms is a gentle song about that.","The person sang, “Heavenly Father, please make my heart clean and new again.” He knew Heavenly Father could help him feel better.","When we say we’re sorry and try to do better, Heavenly Father washes our hearts clean — just like warm water washes away the mud.","Heavenly Father always forgives us when we come to Him. He gives us a fresh, clean heart and a happy new start. Isn’t that a wonderful gift?"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"","feel":"Let’s wash our hearts clean! Rub your hands together like you’re washing with warm, soapy water — scrub, scrub. Now shake them out and feel fresh and new! Can you show me your clean, happy heart?","stones":[["SPOT","Before saying sorry, the person felt sorry and heavy-hearted."],["NAME","That yucky, did — something-wrong feeling is called guilty."],["FIND","When have you felt yucky inside because you did something you knew was wrong?"],["FORK","A guilty feeling can make us hide — or we can say sorry and let Heavenly Father wash our hearts clean. The psalm-writer chose to come to Him."],["CALM","Rub your hands, breathe out, and whisper, “Please make my heart clean, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"When you make a mistake, you can say sorry, and Heavenly Father will make your heart clean and new again! He always forgives you. What helps you feel better after you’ve done something wrong?","ask":["“What did the person ask Heavenly Father to make new?”","“What does Heavenly Father do when we say we’re sorry?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father’s forgiveness give you a genuine fresh start? Share that relief.","How do you teach your child that repentance is gentle and hopeful, not scary? Tell about His mercy to you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor find peace through Heavenly Father’s forgiveness? Describe their clean-heart story."]},"b8-w5-n2":{"story":["When we feel small or shaky, it helps to stand on something big and strong. One of the Psalms calls Heavenly Father a great big Rock.","“Heavenly Father is my Rock,” the person sang. “He is my safe, high place. I will trust in Him.”","A rock doesn’t wobble or tip over. It stays strong and steady, no matter what. That’s just like Heavenly Father.","When we feel weak or worried, we can lean on Heavenly Father, our strong and steady Rock. He will always hold us up. We are safe when we trust in Him."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he leans on Heavenly Father when he feels weak. In Beau’s world today: it was Beau’s first day at a new preschool, and his legs felt wobbly and unsure. He took a deep breath and remembered Heavenly Father was his strong Rock, holding him up. He felt steadier, walked in, and even made a new friend. Just like the psalm-writer trusted Heavenly Father as his Rock, Beau leaned on Him and found his strong.","feel":"Let’s be strong and steady like a rock! Stand with your feet apart, make your body big and firm, and don’t wobble. Now say, “Heavenly Father is my Rock!” Can you stand as steady as a strong rock?","stones":[["SPOT","Feeling small, the person chose to feel steady by trusting his Rock."],["NAME","That shaky, unsure feeling is called wobbly."],["FIND","When have you felt wobbly or unsure, like on a first day somewhere new?"],["FORK","A wobbly feeling can topple us over — or we can stand on Heavenly Father, our strong Rock. The person trusted Him and stood steady."],["CALM","Plant your feet firm, breathe in, and stand steady like a strong rock."]],"remember":"When you feel weak or wobbly, you can lean on Heavenly Father — your strong, safe Rock! He will always hold you up. When could you lean on Heavenly Father to feel steady?","ask":["“What did the person call Heavenly Father?”","“How is Heavenly Father like a strong rock?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father prove to be your steady “Rock” while everything else shook? Share it.","How do you help your child find footing on Heavenly Father in unsteady moments? Tell about a time you did.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stand firm on Heavenly Father as their rock? Describe their steadiness."]},"b8-w5-n3":{"story":["One song-writer loved Heavenly Father so much that he thought about Him all the time — even when he was lying in bed at night.","“I think about You while I rest,” he sang. “My heart wants to be close to You, like a thirsty person wants a cool drink of water.”","When the world got quiet and still, he would remember all the good things Heavenly Father had done, and it made his heart feel happy and full. Bedtime is a wonderful time to think about Heavenly Father. As we snuggle down to sleep, we can remember His love and feel Him close, all through the peaceful night."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"","feel":"Let’s get cozy and think of Heavenly Father. Rest your head on your hands like a little pillow and close your eyes softly. Now think one happy thought about Heavenly Father. Can you show me your sleepy, peaceful face?","stones":[["SPOT","At bedtime, the person felt peaceful and close to Heavenly Father."],["NAME","That cozy, near-to-Him feeling is called peaceful."],["FIND","What good things could you think about as you fall asleep tonight?"],["FORK","At night our minds can fill with worries — or we can think of Heavenly Father and feel peaceful. The psalm-writer chose to think of Him."],["CALM","Rest your head, breathe slow, and think one happy thought about Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"Bedtime is a wonderful time to think about Heavenly Father and feel Him close! As you fall asleep, you can remember His love. What happy thought about Heavenly Father will you take to sleep?","ask":["“When did the person think about Heavenly Father?”","“What did he remember that made his heart full?”"],"share":["How does turning your mind to Heavenly Father at night bring you peace? Share your bedtime practice.","How do you help your child end the day with thoughts of Heavenly Father? Tell about a peaceful nighttime habit.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep Heavenly Father close in quiet, nightly devotion? Describe their nearness to Him."]},"b8-w5-n4":{"story":["When something wonderful happens, we can hardly wait to tell everybody about it! One of the Psalms is exactly like that.","“Come and hear!” the person sang out. “Let me tell you all the wonderful things Heavenly Father has done!”","He was so thankful and excited that he wanted to share Heavenly Father’s goodness with every single person he could find.","When Heavenly Father does good things for us, we can share the happy news too — with our family, our friends, and everyone we love. Telling about Heavenly Father’s goodness makes our joy grow bigger."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves sharing good news. In Scout’s world today: Scout prayed to find a lost stuffed bunny, and later that day, there it was under the bed! Scout was so happy and grateful that he ran to tell the whole family, “Heavenly Father helped me find it!” Just like the psalm-writer said, “Come and hear,” Scout shared how Heavenly Father had helped, and everyone’s hearts felt glad.","feel":"Let’s share the good news! Cup your hands around your mouth and call out, “Come and hear! Heavenly Father is so good!” Now wave everyone over to listen. Can you share the happy news nice and loud?","stones":[["SPOT","The person felt so grateful and excited he had to share it."],["NAME","That bursting-to-tell feeling is called excited."],["FIND","When have you been so excited about good news you couldn’t wait to tell someone?"],["FORK","Good news can be kept to ourselves — or shared to grow the joy. The psalm-writer shared all that Heavenly Father had done."],["CALM","Take a happy breath and think of one good thing Heavenly Father has done for you."]],"remember":"When Heavenly Father does good things for you, you can share the happy news with everyone you love — it makes your joy grow! What good thing could you tell your family about Heavenly Father?","ask":["“What did the person want everyone to come and do?”","“What was he so excited to tell about?”"],"share":["When did sharing what Heavenly Father did for you multiply your joy? Share that experience.","How do you help your child “declare” Heavenly Father’s goodness naturally? Tell about a time you testified.","Did a grandparent or ancestor love to tell of Heavenly Father’s goodness? Describe how their testimony blessed others."]},"b8-w5-n5":{"story":["One song-writer had grown old, and he looked back on his whole long life. He remembered that Heavenly Father had been with him the entire time.","“Heavenly Father, You have been my hope since I was very young,” he sang. “You have taken care of me all my life.”","From the time he was a tiny child until his hair turned gray, Heavenly Father had never once left his side. Heavenly Father has been taking care of you since before you were even born — and He always will. He is with us when we’re little, when we’re big, and every day in between. His love never stops."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she feels Heavenly Father’s gentle, lifelong love. In Willow’s world today: Willow looked at baby pictures and giggled at how tiny she used to be. Mom said, “Heavenly Father has loved you and watched over you this whole time.” Willow’s heart felt warm and safe, knowing she was never once alone. Just like the psalm-writer trusted Heavenly Father his whole life, Willow felt His love from the very beginning until now.","feel":"Let’s show how we grow with Heavenly Father! Crouch down tiny like a little baby, then s-l-o-w-l-y grow up, up, up, standing tall and big. Heavenly Father is with us the whole time! Can you grow from tiny to tall?","stones":[["SPOT","Looking back, the person felt grateful and safe in Heavenly Father’s care."],["NAME","That watched-over, safe-all-along feeling is called secure."],["FIND","Who has taken care of you and loved you since you were a tiny baby?"],["FORK","We can forget how long we’ve been cared for — or remember Heavenly Father has been with us all along. The person remembered and felt grateful."],["CALM","Give yourself a hug and whisper, “Heavenly Father has always loved me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father has been taking care of you since before you were born — and He always will! He is with you when you’re little and when you’re big. How does it feel to know Heavenly Father has always been with you?","ask":["“How long had Heavenly Father been the person’s hope?”","“When does Heavenly Father take care of us?”"],"share":["When you look back over your life, where do you see Heavenly Father’s hand the whole way? Share it.","How do you help your child sense they’ve always been held by Heavenly Father? Tell of His lifelong care for you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor testify of Heavenly Father’s faithfulness across their whole life? Describe their long trust."]},"b8-w5-n6":{"story":["One of the Psalms has a very special job for grown-ups and children alike. It says to remember the wonderful things Heavenly Father has done — and to tell them to the children.","“We will not hide these things from our little ones,” the song says. “We will tell the children about Heavenly Father’s love and His mighty works.”","That way, children grow up knowing Heavenly Father. And when they grow up, they tell THEIR children too. The happy stories get passed down, on and on.","Every time you hear a story about Heavenly Father, you become part of that beautiful chain of love. And one day, you can tell these stories to others too!"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"","feel":"Let’s pass the story along! Cup your hand to your ear and “listen” to a story, then turn and pretend to tell the happy story to someone else — “Let me tell YOU!” Can you listen, then pass it on?","stones":[["SPOT","Sharing the stories, everyone feels connected and thankful."],["NAME","That part-of — something-special feeling is called connected."],["FIND","Who tells YOU stories about Heavenly Father — and who could you tell one day?"],["FORK","Stories can be forgotten — or remembered and passed on. The psalm reminds us to tell the children, so the love keeps going."],["CALM","Take a cozy breath and think of your favorite story about Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"Every time you hear a story about Heavenly Father, you become part of a beautiful chain of love — and one day you can tell these stories too! Who could you tell a story about Heavenly Father?","ask":["“What does the song say to tell the children about?”","“What happens when children grow up and have their own little ones?”"],"share":["How are you intentionally passing your testimony to the next generation? Share what you hope endures.","Which family faith story do you most want your child to carry forward? Tell it tonight.","Did a grandparent or ancestor faithfully pass down their testimony to you? Describe the gift they handed on."]},"b8-w5-n7":{"story":["The very last song this month is full of love and praise. The person sang about how kind and good Heavenly Father is.","“Heavenly Father, You are so good and forgiving,” he sang. “You are full of love for everyone who calls on You. Please teach me Your way.”","He wanted to learn to be more like Heavenly Father — kind, patient, and full of love. So he asked Heavenly Father to help him.","Heavenly Father is good, and He helps us become good too. When we ask Him to teach us His ways, He fills our hearts with love and helps us grow a little more like Him each day."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"","feel":"Let’s praise good Heavenly Father! Put both hands over your heart, then open them wide like you’re giving all your love up to Him. Now say, “Heavenly Father, You are so good!” Can you show Him your biggest love?","stones":[["SPOT","Praising Heavenly Father’s goodness, the person felt loving and hopeful."],["NAME","That warm, want-to-be-good feeling is called loving."],["FIND","What is one good, kind thing you’d like Heavenly Father to help you learn?"],["FORK","We can try to be good all alone — or ask Heavenly Father to teach us His way. The person asked Him for help to grow good."],["CALM","Hands on your heart, breathe in, and whisper, “Teach me Your way, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is so good, and when you ask Him to teach you His ways, He helps you grow good and loving too — a little more each day! What good thing can you ask Heavenly Father to help you with?","ask":["“What did the person say Heavenly Father is full of?”","“What did he ask Heavenly Father to teach him?”"],"share":["How has asking Heavenly Father to “teach you His way” changed you over time? Share it.","What Christlike quality are you asking His help to grow right now? Tell your child why it matters.","Did a grandparent or ancestor grow remarkably Christlike over a lifetime? Describe the goodness He built in them."]},"b7-w1-n1":{"story":["Long ago, wise King Solomon died. His son Rehoboam became the new king. The people came to him and said, “Please be kind and gentle to us.” Rehoboam said, “Give me three days to think.”","First he asked the old, wise men what to do. They said, “Be kind, and the people will love you forever.” But then he asked his young friends. They told him, “Be tough and bossy!”","Rehoboam listened to his friends instead of the wise men. He spoke harsh, unkind words to the people. This made everyone sad and upset. The kingdom split into two pieces.","Heavenly Father gives us wise people to guide us — parents, prophets, and teachers. When we listen to them, our hearts stay happy and safe. When we ignore good counsel, things can fall apart. Wise listening keeps families together."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Cup your hands behind your ears like big bunny ears. Now listen very carefully — can you hear quiet sounds in the room? That is good listening! Rehoboam forgot to listen to the wise men, and it made everything hard. Let’s practice listening to the people who love us.","stones":[],"remember":"YOU can be a good listener! When mom or dad asks you to do something, listen the first time. When your teacher gives you a rule, follow it. Wise people want to keep you safe and happy. Who is someone wise you can listen to this week?","ask":["Who did Rehoboam listen to?","What happened to the kingdom when he didn’t listen to the wise men?"],"share":["Think of a time you followed wise counsel from a parent, leader, or the prophet. How did it bless your life? Share that story tonight.","Recall a moment you learned the hard way from not listening. What did you learn, and how can you share it gently with your child?","Is there a piece of counsel from a grandparent or ancestor your family still treasures? Tell your child where it came from."]},"b7-w1-n2":{"story":["Elijah was a prophet who loved Heavenly Father. He told the wicked king there would be no rain for a long time. Then Heavenly Father said, “Go hide by the little brook. I will take care of you.”","Elijah went and stayed by the brook. He drank the cool, clear water. But where would he get food? There were no stores out there.","Then something amazing happened! Big black birds called ravens flew to Elijah. They carried bread and meat in their beaks. They brought him food every morning and every night.","Heavenly Father took care of Elijah in a surprising way. He uses all sorts of helpers — even birds! When we trust Him, He always finds a way to take care of us. Heavenly Father never forgets His children."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Flap your arms like a bird carrying food. Now “fly” your hands over and drop pretend bread into someone’s lap. That’s what the ravens did for Elijah! Heavenly Father sent them so Elijah would not be hungry. Think about all the ways He takes care of YOU.","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father takes care of you every single day! He gives you food, a home, and people who love you. Sometimes His help comes in surprising ways. When you wonder if He sees you, remember the ravens. What is one way Heavenly Father took care of you today?","ask":["Who brought Elijah food?","Who sent the ravens to help him?"],"share":["When has the Lord provided for your family in an unexpected way? Describe the “ravens” He sent.","Think of a time help arrived just when you needed it. How did you recognize the Lord’s hand?","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a story of being cared for during a hard time? Share how the Lord sustained them."]},"b7-w1-n3":{"story":["Heavenly Father sent Elijah to a poor widow in another town. She had a little boy and almost no food left. She had just a tiny bit of flour and a tiny bit of oil. She thought it was their very last meal.","Elijah said, “Please make me a little bread first. Don’t be afraid.” The woman was kind. She shared her last food with the prophet, even though she was scared.","Then a miracle happened! Her jar of flour never got empty. Her oil never ran out. Every day there was enough food for the widow, her son, and Elijah.","Because the woman trusted God and shared, He blessed her with plenty. When we give even when it’s hard, Heavenly Father takes care of us. Sharing opens the door for blessings. God can make a little become a lot."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend to pour from a jar — tip your hand and say “glug, glug, glug.” Now pour again… and again… it never runs out! That’s the miracle Heavenly Father gave the widow. He turned her little bit into more than enough.","stones":[],"remember":"When you share, Heavenly Father is so pleased! Sharing your snack, your toys, or your turn can feel hard. But God blesses people who give with a happy heart. What is something you can share this week, even if it feels hard?","ask":["What did the widow share with Elijah?","What happened to her flour and oil?"],"share":["When have you given generously while you yourself were short, and seen the Lord make up the difference?","Think of someone who blessed your family through their generosity. Tell your child that person’s story.","Did an ancestor sacrifice or share during hard times? Describe their faith and what came of it."]},"b7-w1-n4":{"story":["The widow’s little boy got very, very sick. He got so sick that he stopped breathing. His mother was heartbroken and cried. She did not know what to do.","Elijah said, “Give me your son.” He carried the boy upstairs and laid him down. Then Elijah prayed to Heavenly Father with all his heart. “Please, Lord, let this child live again.”","Heavenly Father heard Elijah’s prayer. The little boy started breathing again! Elijah carried him back down to his mother. “See,” he said, “your son is alive!”","The mother knew for sure that Elijah was God’s prophet. Prayer is powerful because Heavenly Father is listening. He hears every prayer we say. Nothing is too hard for Him."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Fold your arms and bow your head like Elijah praying. Whisper, “Please help, Heavenly Father.” Feel how quiet and peaceful that is. Heavenly Father heard Elijah, and He hears you too.","stones":[],"remember":"When something feels too big or too scary, YOU can pray! Heavenly Father loves to hear from you. He listened to Elijah, and He listens to little children just the same. What is something you can pray about tonight?","ask":["What did Elijah do when the boy was sick?","Did Heavenly Father hear his prayer?"],"share":["Recall a prayer that was powerfully answered in your life. How did it strengthen your testimony?","Think of a time you prayed for someone else and saw the Lord work. Share that experience.","Does your family have a story of healing or rescue through prayer, passed down from a grandparent? Tell it tonight."]},"b7-w1-n5":{"story":["Many people had forgotten Heavenly Father. They worshiped a pretend god named Baal. Elijah said, “Let’s find out who the real God is!” He gathered everyone on top of a mountain.","The prophets of Baal called and called to their pretend god all day. But nothing happened — no answer at all. Baal was not real, so he could not hear them.","Then Elijah built an altar and prayed to the true God. He even poured water all over it! Suddenly, FIRE came down from heaven and burned it all up — the water, the wood, even the stones!","Everyone fell down and said, “The Lord, He is the real God!” There is only one true God — our Heavenly Father. He is real, He is powerful, and He hears us. We follow Him because He is true."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Wiggle your fingers up high like flickering flames, then bring them whooshing down — that’s the fire from heaven! Heavenly Father showed everyone He was real. Make your hands strong and say, “The Lord, He is God!”","stones":[],"remember":"There is only ONE true God — your Heavenly Father! He is more real and more powerful than anything pretend. We don’t follow toys, or money, or anything else more than Him. We choose Him first. How can you show Heavenly Father that you choose Him?","ask":["What came down from heaven for Elijah?","Who is the one true God?"],"share":["When did you receive a clear witness that God is real and present in your life? Share that moment.","What “Baals” (distractions or idols) have you set aside to follow the Lord more fully?","Did an ancestor make a bold choice to follow God over the world? Describe their courage to your child."]},"b7-w1-n6":{"story":["Elijah was tired and scared and ran far away. He sat down all alone and felt very sad. Heavenly Father sent an angel to bring him food and rest. Then God told Elijah to wait on the mountain.","First came a big, loud WIND that broke the rocks. But God was not in the wind. Then came a shaking EARTHQUAKE. But God was not in the earthquake.","Then came a hot, crackling FIRE. But God was not in the fire either. After all the loud noise came a tiny, gentle whisper. And THAT is where Elijah felt God.","Heavenly Father often speaks in a quiet, peaceful way. It’s a soft feeling inside, called the Holy Ghost. To hear Him, we have to be calm and still. His gentle voice guides us to what is right."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Make a big loud “WHOOOSH” wind sound, then stomp like an earthquake, then “crackle” like fire. Now… get very, very still and quiet. Shhh. That stillness is where Elijah felt God. Can you feel how peaceful quiet can be?","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father usually speaks in a quiet, gentle way — through the Holy Ghost. It’s a warm, peaceful feeling in your heart. To hear Him, you have to be calm and listen. When have you felt that quiet, good feeling inside?","ask":["Was God in the loud wind, earthquake, or fire?","Where did Elijah finally feel God?"],"share":["Describe a time you felt the still, small voice clearly. What was happening, and what did you do?","How do you create stillness in a noisy life so you can hear the Spirit? Share your practice.","Did a grandparent or ancestor follow a quiet prompting that changed their life — or yours? Tell that story."]},"b7-w1-n7":{"story":["King Ahab wanted to go to a big battle. He gathered lots of prophets who only said what he wanted to hear. “You’ll win! You’ll win!” they cheered. But that wasn’t really true.","There was one true prophet named Micaiah. The king didn’t like him because Micaiah always told the truth. The messenger said, “Just say something nice like everyone else.” But Micaiah said, “I will only say what God tells me.”","Micaiah bravely told the truth: “If you go to battle, it will not go well.” The king got angry and put Micaiah away. But Micaiah was right — he had told the truth.","Telling the truth can be hard, especially when others want you to fib. But Heavenly Father wants us to be honest, even when it’s scary. Brave, truthful people please the Lord. Truth is always the right choice."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up tall and strong like brave Micaiah. Put your hand on your heart and say, “I will tell the truth!” It takes courage to be honest when others want you to fib. Feel how good and clean it feels to tell the truth.","stones":[],"remember":"Always tell the truth, even when it’s hard! Sometimes people want you to fib, or you might be scared of getting in trouble. But Heavenly Father is so happy when you are honest. When is a time it might be hard to tell the truth — and you can choose to be honest anyway?","ask":["Did Micaiah say what the king wanted, or did he tell the truth?","Was telling the truth easy or hard?"],"share":["Recall a time being honest was costly but right. How did the Lord bless your courage?","When has someone’s honesty (or your own) protected your family? Share the story.","Is there an ancestor known for integrity? Describe how their honesty shaped your family’s values."]},"b7-w2-n1":{"story":["Elijah the prophet was very old. His helper Elisha loved him and stayed close by his side. Elisha would not leave Elijah, no matter what. They walked together as good friends.","They came to a river. Elijah rolled up his coat and hit the water. The river split apart so they could walk across on dry ground! Heavenly Father gave Elijah great power.","Suddenly a chariot of fire and horses of fire came down! Elijah went up, up, up to heaven in a whirlwind. Elisha watched in wonder. Then Elijah’s coat floated down to him.","Now Elisha became the new prophet. He picked up the coat and trusted God just like Elijah did. Heavenly Father always has a living prophet to lead His people. When one prophet finishes, another is ready to help us."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Whirl your arms around like a spinning whirlwind going up to the sky — wheee! That’s how Elijah went to heaven. Then pretend to pick up his special coat, like Elisha did. Now YOU can be a helper who follows God’s prophet.","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father always gives us a living prophet to lead us! Today we have a prophet too. He helps us know what God wants. When we follow the prophet, we follow Heavenly Father. Do you know the name of our prophet today?","ask":["How did Elijah go up to heaven?","Who became the new prophet after him?"],"share":["When has following the living prophet blessed or protected your family? Share a specific example.","What counsel from a prophet have you chosen to follow on faith before fully understanding why?","Did an ancestor sacrifice to follow a prophet (a pioneer journey, a calling, a move)? Tell their story."]},"b7-w2-n2":{"story":["A kind woman was very sad. Her husband had died, and she owed money she could not pay. All she had left was one little pot of oil. She came to the prophet Elisha for help.","Elisha said, “Go borrow lots of empty jars from your neighbors. Get as many as you can!” So she and her sons gathered jars and jars and jars. Then they shut the door.","She started pouring her little bit of oil. But it kept pouring and pouring! It filled every single jar, right to the top. The oil only stopped when there were no more jars left.","Elisha said, “Now sell the oil and pay what you owe.” Heavenly Father gave her more than enough. When we trust God and obey His prophet, He provides. He cares about our family’s needs."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend you’re holding a jar and pouring oil — tip your hand and watch it fill up… and another… and another! It just keeps coming! That’s the miracle God gave the widow. He took her little bit and made it more than enough. Sweet Boys & Girls","stones":[],"remember":"When you obey, Heavenly Father can do amazing things! The widow listened to the prophet and got a miracle. God knows what your family needs. He can make your “little bit” into plenty. What is something you can obey cheerfully this week?","ask":["What did the widow have at the start?","What happened when she poured her oil?"],"share":["When has obedience to a prompting or counsel opened the way for the Lord to provide?","Recall a time your family’s needs were met in a way only God could arrange.","Did a grandparent or ancestor experience the Lord multiplying their small means? Describe it."]},"b7-w2-n3":{"story":["There was a kind woman who loved Heavenly Father. Whenever Elisha walked by, she gave him food. She told her husband, “Let’s make a little room for the prophet.” So they built him a cozy room with a bed, a table, a chair, and a lamp.","Elisha was so thankful for her kindness. He wanted to do something nice for her too. He asked, “What can I do for you?” The woman had always wished for a baby.","Elisha said, “Next year you will hold a baby boy in your arms.” The woman could hardly believe it! And it came true — she had a sweet baby son. Heavenly Father blessed her kindness.","When we are kind and make room for others, Heavenly Father notices. He blesses people who serve quietly and lovingly. Kindness is never wasted. God remembers every good thing we do."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend to fluff a pillow and pat a cozy bed — you’re making a nice room for someone special! That’s what the kind woman did for the prophet. Now give a warm, welcoming wave like you’re saying, “Come in, you’re welcome here!”","stones":[],"remember":"Being kind and welcoming makes Heavenly Father happy! You can make room for others — share your space, your snack, or a smile. The kind woman served the prophet, and God blessed her. Who can you be kind and welcoming to this week?","ask":["What did the kind woman build for the prophet?","How did Heavenly Father bless her?"],"share":["When has quiet service you offered come back to bless you unexpectedly? Share it.","Think of someone who “made room” for your family with hospitality or kindness. Tell their story.","Did an ancestor open their home or heart to others? Describe their example of welcome."]},"b7-w2-n4":{"story":["Naaman was a strong, important soldier. But he was very sick with sores all over his skin. A little servant girl said, “There’s a prophet who can help you!” So Naaman traveled to find Elisha.","Elisha sent a simple message: “Go wash in the river seven times.” Naaman was upset! “That’s too easy. Why didn’t he do something fancy?” He almost went home still sick.","But his servants said kindly, “Just try it!” So Naaman dipped in the river — one, two, three, four, five, six, seven times. When he came up, his skin was all better! He was completely healed.","Sometimes Heavenly Father asks us to do simple things. They might seem too easy, but obeying brings big blessings. Naaman learned to be humble and obey. Doing the small things God asks really matters."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Let’s count and dip together! Pretend to dip down in the water seven times — “one, two, three, four, five, six, seven!” Now pop up clean and healthy like Naaman! Doing the simple thing God asked made all the difference.","stones":[],"remember":"Do the simple things Heavenly Father asks, even if they seem small! Saying prayers, being kind, listening, and obeying might feel plain. But those little things bring big blessings. What simple, good thing can you do today?","ask":["What did Elisha tell Naaman to do?","Was Naaman healed when he finally obeyed?"],"share":["When has obeying a “small and simple” commandment brought you unexpected blessings?","Recall a time pride almost kept you from a blessing, and humility opened the door. Share it.","Did a child or humble person ever lead someone in your family to truth (like the servant girl)? Tell it."]},"b7-w2-n5":{"story":["Elisha and his helpers were chopping wood by the river. They were building a new place to live and work. One man was swinging hard with a borrowed axe. Suddenly — splash! — the metal axe head flew off into the deep water.","The man felt terrible. “Oh no! That axe wasn’t even mine — I borrowed it!” He was worried because he could not pay for it. He didn’t know what to do.","Elisha asked, “Where did it fall?” The man showed him the spot. Elisha cut a stick and threw it in the water. Then the heavy iron axe head floated right up to the top!","Heavenly Father cares about big things AND little things. He helped with a lost axe head because His prophet asked. No worry is too small for God. He loves us and helps with everything."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend to swing an axe — chop, chop, chop — then “whoops!” let it splash into the water. Now use your finger to make the axe head float up, up to the top! Heavenly Father cared about that little lost axe. He cares about your little worries too.","stones":[],"remember":"No problem is too small for Heavenly Father! Maybe you lost a toy, or you’re worried about something little. You can still talk to Him about it. He cared about a lost axe head, and He cares about YOU. What little thing can you tell Heavenly Father about today?","ask":["What did the man lose in the water?","What did Elisha do to help?"],"share":["When has the Lord helped you with something “small” that mattered to you? Share it.","How does knowing God cares about little things change the way you pray?","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a tender story of a small but meaningful answer to prayer? Tell it."]},"b7-w2-n6":{"story":["An enemy army wanted to catch Elisha. One morning, Elisha’s young helper woke up and saw soldiers all around the city! “Oh no!” he cried. “What are we going to do?”","But Elisha was calm. “Don’t be afraid,” he said. “There are more helpers with us than there are enemies.” The young man looked around and felt scared and confused. He couldn’t see any helpers.","So Elisha prayed, “Lord, open his eyes so he can see.” Suddenly the young man saw the hills full of horses and chariots of FIRE! God’s mighty helpers were everywhere, protecting them.","Heavenly Father sends help we cannot always see. Angels and His power are all around us. When we feel scared, we don’t have to be — God is bigger than anything. We are never really alone."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Cover your eyes with your hands like the scared helper. Now open them wide and look all around — “Wow! Horses of fire everywhere!” Heavenly Father had helpers all around them the whole time. He has helpers around you too, even when you can’t see them.","stones":[],"remember":"When you feel scared, remember: Heavenly Father’s helpers are all around you! You can’t always see them, but they are there. God is stronger than anything that scares you. You are never alone. What helps you feel brave when you’re afraid?","ask":["What did Elisha’s helper see after Elisha prayed?","Who has more helpers — God’s side or the enemy’s?"],"share":["When have you felt unseen help or protection from the Lord? Describe it.","How do you help your children feel God’s nearness when they are afraid?","Did an ancestor experience a miraculous protection or rescue? Share that faith-building story."]},"b7-w2-n7":{"story":["There was no food in the city, and everyone was hungry. Four men sat outside the gate, hungry too. “Let’s go to the enemy camp,” they said. “Maybe they’ll give us food.” But when they got there — the camp was empty!","Heavenly Father had made the enemies run away in fear. They left behind food, gold, clothes, everything! The four men ate and ate. They were so happy and full.","Then they stopped and said, “This is good news! We can’t keep it to ourselves. Everyone in the city is hungry — we have to tell them!” So they hurried back to share the wonderful news.","Soon the whole city had food, just like the prophet said! When we have good news, we share it. The gospel is the best news of all. Heavenly Father wants us to share His love with everyone."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Cup your hands around your mouth and call out, “I have good news!” Now run in place like the four men hurrying to tell everyone. Sharing good news makes everyone happy! The best news of all is that Heavenly Father loves us.","stones":[],"remember":"When you know something wonderful, share it! The men found food and shared it with everyone. You know the best news of all — that Heavenly Father and Jesus love you! Who can you share Heavenly Father’s love with this week?","ask":["What did the four men find in the camp?","Why did they decide to share the good news?"],"share":["When has sharing the gospel or your testimony brought joy to you or someone else? Share it.","Who shared the “good news” of the gospel with your family line? Tell that person’s story.","How can your family share God’s love with someone this week? Make a small plan together."]},"b7-w3-n1":{"story":["Hezekiah became the king of Judah. He loved Heavenly Father with all his heart. The people had been worshiping pretend gods and idols. Hezekiah said, “We will follow the true God!”","He took away all the idols and false things. He helped the people remember Heavenly Father. Hezekiah trusted God more than any king before him. He held tight to the Lord and obeyed Him.","Because Hezekiah trusted God, Heavenly Father was with him. The Lord helped him and blessed the people. When troubles came, Hezekiah knew where to turn. He always looked to God first.","Trusting Heavenly Father means believing He will help us. It means choosing Him over everything else. When we trust God, He stays close to us. Trusting God is the best choice a heart can make."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Reach out and grab a grown-up’s hand and hold on tight — don’t let go! That’s how Hezekiah held onto Heavenly Father, trusting Him completely. Holding God’s hand keeps us safe and brave. Who can you trust and hold onto?","stones":[],"remember":"Trusting Heavenly Father means believing He loves you and will help you. King Hezekiah trusted God and got rid of anything that pulled him away. You can trust God too — He is always there for you. How can you show Heavenly Father you trust Him?","ask":["What did Hezekiah get rid of so the people could follow God?","Did Hezekiah trust the Lord?"],"share":["What does it look like in YOUR life to “trust in the Lord”? Share a recent example.","What “idols” or distractions have you cleared away to draw closer to God?","Did an ancestor make a courageous choice to put God first? Tell their story."]},"b7-w3-n2":{"story":["A scary enemy king sent King Hezekiah a mean letter. The letter said, “We’re going to win! Your God can’t save you!” Hezekiah felt worried and afraid. What should he do?","Hezekiah didn’t fight or panic. Instead, he took the letter straight to the temple. He spread the letter open before Heavenly Father. “Please, Lord, see this and help us,” he prayed.","Heavenly Father heard Hezekiah’s prayer. He sent the prophet Isaiah with a message: “Don’t be afraid. I will protect this city.” And God did! He saved His people that very night.","When we have a big worry, we can bring it to Heavenly Father. We can “spread it out” before Him in prayer. He listens, and He helps. Taking our troubles to God is always the right thing to do."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend you have a worry in your hands. Now “spread it out” and lay it down in front of you, like Hezekiah laid the letter before God. Take a deep breath out — whoosh — and let the worry go. Heavenly Father can carry our worries for us.","stones":[],"remember":"When you’re worried or scared, take it to Heavenly Father! Hezekiah didn’t keep his worry inside — he spread it out before God in prayer. You can tell Heavenly Father exactly what’s bothering you. What worry can you give to Heavenly Father tonight?","ask":["What did Hezekiah do with the scary letter?","Did Heavenly Father help him?"],"share":["When have you “laid a problem before the Lord” and felt peace or deliverance? Share it.","How do you teach your children to take worries to God instead of carrying them alone?","Did a grandparent or ancestor face a frightening trial with faith? Describe how the Lord helped them."]},"b7-w3-n3":{"story":["One day King Hezekiah got very, very sick. The prophet said he might not get better. Hezekiah was sad. He turned his face to the wall and prayed and cried to Heavenly Father.","“Please, Lord, remember how I have tried to follow You.” Heavenly Father heard his prayer right away. He told the prophet, “Go back and tell Hezekiah I have heard him. I will make him well.”","God promised Hezekiah fifteen more years of life! As a special sign, He even made a shadow move backward. Hezekiah got better, just like Heavenly Father said.","Heavenly Father hears our prayers, even when we are very sad. He loves us and answers in the way that is best. Hezekiah trusted God, and God blessed him. Prayer brings us close to our Father in Heaven."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put your hand over your heart and say a quiet “thank you” to Heavenly Father, like Hezekiah did when he got better. Feel your heart beating — that’s a gift from God! Heavenly Father gives us each day. Let’s be thankful for the days we have.","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father hears your prayers, even when you’re sad! Hezekiah prayed when he was sick, and God answered him. You can pray when you feel sick, sad, or scared. What is something you can thank Heavenly Father for today?","ask":["What did Hezekiah do when he was very sick?","How did Heavenly Father answer him?"],"share":["When has the Lord answered a prayer offered in deep sorrow or sickness? Share it.","How has gratitude for each day shaped your faith? Tell your child about a day you felt especially thankful.","Did an ancestor experience healing or extra years they credited to God? Describe their testimony."]},"b7-w3-n4":{"story":["There was a little boy named Josiah. When he was only eight years old, he became the king! That’s younger than some of your friends. But Josiah had a good and tender heart.","Even though he was young, Josiah wanted to do what was right. The kings before him had made many bad choices. But Josiah chose to follow Heavenly Father. He did not turn to the left or the right.","Josiah didn’t say, “I’m too little to be good.” He just tried his best to please the Lord every day. People could see his good heart. He became a wonderful king.","You are never too young to be good! Heavenly Father is pleased when little children choose the right. Josiah was small, but his good choices were big. You can be like Josiah today."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up tall and proud, even though you’re little — like young King Josiah! Put on a pretend crown and say, “I can choose the right!” You don’t have to be big to be good. Heavenly Father loves your good choices, no matter how little you are.","stones":[],"remember":"You are NEVER too young to choose the right! Josiah was just eight years old, but he had a good heart and followed God. You can be kind, honest, and obedient right now, today. What good choice can you make today, even though you’re little?","ask":["How old was Josiah when he became king?","Did he choose to follow Heavenly Father?"],"share":["Recall a time a child taught YOU about goodness or faith. Share what you learned.","What good habits did you begin young that still bless you? Tell your child about them.","Did an ancestor show great faith or character as a child or youth? Describe their early goodness."]},"b7-w3-n5":{"story":["King Josiah wanted to fix up the temple, God’s house. The workers cleaned and repaired it carefully. While they were working, they found something amazing. It was a long-lost book — the holy scriptures!","The people had forgotten about God’s words for a long, long time. The priest brought the book to King Josiah. When Josiah heard the scriptures read out loud, he listened with all his heart.","Josiah was so moved that he tore his clothes — that’s how people showed strong feelings back then. He realized the people had not been following God’s words. “We need to obey what the scriptures say!” he said.","The scriptures are God’s words to us, and they are precious. They teach us how to be happy and good. Josiah treasured them. We can read and listen to the scriptures too."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend to open a big, special book and hug it close to your heart. The scriptures are a treasure! Run your finger across a pretend page like you’re reading God’s words. Heavenly Father speaks to us through the scriptures. Aren’t we lucky to have them?","stones":[],"remember":"The scriptures are a special treasure from Heavenly Father! They are full of His words, just for us. King Josiah was so happy to find them and obey them. You can listen to scripture stories every day, like right now! How does it feel to hear stories from God’s word?","ask":["What special book did the workers find in the temple?","How did King Josiah feel when he heard God’s words?"],"share":["When has a scripture come alive and guided you at just the right moment? Share it.","How did the scriptures become a treasure in your own life? Tell your story.","Did a grandparent or ancestor cherish the scriptures? Describe their love of God’s word."]},"b7-w3-n6":{"story":["King Josiah had a big question. He wanted to know what Heavenly Father thought about His people. So he sent his helpers to ask a wise woman named Huldah. Huldah was a prophetess — God spoke through her.","The helpers came to Huldah and asked for God’s message. Huldah listened to the Lord. Then she told them exactly what Heavenly Father wanted to say. She spoke God’s truth bravely and clearly.","Huldah said the Lord saw Josiah’s tender, humble heart. Because Josiah had listened and tried so hard, God would bless him. The king was grateful for her message from the Lord.","Heavenly Father speaks through people He chooses, like Huldah and the prophets. When we listen to them, we hear God’s words. God can use anyone with a willing heart. We can listen and obey His messengers."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Cup your hand to your ear and listen carefully, like Josiah’s helpers listening to Huldah. Now nod your head “yes” to show you’ll obey. Heavenly Father gives us messengers to share His words. When we listen, we stay close to God.","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father speaks through special people He chooses! Huldah listened to God and shared His message bravely. When we listen to prophets and good leaders, we hear what God wants. Who is someone who teaches you about Heavenly Father?","ask":["Who did the king’s helpers ask about God’s message?","What did Huldah do?"],"share":["When has the Lord taught you through an inspired leader or friend? Share it.","Who are the “Huldahs” in your life who speak truth to you? Tell your child about one of them.","Did an ancestor have a spiritual gift used to bless others? Describe how God worked through them."]},"b7-w3-n7":{"story":["After hearing God’s words, King Josiah gathered all the people together. He read the holy scriptures out loud so everyone could hear. Then he made a special promise to Heavenly Father. “We will love God and keep His commandments!”","All the people stood up and made the promise too. Josiah helped them clean every bad and false thing out of the land. They got rid of all the idols. They wanted to follow only the true God.","Josiah also helped the people celebrate a special holy day to remember God. There had not been a celebration like it for a very long time. Everyone’s heart turned back to Heavenly Father.","When we make promises to God, we call them covenants. Keeping our promises to Heavenly Father makes us strong and happy. Josiah led the people back to God. We can keep our promises too."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put your hand over your heart and make a promise: “I will follow Heavenly Father.” That’s a covenant — a special promise to God! Josiah and all the people did this together. Promises to God make our hearts strong. Let’s keep our promises!","stones":[],"remember":"A promise to Heavenly Father is called a covenant, and keeping it makes you strong! Josiah promised to follow God, and so did all the people. You make promises too — to be kind, to obey, to pray. What good promise can you keep this week?","ask":["What promise did Josiah and the people make to God?","What did they clean out of the land?"],"share":["Which covenant or promise to God means the most to you, and why? Share it simply.","When has keeping a promise — even a hard one — strengthened your family?","Did an ancestor make and keep sacred covenants? Describe their faithfulness to your child."]},"b7-w4-n1":{"story":["Asa was a good king of Judah who loved Heavenly Father. He helped the people get rid of idols and follow God. One day, a HUGE army came to fight against them. There were so many enemies — way more than Asa’s soldiers!","Asa wasn’t sure how they could win. But he didn’t give up or panic. Instead, he cried out to Heavenly Father. “Lord, only You can help us! We trust in You.”","Heavenly Father heard Asa’s prayer. He helped the small army of Judah win! The big enemy army ran away. God’s power is bigger than any problem.","It doesn’t matter how big a problem is. With Heavenly Father, anything is possible! Asa didn’t count on numbers — he counted on God. When we call on the Lord, He helps us. He is our greatest helper."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Make yourself small and say “uh oh, the problem is so big!” Now reach up high to Heavenly Father and call, “Help me, Lord!” With God, even big problems become small. He is stronger than anything you face!","stones":[],"remember":"No problem is too big for Heavenly Father! Asa had a tiny army and a giant enemy, but he trusted God and won. When something feels too big for you, call on the Lord. What big thing can you ask Heavenly Father to help you with?","ask":["Was Asa’s army big or small?","Who helped Asa win against the giant army?"],"share":["When did you face something far bigger than your own strength, and God carried you through?","How do you turn to the Lord first when problems feel overwhelming? Share your habit.","Did an ancestor overcome impossible odds through faith? Tell that story of God’s deliverance."]},"b7-w4-n2":{"story":["A man named Azariah had a message from Heavenly Father for King Asa. He said, “The Lord is with you when you are with Him. If you look for God, you will find Him!” That was wonderful news.","Then Azariah said something important: “Be strong! Don’t let your hands be weak. Your good work will be rewarded.” This helped King Asa feel brave and happy.","So Asa kept working hard to follow God. He kept getting rid of bad things and doing good. The people gathered together and promised to seek the Lord with all their hearts.","Heavenly Father wants us to be strong and not give up doing good. When we seek Him, we always find Him. Our good work is never wasted. Keep going — God will reward you!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Flex your arms and make your strongest muscles! Say loudly, “Be strong! Don’t give up!” That’s the message God gave to King Asa. When you feel like quitting, be strong and keep doing good. God sees all your good work!","stones":[],"remember":"Be strong and don’t give up doing good! Sometimes good things are hard, and you might want to quit. But Heavenly Father says, “Be strong — your good work will be rewarded!” What good thing can you keep doing, even when it’s hard?","ask":["What did Azariah tell King Asa to do?","What happens when we look for Heavenly Father?"],"share":["When did “be strong and don’t give up” describe a season of your life? How did God reward your faithfulness?","What good work are you tempted to quit, and how does this promise encourage you?","Did an ancestor persevere through long, hard effort with faith? Share their endurance."]},"b7-w4-n3":{"story":["King Asa had trusted God for many years. But when a new problem came, Asa forgot to ask Heavenly Father first. Instead, he tried to fix it all by himself. He made a deal with another king for help.","A wise messenger named Hanani came to Asa. He said, “Asa, why didn’t you trust the Lord this time? God’s eyes look all over the earth to help those who trust Him.”","Asa felt upset because he knew he had made a mistake. Even good King Asa forgot to lean on God this time. We all make mistakes sometimes. The important thing is to remember to trust God again.","Heavenly Father wants us to trust Him in EVERYTHING — big and small. Even when we forget, we can turn back to Him. God is always watching, ready to help. Let’s remember to ask Him first."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Point to your eyes, then point all around the room — Heavenly Father’s eyes see everywhere, looking for people to help! Now point up and say, “I’ll trust You first, Heavenly Father.” Even when we forget, we can always turn back to God.","stones":[],"remember":"Remember to trust Heavenly Father in everything — even the little things! Asa was a good king, but one time he forgot to ask God first. We all forget sometimes, and that’s okay. We can always turn back and trust Him again. What can you remember to ask Heavenly Father about?","ask":["What did Asa forget to do when a new problem came?","Who is always watching, ready to help us?"],"share":["When did you try to solve something on your own before remembering to involve the Lord? What did you learn?","How do you “turn back” to God after forgetting Him? Share your way of recommitting.","Did an ancestor return to faith after a wandering season? Tell that hopeful story of coming back."]},"b7-w4-n4":{"story":["King Jehoshaphat loved Heavenly Father. He wanted everyone in the land to be treated fairly. So he chose good, wise people to be judges. Judges help decide what is right and fair.","Jehoshaphat told the judges something important. “Be careful and fair. Remember, you are not just judging for people — you are judging for the Lord!” He wanted them to be honest and kind.","He told them, “Do your work with a happy, brave heart, and God will be with the good.” The judges promised to be fair to everyone. They wanted to please Heavenly Father.","Heavenly Father wants us to be fair and kind to everyone. We treat all people the way God would want. Being fair makes families and friends happy. Fairness shows we love God and His children."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Hold out both hands flat like a balance scale and keep them even and level. That’s being fair — treating everyone the same! Jehoshaphat wanted everyone to be treated fairly. Can you keep your hands nice and even, like fairness?","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father wants you to be fair and kind to everyone! That means taking turns, sharing, and not leaving anyone out. King Jehoshaphat wanted everyone treated fairly. How can you be fair and kind to someone today?","ask":["What did King Jehoshaphat want the judges to be?","Who did he say they were really judging for?"],"share":["When has being fair or just been difficult but right for you? Share the experience.","How do you teach fairness and kindness in your home? Tell about a time it mattered.","Did an ancestor stand for justice or treat others fairly when it was hard? Describe their integrity."]},"b7-w4-n5":{"story":["A huge army was coming to fight King Jehoshaphat’s people. There were so many enemies! Jehoshaphat felt afraid. But he knew exactly what to do — he gathered everyone to pray.","Jehoshaphat prayed a beautiful prayer: “Heavenly Father, we don’t know what to do. But OUR EYES ARE UPON THEE.” That means, “We are looking to You for help, Lord!”","Heavenly Father answered, “Don’t be afraid. This battle is Mine. Just stand still and watch Me save you.” So the next day, the people SANG praises to God as they marched out. And the enemies were defeated without Judah even fighting!","When we don’t know what to do, we can look to Heavenly Father. “Our eyes are upon Thee” means we trust God to lead us. He fights our battles. We just need to keep our eyes on Him."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Point to your eyes, then look up toward heaven and say, “Our eyes are upon Thee!” That means, “I’m looking to You, Heavenly Father!” When you don’t know what to do, look up to God. He always knows the way.","stones":[],"remember":"When you don’t know what to do, look to Heavenly Father — “our eyes are upon Thee!” Jehoshaphat didn’t know how to win, so he kept his eyes on God. And God saved them! When have you not known what to do? You can look to Him!","ask":["What did Jehoshaphat pray when he was scared?","What did the people do instead of fighting?"],"share":["When have you prayed “I don’t know what to do, but my eyes are on You,” and seen God act? Share it.","How do you “keep your eyes on the Lord” during uncertain times? Tell your child your practice.","Did an ancestor face a battle they couldn’t win alone, and watch the Lord deliver them? Tell that story."]},"b7-w4-n6":{"story":["Uzziah became king when he was just sixteen. He loved God, and as long as he sought the Lord, God helped him do amazing things. He built towers and won battles. He became very strong and famous.","But when Uzziah got strong, he started to feel proud. He forgot that his blessings came from Heavenly Father. He thought he could do whatever he wanted, even break God’s rules.","Uzziah went into the temple to do something only the priests were supposed to do. The priests said, “Please don’t — that’s not your job!” But Uzziah got angry instead of humble. Then he got sick right away.","Pride means thinking we’re better than others and forgetting God. Heavenly Father wants us to stay humble and grateful. When we remember our blessings come from Him, we stay happy and close to God. Humble hearts please the Lord."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Puff up your chest big and proud, then let all the air out and bow your head humbly. Ahh, that feels better! Heavenly Father loves a humble heart. Let’s stay thankful and remember our blessings come from Him.","stones":[],"remember":"Stay humble and remember that your blessings come from Heavenly Father! King Uzziah did great things, but then he got proud and forgot God. Being humble means saying “thank you” to God and following His rules. What can you thank Heavenly Father for tonight?","ask":["What happened to Uzziah’s heart when he became strong?","Does Heavenly Father want us proud or humble?"],"share":["When did success tempt you toward pride, and how did you stay (or return to) humble? Share gently.","How do you remember that your blessings come from God? Tell about a habit of gratitude.","Did an ancestor stay humble through prosperity or success? Describe their grounded faith."]},"b7-w4-n7":{"story":["Good King Hezekiah wanted everyone to come back to Heavenly Father. He sent invitations all across the land. “Come to God’s house! Let’s celebrate and remember the Lord together!”","Some people laughed and didn’t want to come. That made Hezekiah a little sad. But many other people humbled their hearts and said, “Yes! We will come!” They traveled to join the celebration.","Everyone gathered together to worship and remember Heavenly Father. There was so much joy and singing and gladness! It was the happiest celebration in a very long time. The people’s hearts turned back to God.","Heavenly Father always invites us to come back to Him. There is great joy in worshiping God together. When we come to Him with a humble heart, He welcomes us with love. Coming back to God always brings happiness."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Wave your arm like you’re inviting friends over: “Come, come celebrate with God!” Now clap and cheer for the happy celebration! Heavenly Father loves it when we come to Him. There is so much joy in worshiping together. Can you feel the happiness?","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father always invites you to come close to Him — and it brings great joy! King Hezekiah invited everyone to celebrate God, and those who came were so happy. You can come to Heavenly Father anytime, through prayer, church, and good choices. How can you come closer to Heavenly Father this week?","ask":["What did King Hezekiah invite everyone to do?","How did the people feel when they came to celebrate God?"],"share":["When have you felt the joy of returning or drawing closer to God? Share that feeling.","How does your family find joy in worshiping together? Tell about a meaningful time.","Did an ancestor gather family to God or invite others back to faith? Describe their loving invitation."]},"b6-w1-n1":{"story":["Naomi lived far from home with her husband and two sons. Sadly, her husband died. Then both of her sons died too. Naomi felt very sad and alone.","Naomi’s daughter-in-law Ruth loved her very much. Naomi said, “You should go back to your family.” But Ruth said something beautiful: “Where you go, I will go. Your people will be my people. Your God will be my God.”","Ruth chose to stay with Naomi even though it was hard. They traveled together to a new land called Bethlehem. Ruth took care of Naomi because she loved her.","Ruth was loyal and kind. She didn’t leave Naomi when things got hard. Her love was stronger than her troubles."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put your arm around someone next to you, or give yourself a hug. That’s what Ruth did for Naomi — she stayed close when Naomi needed her most. Real love means staying with people even when things are hard. Close your eyes and think of someone you love. Can you picture yourself staying with them and helping them?","stones":[],"remember":"YOU can be loyal like Ruth! When your friend is sad, stay with them. When your sibling needs help, don’t leave. When things get hard, don’t give up on people you love. Ruth didn’t run away when Naomi was sad — she stayed and helped. You can do the same thing! Who needs you to be a loyal friend this week?","ask":["What did Ruth say to Naomi?","Why did Ruth stay with her?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you stayed beside someone — a friend, a spouse, a family member — when it would have been easier to walk away. What did that loyalty cost you, and what did it give back?","When have you felt Heavenly Father’s love come through during a hard season? How did that teach you what His love feels like?","Is there a grandparent or ancestor in our family known for their loyalty or devotion? Share their story so your children can carry it forward."]},"b6-w1-n2":{"story":["Ruth and Naomi needed food. Ruth said, “I will go work in the fields.” She went to pick up leftover grain that the workers dropped.","Ruth worked in a field that belonged to a kind man named Boaz. Boaz saw Ruth working hard. He told his workers, “Be extra kind to Ruth. Leave extra grain for her to find.”","At the end of the day, Ruth had lots of grain! She hurried home to show Naomi. Ruth worked hard all day to take care of Naomi. p013_sceneB Ruth was humble and worked hard. Boaz was kind and generous. They both pleased Heavenly Father with their choices."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend you’re picking things up from the ground — bend down, pick up, put in a basket. Do it a few times. That’s hard work! Ruth did that ALL day to get food for Naomi. Your arms would get tired! But Ruth kept working because she loved Naomi. Think about how it feels when you work hard to help someone you love.","stones":[],"remember":"YOU can work hard to help people too! When you clean up your toys, you’re helping your family. When you help set the table, you’re being useful. When you do your chores without complaining, you’re being like Ruth! Hard work shows love. Ruth could have complained or been lazy, but she worked happily to help Naomi. What’s one job you can do this week to help your family?","ask":["How did Ruth get food?","What did Boaz do to help her?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you worked hard — maybe quietly, maybe when no one noticed — to take care of our family. What kept you going?","When have you seen Heavenly Father provide for you through honest work, the way He provided for Ruth in the fields?","Share a story of an ancestor whose hard work blessed the people who came after them — including you."]},"b6-w1-n3":{"story":["Boaz thought Ruth was wonderful. Ruth was kind, hard-working, and loyal. Boaz wanted to take care of Ruth and Naomi.","Boaz asked Ruth to marry him. Ruth said yes! They had a beautiful wedding. Now Ruth had a loving husband who would take care of her and Naomi.","Later, Ruth and Boaz had a baby boy named Obed. Naomi was so happy to hold the baby! Ruth’s hard work and kindness led to great blessings.","Ruth’s story shows that when we’re faithful and kind, Heavenly Father blesses us. Ruth went from being sad and alone to having a family and being happy!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Smile really big and hug yourself tight. That’s how happy endings feel! Ruth started out sad — her husband died, she had no food, everything was hard. But she stayed faithful and kind, and look what happened! She got a loving husband, a baby, and Naomi was taken care of. When you do what’s right, even when it’s hard, Heavenly Father gives you blessings.","stones":[],"remember":"Sometimes things are hard for a while. Maybe you’re sad, or scared, or something isn’t going well. But if you keep being kind and working hard like Ruth, good things will come! Heavenly Father sees everything you do. He sees when you’re kind, when you work hard, when you’re loyal. And He WILL bless you for it, just like He blessed Ruth! What’s something hard you’re going through? Can you keep being faithful like Ruth?","ask":["What blessings did Ruth receive for being faithful and kind?"],"share":["Tell your children about a hard chapter in your life that you stayed faithful through — and a blessing on the other side that you didn’t see coming.","How did you come to know that Heavenly Father keeps His promises, even when the waiting was long?","What blessing in our family today can you trace back to someone’s faithfulness — yours, or an ancestor’s? Help your children see the thread."]},"b6-w1-n4":{"story":["Hannah wanted a baby very much. But she didn’t have any children. This made her so sad that she cried and cried.","Hannah went to the temple to pray. She prayed with all her heart: “Heavenly Father, please give me a son. If You do, I’ll let him serve You all his life.”","Heavenly Father heard Hannah’s prayer. He gave her a baby boy! Hannah named him Samuel. She was SO happy!","Hannah kept her promise. When Samuel was old enough, she took him to the temple to serve Heavenly Father. Hannah’s prayers were answered because she had faith."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put your hands together like you’re praying. Now close your eyes and imagine praying about something you REALLY want. That’s what Hannah did! She prayed with all her heart. When you pray like that — really meaning it, really trusting Heavenly Father — He hears you. Open your eyes. Did you feel how powerful prayer can be?","stones":[],"remember":"When you want something really badly, YOU can pray like Hannah! Tell Heavenly Father what you need. Pray with all your heart. Then trust that He will answer in the best way. Sometimes He says yes right away. Sometimes He makes you wait. Sometimes He says yes in a different way than you expected. But He ALWAYS hears you! What’s something you can pray about with all your heart this week?","ask":["What did Hannah pray for?","Did Heavenly Father answer her prayer?"],"share":["Tell your children about a prayer you once prayed with your whole heart. What were you asking for, and how did Heavenly Father answer?","When have you felt that Heavenly Father truly heard you could feel?","Share a prayer you prayed for your children before or after they were born. Let them know they were wanted and prayed for, the way Hannah prayed for Samuel."]},"b6-w1-n5":{"story":["When Samuel was still a little boy, Hannah kept her promise. She brought Samuel to the temple. An old priest named Eli lived there.","Hannah said, “This is the son I prayed for. Now I’m giving him back to Heavenly Father to serve Him.” Samuel would live at the temple and help Eli.","Every year, Hannah came to visit Samuel. She brought him a new coat that she made. Samuel grew bigger and stronger each year.","Samuel was obedient and helpful. He learned to serve Heavenly Father from a young age. Hannah’s gift to God was her precious son."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Imagine your mommy has to leave you somewhere for a long time. You’d miss her SO much! That’s what happened to Samuel. But he was brave. He knew he was doing something important for Heavenly Father. Give yourself a brave squeeze on your shoulders. That’s what it feels like to do something hard because it’s right.","stones":[],"remember":"Sometimes Heavenly Father asks us to do hard things. Maybe go to a new school. Maybe share something special. Maybe be brave when you’re scared. Samuel was just a little boy when he went to live at the temple — probably younger than you! But he was brave and obedient. YOU can be brave too when Heavenly Father asks you to do hard things. What’s something hard Heavenly Father might want you to do?","ask":["Why did Hannah take Samuel to the temple?","Was Samuel obedient?"],"share":["Tell your children about something precious you once gave back to Heavenly Father — your time, a plan, a dream — trusting Him with it.","When did serving Heavenly Father, even when it was hard or meant giving something up, bring you a peace you can still feel?","Share a memory of a parent, grandparent, or ancestor who gave their best to the Lord. What did their example plant in you?"]},"b6-w1-n6":{"story":["One night, Samuel was sleeping at the temple. Suddenly he heard someone call his name: “Samuel! Samuel!” He ran to Eli and said, “Here I am!”","But Eli said, “I didn’t call you. Go back to bed.” Samuel went back to bed. Again he heard, “Samuel!” He ran to Eli again, but Eli said, “I didn’t call you.”","This happened THREE times! Finally Eli understood: it was Heavenly Father calling Samuel! Eli said, “Next time, say: ‘Speak, Lord, for I am listening.’”","When Samuel heard the voice again, he said, “Speak, Lord, for I am listening.” Heavenly Father gave Samuel an important message. Samuel became a great prophet!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Be very, very quiet. Put your finger to your lips. Shhhh. Now listen really carefully. Can you hear tiny sounds? That’s how Samuel had to listen to hear Heavenly Father’s voice. It wasn’t loud like thunder. It was quiet and gentle. Heavenly Father speaks to you too, but you have to be still and quiet to hear Him.","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father speaks to YOU through the Holy Ghost! It’s like a quiet feeling in your heart that tells you what’s right. But you have to be quiet and listen, just like Samuel. When you pray, don’t just talk — be quiet and listen too. When you’re trying to make a choice, listen to that feeling inside. That’s Heavenly Father talking to you! When have you felt the Holy Ghost helping you know what to do?","ask":["Who was calling Samuel’s name?","What did Samuel say when he listened?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you felt a quiet prompting from the Holy Ghost and followed it. What happened because you listened?","How did you first learn to recognize the still, small voice in your own life?","Share a story of a prompting that protected or guided our family — one you want your children to remember as they learn to hear Him too."]},"b6-w1-n7":{"story":["Israel had a special golden box called the Ark of the Covenant. It held the Ten Commandments. The Ark showed that Heavenly Father was with His people.","Israel’s enemies captured the Ark in battle! They took it to their city. But wherever they put the Ark, bad things happened. Their false god statue fell down and broke!","The enemies got scared. They said, “This God is too powerful! We need to send the Ark back!” They put it on a cart and sent it back to Israel.","The Israelites were SO happy to have the Ark back! They learned that Heavenly Father is more powerful than anything. When they followed Him, He protected them."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up and make yourself as big and strong as you can — stretch your arms wide, stand on your tiptoes, make a strong face! That’s how POWERFUL Heavenly Father is — even MORE powerful than that! Nothing is stronger than God. Not enemies, not false gods, not anything! When you trust in Heavenly Father, you have the STRONGEST helper ever!","stones":[],"remember":"When you’re scared of something, remember: Heavenly Father is more powerful than ANYTHING! More powerful than bullies. More powerful than the dark. More powerful than anything that scares you. The enemies learned that the hard way when they tried to keep the Ark! You can pray to Heavenly Father when you’re scared, and His power will protect you. What’s something you’re scared of? Can you ask Heavenly Father to help you with His power?","ask":["What was special about the Ark?","What did it teach the enemies?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you were truly afraid, and Heavenly Father’s power carried you through it.","When have you seen that no problem and no fear is bigger than Heavenly Father? How did you come to trust His strength?","Share a moment in our family’s history when you saw God’s hand protect or rescue someone. Let your children know they come from people God watched over."]},"b6-w2-n1":{"story":["The prophet Samuel was getting old. He had been Israel’s leader for a long time. The people came to Samuel and said, “We want a king like all the other nations have!”","Samuel felt sad. He prayed to Heavenly Father. God said, “The people aren’t rejecting you, Samuel. They’re rejecting ME as their king.”","Samuel warned the people: “A king will take your sons for his army. He’ll take your daughters to work for him. He’ll take your best fields and animals.” But the people said, “We still want a king!”","Sometimes we want things just because others have them. But Heavenly Father knows what’s best for us. The Israelites learned this the hard way."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up tall like you’re a king with a crown. Now bow down low. The Israelites wanted a human king they could see, but they already HAD the best King ever — Heavenly Father! Sometimes we want what others have and forget to be grateful for what Heavenly Father has already given us. Close your eyes and think of three blessings you already have.","stones":[],"remember":"Do you ever want something just because your friend has it? A toy, a treat, or something special? The Israelites did that! They wanted a king because everyone else had one. But wanting things just because others have them isn’t always wise. Instead, YOU can: be grateful for what you have, trust Heavenly Father’s plan, and remember that He knows what’s best. What’s something you’re grateful for that you already have?","ask":["Why did Israel want a king?","Was this what Heavenly Father wanted?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you stopped wanting what others had and saw how blessed you already were.","When did trusting Heavenly Father’s plan — instead of copying someone else’s — turn out to be exactly right for you?","Share something you’re deeply grateful for in our family that you might have overlooked if you were busy comparing. Let your children hear what you treasure."]},"b6-w2-n2":{"story":["There was a young man named Saul. He was tall and handsome. One day, his father’s donkeys ran away. Saul and his servant went to look for them everywhere.","After searching for days, they couldn’t find the donkeys. Saul said, “Let’s go home. My father will worry about us!” But his servant said, “There’s a prophet in this town. Maybe he can help us.”","They went to find the prophet Samuel. But Samuel already knew Saul was coming! Heavenly Father had told Samuel, “Tomorrow I’ll send you the man who will be Israel’s king.”","Saul was just looking for donkeys, but Heavenly Father had a much bigger plan! Sometimes when we’re doing simple things, God is preparing us for something great."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend you’re looking for something lost — look left, look right, look behind you. That’s what Saul was doing! He was just trying to find donkeys, doing a regular job for his father. But Heavenly Father was watching and had a special plan. When YOU do your regular jobs — like cleaning your room or helping your family — Heavenly Father sees you! He might be preparing you for something special too!","stones":[],"remember":"When you’re doing everyday things like chores, homework, or helping your family, remember: Heavenly Father is watching! Saul was doing a simple job — looking for lost animals. But that job led him to Samuel, and then to becoming king! YOU never know when doing a good job on small things will lead to big blessings. So do your best, even in little tasks! What’s a small job you can do well this week?","ask":["What was Saul looking for?","Who did he find instead?"],"share":["Tell your children about an ordinary, everyday moment that Heavenly Father used to lead you somewhere unexpected and good.","When have you seen that doing small things faithfully opened a door you never could have planned?","Share how a “small” decision by you or an ancestor changed the whole course of our family. Help your children see God in the ordinary."]},"b6-w2-n3":{"story":["Samuel took a special bottle of oil. He poured it on Saul’s head. This meant Saul was chosen to be king! Samuel said, “Heavenly Father has anointed you to be the leader of His people.”","Later, Samuel gathered all of Israel together. He said, “Heavenly Father has chosen your king!” They drew lots, and Saul was chosen. But where was Saul? He was hiding!","They found Saul hiding among the baggage. When he stood up, he was taller than everyone else! The people shouted, “Long live the king!”","Saul started out humble and obedient. But being humble is important, even when you become important! God chose Saul because his heart was right at the beginning."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up as tall as you can — stretch way up! Saul was SO tall, taller than everyone! But even though he was tall and chosen to be king, he felt scared and hid. Put your hands over your face like you’re hiding. Sometimes when we’re asked to do big things, we feel scared. But Heavenly Father will help you, just like He helped Saul! Take your hands away — be brave!","stones":[],"remember":"Sometimes YOU might be asked to do something that feels too big or scary! Maybe speak in Primary, make a new friend, or try something new. You might want to hide like Saul did! But when Heavenly Father asks you to do something, He will help you be brave enough. You don’t have to be perfect or feel ready — just be willing! What’s something that feels scary that you can ask Heavenly Father to help you with?","ask":["How did Samuel show Saul was chosen?","Why was Saul hiding?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time Heavenly Father asked you to do something that scared you — and you said yes anyway.","When did you feel Heavenly Father make you brave enough, even when you didn’t feel ready?","Share a moment a parent or grandparent stepped forward in courage when God called. What did their willingness teach you about your own?"]},"b6-w2-n4":{"story":["Israel’s enemies were coming to attack! King Saul and his soldiers were scared. Samuel told Saul, “Wait seven days. I’ll come and make an offering to Heavenly Father for you.”","Saul waited. Day one, day two, day three... His soldiers started to leave because they were afraid! On day seven, Samuel still hadn’t come. Saul got impatient.","Saul said, “I can’t wait anymore!” He made the offering himself, even though only priests were supposed to do that. Right after he finished, Samuel arrived!","Samuel said, “You disobeyed! Because you didn’t wait and didn’t obey, you won’t be king forever. Heavenly Father will find someone else.” Saul’s impatience cost him everything."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Let’s practice waiting! Close your eyes and count slowly: 1... 2... 3... 4... 5. That was only five seconds! Saul had to wait SEVEN DAYS! But he couldn’t wait. Tap your foot like you’re impatient — tap, tap, tap. That’s how Saul felt. Waiting is HARD! But sometimes Heavenly Father asks us to wait, and we need to obey even when it’s hard.","stones":[],"remember":"Do you ever have to wait for something and it feels SO hard? Maybe waiting for your birthday, waiting for dinner, or waiting for Mommy to be ready? Saul couldn’t wait for Samuel, and it got him in BIG trouble! YOU can practice patience by: waiting your turn, not interrupting when others are talking, waiting without complaining, and trusting that good things come to those who wait. What’s something you need to be patient about?","ask":["What was Saul supposed to wait for?","What happened when he didn’t wait?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you were tempted to rush ahead of Heavenly Father — and what you learned about waiting on Him.","When has patience and obedience, even when it was hard, protected you or brought a blessing?","Share a family story about the cost of impatience or the reward of waiting faithfully. Let your children learn it through your experience instead of their own."]},"b6-w2-n5":{"story":["Heavenly Father told King Saul through Samuel: “Fight the Amalekites and destroy everything.” This was a test to see if Saul would obey completely.","Saul fought the battle. But he didn’t obey all the way! He kept the best animals and treasures. He thought, “I’ll sacrifice these to God. That will make up for not obeying.”","Samuel came and heard the animals. “What’s this noise?” he asked. Saul said, “I kept them to sacrifice to God!” Samuel said something important: “To OBEY is better than sacrifice!”","Saul tried to make excuses. But Heavenly Father doesn’t want excuses — He wants obedience! Because Saul disobeyed again, God chose a new king. Obedience matters more than anything!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Let’s try this: a grown-up will gently ask you to do something. When they say “Touch your nose,” you touch your nose. Good! Now imagine they said “Touch your nose” but you touched your ears instead and said “It’s close enough!” That’s what Saul did! Put your finger on your ear. Does that feel like obeying? No! Real obedience means doing EXACTLY what you’re asked, not almost. feelingA","stones":[],"remember":"Sometimes YOU might want to obey only partway. Maybe Mommy says “clean your whole room” but you only make your bed. Or Daddy says “no treats before dinner” but you sneak one. That’s like Saul! Partial obedience isn’t obedience. Heavenly Father wants us to obey completely, not make excuses or do it our own way. When someone asks you to do something, do ALL of it, not just the easy parts! Can you think of a time you obeyed only partway?","ask":["What did God tell Saul to do?","Did Saul obey all the way?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you chose to obey Heavenly Father fully partway would have been easier.","How did you come to know that Heavenly Father wants our obedience more than our excuses?","Share a memory of someone in our family known for quiet, complete obedience to God. What did their example build in you?"]},"b6-w2-n6":{"story":["Heavenly Father told Samuel, “Go to Jesse’s house. I’ve chosen one of his sons to be the next king.” Samuel went. Jesse had EIGHT sons!","Jesse’s oldest son came first. He was tall and handsome! Samuel thought, “This must be the one!” But God said, “No. People look at the outside, but I look at the HEART.”","Seven sons came. They were all strong and impressive. But God said no to each one! Samuel asked, “Are these all your sons?” Jesse said, “Well, there’s David. He’s the youngest, watching the sheep.”","They brought David in. He was just a young shepherd boy! But God said, “This is the one!” Samuel anointed David. God chose David not for how he looked, but for his good heart!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Look in a pretend mirror. What do you see on the OUTSIDE? Your face, your hair, your clothes. Now put your hand over your heart. THAT’S what Heavenly Father sees! He looks at what’s INSIDE — if you’re kind, honest, faithful, and loving. You can’t see a heart in a mirror, but Heavenly Father can see your heart all the time! Make your heart happy by being kind!","stones":[],"remember":"Sometimes people might judge you by how you look, how tall you are, or what clothes you wear. But Heavenly Father doesn’t care about that! HE looks at your heart. Is your heart kind? Is it honest? Is it faithful? YOU can have a good heart like David by: being kind to everyone, telling the truth, helping others, and loving Heavenly Father. It doesn’t matter if you’re the smallest or the youngest — if you have a good heart, you’re special to God! What’s one way you can make your heart better?","ask":["Why didn’t God choose the older, bigger brothers?","What did God see in David?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you felt judged by the outside — and how it changed you to know Heavenly Father sees your heart.","When have you felt truly seen Heavenly Father for who you really are inside?","Share who in our family had the kind of heart God loves — kind, honest, faithful. Tell your children the story, so they know whose heart they carry."]},"b6-w2-n7":{"story":["After Saul disobeyed, an evil spirit troubled him. He felt sad and angry all the time. His servants said, “Let’s find someone to play music for you. Music might help!”","Someone said, “Jesse’s son David plays the harp beautifully. He’s brave, speaks well, and the Lord is with him.” So they sent for David.","Whenever the evil spirit troubled Saul, David would play his harp. The beautiful music calmed Saul down and made him feel better. The evil spirit would leave!","David used his talent to help someone who was hurting. Music from a pure heart has special power! When we use our gifts to help others, we’re serving Heavenly Father."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Hum a soft, pretty tune. Feel how peaceful that is? Now make angry sounds — “GRRRR!” Feel how different that is? King Saul felt angry and troubled. But when David played beautiful music, Saul felt peaceful again. Music can change how we feel! When you’re upset, try singing a Primary song. When you’re scared, hum your favorite hymn. Music is a gift from Heavenly Father!","stones":[],"remember":"YOU have special talents and gifts too! Maybe you can sing, draw, tell jokes, give hugs, or help others. Whatever your talent is, you can use it to help people like David did! David didn’t just play music for himself — he used it to help King Saul feel better. When you use your gifts to help others, you’re being like Jesus! What’s a talent you have? How can you use it to help someone this week?","ask":["What was David’s talent?","How did he use it to help Saul?"],"share":["Tell your children about a talent or gift Heavenly Father gave you, and a time you used it to help someone who was hurting.","When have you felt that using your gift for someone else was really serving Heavenly Father?","Share a talent that runs in our family — music, kindness, making people laugh, working with your hands. Where did it come from, and who passed it to you?"]},"b6-w3-n1":{"story":["Israel’s enemies had a HUGE giant soldier named Goliath. He was over nine feet tall! Every day he yelled, “Send me a fighter!” All of Israel’s soldiers were too scared.","A young shepherd boy named David came to bring food to his brothers. He heard Goliath shouting mean things. David said, “I’ll fight him! Heavenly Father will help me!”","David didn’t wear heavy armor. He just had his sling and five smooth stones. He told Goliath, “You have a sword, but I come in the name of the Lord!”","David swung his sling and a stone flew through the air. THUD! It hit Goliath right on the forehead. The giant fell down! David won because Heavenly Father was with him!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up tall like a giant — be as big as you can! Now shrink down small like little David. Sometimes problems feel like GIANTS to us! But with Heavenly Father, we can be brave like David. Now pretend to swing a sling and let go! Even small kids can do big things when Heavenly Father helps them. You’re strong with God on your side!","stones":[],"remember":"YOU might face “giants” in your life too! Maybe a giant fear of the dark, a giant problem at school, or a giant feeling of being scared. But you don’t have to fight your giants alone! Heavenly Father will help you, just like He helped David. You can pray for courage, remember God is with you, and be brave even when you’re small. The battle is the Lord’s — He fights for YOU! What’s a “giant” in your life you can ask God to help you with?","ask":["Why was David brave?","Who helped David beat Goliath?"],"share":["Tell your children about a “giant” you once faced — something that felt far too big for you — and how Heavenly Father helped you face it.","When did you learn that you didn’t have to be the strongest, only willing, because the battle was really the Lord’s?","Share a story of a parent or ancestor who faced something impossible with faith. What did their courage do for yours?"]},"b6-w3-n2":{"story":["King Saul’s son Jonathan saw how brave David was. They became best friends right away! Jonathan loved David like a brother.","Jonathan gave David his special royal robe. He gave him his sword and his bow. That was a HUGE gift — Jonathan was the prince!","Even though Jonathan should have been the next king, he didn’t get jealous of David. He helped David and supported him. That’s what real friends do!","God blessed David in everything he did. He won battles. Everyone loved him. But Jonathan was happy for his friend, not jealous. Real friends are happy when good things happen to each other!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stretch your arms out wide for a big hug. Now give yourself a big squeeze! That’s how Jonathan and David loved each other — like brothers! Now think of your best friend or your sibling. Send them a big invisible hug right now. Real friends share, help each other, and are happy when good things happen to their friend. That’s a treasure!","stones":[],"remember":"YOU can be a friend like Jonathan! When your sibling or friend does something good, be HAPPY for them, not jealous! When you have something special, SHARE it with people you love. When your friend is sad, HELP them feel better. A true friend is one of God’s greatest gifts! You can be that kind of friend by sharing your toys, being happy when others succeed, helping when someone is hurt, and never being mean. Who is your friend? How can you be a better friend this week?","ask":["What did Jonathan give David?","How can YOU be a good friend?"],"share":["Tell your children about a true friend who loved you the way Jonathan loved David — and what that friendship has meant to your life.","When have you had to choose to be genuinely happy for someone else’s success instead of jealous? How did Heavenly Father change your heart?","Share a friendship from our family’s past — yours, or a grandparent’s — that lasted through everything. Let your children see what loyal love looks like up close."]},"b6-w3-n3":{"story":["The people loved David! They sang a song: “Saul has won thousands, but David has won TENS of thousands!” Hearing this made King Saul angry.","Saul felt jealous. He thought, “Why do people love David more than me?” His heart grew bitter. Jealousy can make people do terrible things!","Saul got so jealous, he tried to hurt David. He threw a spear at him! But David ducked away. Twice the spear missed!","David didn’t fight back. He kept being kind, even when Saul was mean. Jealousy hurts everyone. We must keep it OUT of our hearts!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Make a big GRUMPY face. Cross your arms. That’s how jealous feels — yucky and grumpy! Now make a HAPPY face and clap for someone else. That feels better, right? When your friend gets something good, choose to be HAPPY for them, not jealous. Push that jealousy feeling away! Smile big and clap! That’s how to keep your heart happy.","stones":[],"remember":"Jealousy is when we feel mad because someone else has something good. Maybe your sibling got a new toy and you’re jealous. Maybe your friend got praised and you wanted the praise. Saul let jealousy fill his heart and it made him do terrible things! Don’t let jealousy in YOUR heart! Instead: be HAPPY for others, be grateful for what you have, and remember Heavenly Father loves YOU. When you feel jealous, take a deep breath and say “I’m happy for them!” Have you ever felt jealous?","ask":["Why did Saul get jealous?","What can you do when you feel jealous?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time someone was unkind to you, and you chose not to be unkind back. What helped you make that choice?","When have you felt jealousy try to creep into your heart, and how did turning to Heavenly Father help you let it go?","Share a memory of someone in our family who stayed gentle even when they were treated unfairly. What did their example teach you?"]},"b6-w3-n4":{"story":["King Saul was still angry. He chased David with his soldiers! David and his men hid in a big dark cave. Then something amazing happened.","King Saul came RIGHT into the same cave! He didn’t see David in the dark. David’s men whispered, “Now you can hurt him! He’ll never know!”","But David said, “No! He is the king. I won’t hurt him.” David quietly snuck up and cut just a tiny piece off Saul’s robe.","When Saul left the cave, David called out, “King Saul! See? I could have hurt you, but I didn’t!” Saul cried and said, “You are kinder than I am!” David chose kindness over revenge."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Hold out your hands like you’re holding something fragile. That’s what David did — he held back when he could have hurt Saul. Now imagine someone is being mean to you. What’s harder — getting them back, or being kind? Being kind is HARDER! But it’s better. Put your hands on your heart. That’s where kindness lives. Choose kindness, even when it’s hard!","stones":[],"remember":"When someone is mean to you, what should YOU do? It’s so tempting to be mean BACK! But David showed us a better way — be KIND, even to people who aren’t kind to you. When someone hurts your feelings: don’t hit back, don’t say mean things back, pray for them, and be kind anyway. That’s what Jesus teaches us! It’s HARD, but it’s right. When you choose kindness, you’re being like David and like Jesus! Has someone been mean to you? How can you be kind back?","ask":["What could David have done to Saul?","What did he do instead?","Why?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you could have gotten away with something wrong — but chose to do right because Heavenly Father was watching.","When did showing mercy to someone who had hurt you bring you a peace you still remember?","Share a story of integrity from our family’s past — a parent or ancestor who did the right thing quietly, when it cost them something."]},"b6-w3-n5":{"story":["There was a rich, mean man named Nabal. David and his men had been kind to Nabal’s workers. So David asked Nabal politely for some food.","But Nabal said meanly, “Who is David? I won’t give him anything!” David got SO angry. He gathered his men to fight Nabal!","Nabal’s wife Abigail was wise and kind. When she heard, she quickly packed lots of food. She rode out to meet David herself!","Abigail bowed down and said, “Please forgive my husband. Don’t do something you’ll regret!” David thanked God for sending wise Abigail. Her kind words stopped a big fight!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Take a deep breath in... and let it out slowly. That’s what we do when we feel angry. Abigail used WISE, KIND words to stop trouble. Now make a peaceful face — eyes soft, lips smiling gently. That’s the face of a peacemaker! When people are angry, soft kind words can fix things faster than mean words.","stones":[],"remember":"When people are mad, YOU can be a peacemaker like Abigail! Use kind words. Say “I’m sorry” if you can. Help fix what’s broken. Sometimes a peaceful word stops a big fight! Wise Abigail used her brain AND her heart. You can too! When your sibling is mad, when your friend is upset, when someone is angry — try kind words first. They work like magic! Heavenly Father loves peacemakers. How can you be a peacemaker at home this week?","ask":["How did Abigail stop the fight?","Can YOU be a peacemaker?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time your calm, kind words stopped a fight or healed a hard moment in our family.","When have you felt Heavenly Father give you the right words at exactly the right time? How did you know they came from Him?","Share a memory of a peacemaker in our family — someone whose gentleness held people together. What did you learn from watching them?"]},"b6-w3-n6":{"story":["King Saul finally died in a battle. The Israelites came together. They said to David, “We want YOU to be our king!”","David was 30 years old when he became king. He ruled for 40 years! He was a king after Heavenly Father’s own heart.","David captured the city of Jerusalem. He made it his special city! It became known as the City of David. It was beautiful and holy.","David didn’t become king by being mean or pushy. He waited patiently for Heavenly Father’s time. When you’re patient and faithful, blessings come!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend you’re putting a crown on your head — slowly, carefully. Now sit up straight like a king or queen! David waited a LONG time to become king. He could have grabbed the throne by force, but he didn’t. He waited for Heavenly Father’s perfect time. Patience is a powerful thing! Take a deep breath — being patient feels peaceful.","stones":[],"remember":"Sometimes YOU have to wait for good things. Maybe you want a turn on the swing. Maybe you want to grow up bigger. Maybe you want something special. Don’t grab or push or rush! David waited many years to be king. He trusted Heavenly Father’s timing. When you wait patiently and faithfully, Heavenly Father blesses you in the perfect time! What’s something you’re waiting for? Can you be patient like David?","ask":["How old was David when he became king?","Why is being patient important?"],"share":["Tell your children about something you waited a long, long time for — and how Heavenly Father’s timing turned out better than your own.","How did you come to trust that the waiting wasn’t wasted — that Heavenly Father was preparing you the whole time?","Share a promise or blessing that took years to unfold in our family. Help your children see that God’s timing is part of His love."]},"b6-w3-n7":{"story":["The Ark of the Covenant was Heavenly Father’s special holy box. David wanted to bring it to Jerusalem! He was SO happy and excited!","As the Ark came into the city, David couldn’t stop being happy! He danced and danced with all his might! He praised Heavenly Father!","David’s wife saw him dancing. She thought he looked silly for a king. She said, “Why are you dancing like that?” But David said, “I’m dancing for the Lord!”","David didn’t care what others thought. He just wanted to show Heavenly Father how much he loved Him! When we love God, we can be joyful and praise Him too!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up and DANCE! Wiggle, spin, jump, clap! Do a silly happy dance! That’s how David danced for Heavenly Father! He didn’t care if he looked silly — he just wanted to praise God! Now sing “I Am a Child of God” or hum your favorite Primary song. When we praise Heavenly Father with our whole hearts, it makes Him smile!","stones":[],"remember":"How do YOU show Heavenly Father you love Him? You don’t have to dance like David (but you can!). You can sing songs to Him, say “I love you, Heavenly Father” in your prayers, smile when you think of Him, be happy in church, and share your joy with others! Don’t be shy about loving God! David didn’t care if people thought he looked silly — he just praised God. Will you praise Heavenly Father with joy this week? How?","ask":["Why was David dancing?","How can YOU show Heavenly Father you love Him?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time your heart was so full of love for Heavenly Father that you couldn’t keep the joy inside.","When have you worshipped or praised God without caring what anyone thought — and how did it feel to give Him all of you?","Share how worship or music has lifted your heart through hard times. Did a parent or grandparent pass that gift of praise down to you?"]},"b6-w4-n1":{"story":["King David was a good king who loved Heavenly Father. But one day, he made a very big mistake. He saw something that wasn’t his and wanted it.","Instead of looking away, David kept thinking about it. He made bad choice after bad choice. He even did something that hurt other people very badly.","David tried to hide what he did. He thought no one would know. But Heavenly Father sees everything we do, even when no one else is watching!","David made very bad choices. But this story isn’t over yet. Tomorrow we’ll learn what David did to make things right with Heavenly Father."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put your hands over your eyes like you’re hiding. Can you still see Heavenly Father? Can He still see you? Yes! Heavenly Father can ALWAYS see us, even when we hide. David tried to hide his bad choices, but Heavenly Father knew. Take your hands off your eyes. Heavenly Father loves you whether you’re being good or making mistakes — but He wants you to make GOOD choices.","stones":[],"remember":"Sometimes we want things that aren’t ours, or we want to do things we shouldn’t. When that happens, we need to look away and choose good! David didn’t look away from his bad thoughts, and one bad choice led to another. YOU can stop bad choices early by looking away from things that aren’t good, telling Mommy or Daddy when you’re tempted, praying for help, and remembering Heavenly Father is watching. What’s something you can do when bad thoughts come into your head?","ask":["Did David make good choices or bad choices?","Can Heavenly Father see everything we do?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you stopped a wrong choice early — you looked away, you walked away — and how that protected you.","When have you felt comforted knowing Heavenly Father sees everything and is always near?","Share a simple lesson you learned the hard way about small choices leading to bigger ones. What do you hope your children learn from your story?"]},"b6-w4-n2":{"story":["Heavenly Father sent the prophet Nathan to talk to King David. Nathan told David a sad story about a rich man who took a poor man’s only sheep.","David got SO angry! He said, “That rich man should be punished!” Then Nathan looked at David and said, “YOU are that man! You took what wasn’t yours.”","David’s heart broke. He realized what he had done was very wrong. He cried and said, “I have sinned against Heavenly Father!” He was truly, deeply sorry.","David repented. That means he said sorry, asked for forgiveness, and tried hard not to do it again. Heavenly Father loves us when we repent — He always wants us back!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put your hands together and bow your head. Imagine telling Heavenly Father, “I’m sorry.” Real sorry comes from your heart, not just your mouth. David felt SO sad about his bad choices that he cried real tears. When you do something wrong, you might feel that sad feeling too. That’s good! It means your heart knows the difference between right and wrong. Now lift your head — when we say sorry, Heavenly Father forgives us!","stones":[],"remember":"Everyone makes mistakes — even kings, even moms and dads, even YOU! When you do something wrong, what should you do? REPENT! That means: 1) Say “I’m sorry” to Heavenly Father, 2) Say “I’m sorry” to anyone you hurt, 3) Try to fix what you broke if you can, and 4) Try not to do it again. When you repent, Heavenly Father forgives you! He doesn’t stay mad — He’s so happy when we come back to Him. Is there anything you need to say sorry for?","ask":["What did David do when he realized he was wrong?","What does it mean to repent?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you said a heartfelt “I’m sorry” — to a person or to Heavenly Father — and felt the relief of being forgiven.","How did you come to know that Heavenly Father always welcomes us back when we truly repent?","Share a time forgiveness — given or received — healed something in our family. Let your children see that repentance is a gift, not a punishment."]},"b6-w4-n3":{"story":["After David, his son Solomon became king. Solomon was young. He wanted to be a good king like his father had been.","One night, Solomon had a dream. Heavenly Father said, “Solomon, ask Me for anything! What do you want?” Solomon could have asked for anything!","Solomon didn’t ask for money. He didn’t ask for power. He didn’t ask to live a long time. Instead, he said, “Please give me wisdom to lead Your people well.”","Heavenly Father was SO pleased! He said, “Because you asked for wisdom and not selfish things, I’ll give you wisdom AND riches AND honor!” Solomon became the wisest king ever!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend a wish-granting genie just said you can have ANYTHING. What would you ask for? Maybe candy? A toy? A pony? Solomon could have asked for anything too — but he asked for WISDOM, the ability to make good choices! Tap your forehead three times. That’s your brain — wisdom helps your brain make good choices. When we ask Heavenly Father for things that help us be better, He’s so happy with us!","stones":[],"remember":"When YOU pray, what do you ask for? It’s okay to ask for help with your hopes and dreams. But the BEST things to pray for are things like Solomon asked for: wisdom, kindness, courage, patience, faith, and love. Heavenly Father loves it when we ask to be BETTER, not just to GET more stuff. The next time you pray, try asking Heavenly Father to help you be wise, kind, or brave! What’s something good (not a thing or toy) you could ask Heavenly Father for?","ask":["What did Solomon ask Heavenly Father for?","Why did this please God so much?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you prayed for something better than “stuff” — for wisdom, patience, or strength — and how Heavenly Father answered.","When did Heavenly Father give you wisdom you didn’t have on your own, right when you needed it most?","Share a piece of wisdom passed down to you from a parent or grandparent that still guides your choices today."]},"b6-w4-n4":{"story":["Two mothers came to King Solomon. They were arguing about a baby! Both of them said, “This baby is MINE!” They needed Solomon to help them.","Solomon thought carefully. Then he said something shocking: “Bring me a sword! I’ll cut the baby in half — half for each mother.”","The REAL mother cried out, “NO! Don’t hurt the baby! Give him to her — just don’t hurt him!” The other woman said, “Yes, cut him in half!”","Solomon smiled. He knew the real mother right away! A real mother would rather lose her baby than see him hurt. Solomon gave the baby back to his real mommy. Everyone saw how WISE Solomon was!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Hold an imaginary baby in your arms — gently, gently, like it’s the most precious thing! That’s how a real mommy holds her baby. The real mother loved her baby SO much that she’d give him up rather than see him hurt. That’s REAL love. Now think of the people you love most — your mommy, daddy, brothers and sisters. How much do you love them? Real love means wanting what’s best for them, even if it’s hard for you.","stones":[],"remember":"Solomon used the wisdom Heavenly Father gave him to find the truth. YOU can ask for wisdom too! When you have a hard problem to solve — like figuring out who took your toy, or what’s the right thing to do — pray to Heavenly Father. He gives wisdom to those who ask! Also, real love means wanting what’s best for someone, even when it’s hard. The real mother loved her baby more than herself. Can you love your sibling or friend like that — wanting what’s best for them?","ask":["How did Solomon know which mother was the real mother?","What does real love look like?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you put what was best for someone you love ahead of what you wanted for yourself.","When have you felt Heavenly Father’s kind of love — love that gives, even when it’s hard — either from Him or for your own family?","Share a story of sacrificial love in our family’s history. Who loved someone that much, and how did it shape the family you have today?"]},"b6-w4-n5":{"story":["King Solomon wanted to build a beautiful temple for Heavenly Father. He gathered the BEST materials — strong cedar wood, smooth stones, and shining gold!","Thousands of workers helped. They cut the stones perfectly far away. When they put the stones together at the temple, no one heard a hammer or chisel! It was so quiet and holy.","The temple took SEVEN whole years to build! Inside, everything was covered in gold. There were carvings of angels and flowers everywhere. It was the most beautiful place ever!","The temple was a special place where people could feel close to Heavenly Father. Solomon wanted to give Heavenly Father his very best. We can give Heavenly Father our best too!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend you’re holding the most precious, most beautiful thing in the world. Hold it gently. Look at it carefully. That’s how Solomon felt about the temple! He wanted EVERYTHING to be perfect for Heavenly Father. Now think about how special temples still are today. We have temples in our church! They’re beautiful places where families can be together forever. When you grow up, you can go to the temple too!","stones":[],"remember":"Solomon gave Heavenly Father his BEST work, his BEST materials, his BEST effort. YOU can give Heavenly Father your best too! How? You can do your best at school, give your best at chores, sing your best in Primary, behave your best in church, and pray your best prayers. Don’t just do “good enough” — try your very BEST for Heavenly Father! And someday, you can go to the temple — a special place even better than Solomon’s. What’s something you can do your best at this week?","ask":["What was the temple like?","Why did Solomon want it to be so beautiful?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you gave Heavenly Father your very best effort — not “good enough,” but your best — and how it felt.","When have you felt closest to Heavenly Father in a temple or holy place? What made it sacred to you?","Share what the temple has meant in our family across generations. Help your children see the blessing of giving God our best."]},"b6-w4-n6":{"story":["When the temple was finished, Solomon gathered ALL of Israel together. He stood in front of the new temple. It was time to dedicate it to Heavenly Father!","Solomon knelt down. He spread out his hands toward heaven. He prayed a beautiful prayer: “Heavenly Father, when Your people pray here, please HEAR them!”","Solomon prayed for everyone — for the people of Israel, for strangers visiting from far away, even for people who would pray years later. He asked God to hear ALL their prayers!","When Solomon finished praying, a bright cloud filled the temple! Everyone knew Heavenly Father had heard the prayer. He was there!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Get on your knees right where you are. Now spread out your arms toward the sky like Solomon did. Doesn’t that feel like you’re reaching up to Heavenly Father? When you pray like this — humble, with your heart open — Heavenly Father hears you. Solomon prayed not just for himself, but for everyone! You can pray for other people too. Who do you want to pray for tonight?","stones":[],"remember":"When YOU pray, Heavenly Father HEARS you! Just like Solomon prayed and the cloud came, your prayers are heard by God. You don’t have to be in a temple to pray. You can pray in your bedroom, in the car, at school, when you’re scared, when you’re happy — anywhere! And like Solomon, you can pray for OTHER people too: your family, your friends, people who are sick, people who are sad. Praying for others is one of the best things you can do! Who can you pray for today?","ask":["What did Solomon ask Heavenly Father to do?","Does Heavenly Father hear OUR prayers?"],"share":["Tell your children about a specific prayer of yours that Heavenly Father answered — proof to you that He really hears.","How did you come to know, for yourself, that your prayers are heard?","Share a time you prayed for someone else and saw Heavenly Father answer. Let your children know they have been carried in your prayers, too."]},"b6-w4-n7":{"story":["After the beautiful temple was built, Heavenly Father appeared to Solomon again. He had something very important to tell him.","Heavenly Father said, “Solomon, if you keep My commandments and follow Me, you and your family will be blessed forever! But if you turn away and worship other gods, things will go badly.”","This was a big promise — and a big warning! Heavenly Father wanted Solomon to keep his heart close to Him. Even kings need to obey God!","Sadly, when Solomon got older, he didn’t always follow Heavenly Father. He started worshiping other gods. We must always keep our hearts close to Heavenly Father — every day, our whole life!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put both hands over your heart. Feel it beating? That’s the heart Heavenly Father wants! He wants your WHOLE heart, all the time. Now imagine pulling something little out of your heart — that’s what happens when we let little bad things in. Solomon did that, little by little, until his heart was far from God. Push your hands back to your heart and squeeze tight. Keep your heart close to Heavenly Father always!","stones":[],"remember":"It’s not enough to love Heavenly Father just once. We have to choose Him EVERY day, our WHOLE life! Solomon was wise and built a beautiful temple, but later he stopped following God. Don’t be like older Solomon — be faithful your WHOLE life! You can keep your heart close to Heavenly Father by praying every day, going to church, reading scriptures, choosing the right, and remembering Him always. Even when you grow up, keep loving Heavenly Father! Will you promise to keep your heart close to God forever?","ask":["What did Heavenly Father warn Solomon about?","How can we keep our hearts close t o God?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time in your life when staying close to Heavenly Father — every day — made all the difference.","When have you felt your heart begin to drift, and what brought you back to Him?","Share how a parent or grandparent kept their heart close to God their whole life. What did their steadiness teach you about staying faithful?"]},"b10-w1-n1":{"story":["Long ago the people of Israel were tired and sad. Heavenly Father sent them a message, and it began with the softest word of all: Comfort.","Then a voice called out in the wilderness. Make the road straight, it said, for the Lord is coming.","Isaiah told the people how the Lord would come to them. Not with stomping feet. He would come like a shepherd walking slow among his sheep.","He gathers the lambs up in his arms, Isaiah said, and carries them close against his heart. The tired mother sheep he leads gently, never fast.","You are one of His little lambs. When your legs are too tired for one more step, He is the One who carries you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because comfort is her gift, and this story is Heavenly Father comforting His tired people. In Willow’s world today: her cousin cries when her block tower falls over. Willow does not fix it. She sits down close and pats her back until the crying goes quiet. Just like the Shepherd gathering the lambs in His arms, Willow learned that being held is the very first comfort.","feel":"Curl up small like a little lamb in the grass. Now let me scoop you into my arms and hold you close to my heart, the way the Shepherd does. Can you show me how a lamb rests?","stones":[["SPOT","The people of Israel felt worn out and sad, and their hearts needed comforting."],["NAME","That tired, needing-to-be-held feeling is called weary."],["FIND","When did your body feel so tired that you wanted somebody to carry you?"],["FORK","Cry alone in the dark, or let someone hold you. The lambs let the Shepherd pick them up."],["CALM","Lay your hand on your heart and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please carry me tonight.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father never rushes His littlest lambs, He carries them!","ask":["“What soft word did Heavenly Father send first to His tired people?”","“How does the shepherd carry the little lambs?”","“What did Willow do when her cousin’s block tower fell?”"],"share":["When has the Lord carried you through a season you could not walk through alone? Tell about it.","How do you comfort your child before you correct them? Tell about a moment you chose holding first.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a gentle, gathering way with tired people? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w1-n2":{"story":["Isaiah asked the people questions nobody could answer. Who held all the oceans in the hollow of one hand?","Who weighed the mountains on a scale? Who spread the sky out like a tent for us to live under?","Some people had made statues out of wood and gold and bowed down to them. The workmen had to fasten each statue tight so it would not tip over.","Then Isaiah said, lift up your eyes and look at all the stars. Heavenly Father brings them out one by one, and He calls every single star by its name.","Not one star ever gets forgotten or left behind. And Heavenly Father knows your name even better than He knows theirs."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she is the one who stops and ponders how big Heavenly Father is. In Wren’s world today: she lies on the driveway after dinner with her coral red jacket zipped up. She counts nine stars, loses count, and starts over again. She asks if the stars have names, and her mom says every one. Just like Isaiah asking the people to lift up their eyes, Wren learned that wondering is a kind of praying.","feel":"Reach both arms up and open your hands wide, like you are holding the whole night sky. Now point one finger at a star only you can see. Can you show me how many stars you can count?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah’s people felt small, and wondered if Heavenly Father was too big to notice them."],["NAME","That small-in-a-very-big-world feeling is called wonder."],["FIND","When did something feel so big to you that it made your eyes go wide?"],["FORK","Feel forgotten because you are little, or trust that you are known. Isaiah looked up and trusted."],["CALM","Open your palms on the blanket and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank you for knowing my name.’"]],"remember":"The One who names every star already knows your name by heart!","ask":["“Who holds all the water in the world in the hollow of His hand?”","“What does Heavenly Father call every single star?”","“What was Wren doing on the driveway after dinner?”"],"share":["When did the size of creation last make you feel small in a good way? Tell about it.","How do you make room for your child’s questions instead of answering too fast? Tell about a moment.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a quiet, pondering kind of faith? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w1-n3":{"story":["The people said the long road was too hard and their legs had nothing left in them. Isaiah told them Heavenly Father never gets tired at all.","He made the ends of the whole earth, and He is never weary. To the ones with no strength left, He gives more.","Even strong young men fall down tired, Isaiah said. Even the fastest runners stop and sit right down in the dust.","But they that wait upon the Lord get brand new strength. They rise up with wings like eagles. They run and are not weary; they walk and do not faint.","Some days your legs get so tired. When you wait on Heavenly Father, He gives you enough for one more step."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because waiting for strength you do not have yet takes real courage. In Beau’s world today: the walk home from the park is long and his legs ache. He wants to be carried. He takes three slow breaths, holds his dad’s hand, and finds one more step, and then one more. Just like the tired people who waited on the Lord and rose up like eagles, Beau learned that new strength comes to the ones who wait.","feel":"Stretch both arms out wide like eagle wings and lift them up slow, slow, slow. Now let them float back down to the blanket. Can you show me how an eagle rises without flapping?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah’s people were worn all the way out, with no strength left for the road ahead."],["NAME","That empty-tank, legs-too-heavy feeling is called weary."],["FIND","When did your body run all the way out of go, and you still had further to walk?"],["FORK","Give up in the dust, or wait on the Lord for new strength. Isaiah’s people waited and rose up."],["CALM","Take one slow breath in and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please give me strength.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father never runs out of strength, and He shares it with tired little legs!","ask":["“Does Heavenly Father ever get tired?”","“What happens to the people who wait upon the Lord?”","“How did Beau find one more step on the long walk home?”"],"share":["When did strength arrive in you that was clearly not your own? Tell about it.","How do you teach a child to wait rather than quit? Tell about a moment you saw it work.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep going long past their own strength? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w1-n4":{"story":["The poor and the needy went looking for water. They searched and searched, and there was none, and their tongues were dry with thirst.","But Heavenly Father heard them calling. He promised He would never leave them alone in that dry land.","I will open rivers up in the high places, He said, and springs down in the middle of the valleys. I will make the wilderness a pool of water.","And in that dry desert He promised to plant trees, cedar and myrtle, fir and pine, so the people could see and know and understand who had done it.","When you are thirsty for something and cannot find it, Heavenly Father hears you too. He knows how to make rivers where you thought there were none."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because he keeps looking, and this story is about seeking and being heard. In Scout’s world today: his water bottle runs empty at the park and the fountain is broken. He does not sit down and wail. He walks the whole long path in his bright green shoes until he finds another fountain, low and cold and perfect. Just like the thirsty people Heavenly Father heard, Scout learned that seeking gets answered.","feel":"Cup both your hands together like a little bowl and hold them up. Now pretend cool water is filling them all the way to the top. Can you show me how you would drink from your own hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were thirsty and searching, and there was no water anywhere they looked."],["NAME","That searching-and-not-finding feeling is called thirsty."],["FIND","When did you want something so much, and it just was not there?"],["FORK","Give up and stay dry, or keep asking Heavenly Father. The thirsty people cried out, and He heard."],["CALM","Cup your hands together and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, I am thirsty. Please help me.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father can make a river in the driest place you know!","ask":["“What were the people looking for that they could not find?”","“What did Heavenly Father promise to plant in the dry desert?”","“What did Scout do when the park fountain was broken?”"],"share":["When did help arrive in a season of your life that felt completely dry? Tell about it.","How do you help your child keep asking instead of giving up? Tell about a moment they tried again.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep hoping all through a dry season? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w1-n5":{"story":["Heavenly Father spoke to His people about the hard road up ahead. He did not tell them that the road would be easy.","He told them something better. Fear not, He said, for I made you, and I have called you by your name. You are mine.","When you pass through the waters, He promised them, I will be with you. The rivers will not wash over you.","When you walk through the fire, you will not be burned, and the flame will not hurt you. For I am the Lord, your Savior.","Heavenly Father did not promise there would be no deep water. He promised something better: that He would be right there with you inside it."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because walking into deep water takes courage, and courage means knowing Someone is with you. In Beau’s world today: the swim teacher asks him to step off the pool stair. His stomach flips over. He looks up, finds his mom’s eyes, hears her say his name, and lets go of the wall. Just like the people who heard Fear not, I have called you by name, Beau learned that a loving voice is braver than the wall.","feel":"Wiggle your fingers like little waves in front of you. Now let me hold your hand and walk our fingers right through the middle of them. Can you show me how brave hands walk through water?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were afraid of the deep waters and the long road waiting in front of them."],["NAME","That tight-tummy, please-not-yet feeling is called afraid."],["FIND","When did something look too deep or too big for you to walk into?"],["FORK","Freeze at the edge, or step in with Heavenly Father beside you. The people heard Fear not and went."],["CALM","Squeeze my hand one time and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please walk with me.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father knows your name, and He never lets go in the deep parts!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father say before He told the people about the waters?”","“Did the people have to go through the water all by themselves?”","“What helped Beau let go of the pool wall?”"],"share":["When did the Lord meet you inside the hard thing rather than around it? Tell about it.","How do you use your child’s name to steady them? Tell about a moment your voice was enough.","Did a grandparent or ancestor walk through deep water with their faith intact? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w1-n6":{"story":["Sing, O heavens, Isaiah said. Be joyful, O earth, and break out singing, O mountains, because the Lord has comforted His people.","But some of them were certain they had been forgotten. My Lord has left me, they said very quietly.","So Heavenly Father asked them a gentle question. Can a mother forget the little baby at her side? Can she stop loving the child she carried?","Even if a mother somehow could forget, He said, I will never forget you. Look, I have carved you right into the palms of my hands.","You are carved into Heavenly Father’s hands, in the very place He would see you every single time He opens them."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she notices whoever feels left out. In Willow’s world today: a new boy stands alone by the fence during outside time. Willow walks over with a dandelion and sits down beside him without a word. When the bell rings, they walk inside together. Just like Heavenly Father telling His people He could never forget them, Willow learned that nobody stays forgotten once somebody comes and sits down.","feel":"Open both hands wide and look at your palms. Now trace a tiny circle in the middle of one hand with your finger, right where He can see you. Can you show me your open hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The people believed they had been left behind and forgotten by Heavenly Father."],["NAME","That left-behind, does-anybody-see-me feeling is called forgotten."],["FIND","When did you feel like nobody remembered you were even there?"],["FORK","Believe you are forgotten, or believe you are carved in His hands. The people chose to sing again."],["CALM","Open both palms on the blanket and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank you for remembering me.’"]],"remember":"You are carved into Heavenly Father’s hands, and hands like that never forget!","ask":["“Who did Isaiah say should sing because the Lord had comforted His people?”","“Where did Heavenly Father say His people were carved?”","“What did Willow bring to the boy standing alone at the fence?”"],"share":["When have you quietly believed the Lord had forgotten you? Tell about it.","How do you reach a child who feels overlooked at home? Tell about a moment you caught it in time.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a way of making people feel remembered? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w1-n7":{"story":["I am the One who teaches you what is good, Heavenly Father told His people, and I lead you in the way that you should go.","If only you had listened to me, He said. Then your peace would have been wide and quiet as a river.","Then Isaiah reminded them of the long walk through the deserts, when Heavenly Father led their families all the way home.","They were not thirsty on that road. He split the rock wide open, and the water came gushing out of it for them to drink.","Heavenly Father knows the way you should go, and He knows where the water is hiding. Listen for Him, and your heart can be quiet as a river."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she stops and listens before she moves. In Wren’s world today: her big sister says the shortcut goes left, but her grandpa said right. Wren stands still at the corner in her coral red boots and thinks about it. She waits, asks one more time, then takes the way grandpa said. Just like the people who were told to listen so their peace could be like a river, Wren learned that listening comes before walking.","feel":"Put one finger on your lips and listen to the quiet room. Now make the softest river sound you can with your breath, all the way out. Can you show me your listening ears?","stones":[["SPOT","The people had not listened, and their hearts were rough and restless instead of quiet."],["NAME","That rushing-ahead, did-not-listen feeling is called restless."],["FIND","When did you go your own way fast, and wish after that you had listened?"],["FORK","Rush off on your own, or listen for the way you should go. Heavenly Father led His people home."],["CALM","Breathe out long like a slow river and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, I am listening.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father leads the way, and He brings water right out of a rock!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father say their peace could have been like?”","“Where did the water come from out in the desert?”","“Which way did Wren choose to go at the corner?”"],"share":["When did listening change the direction of your life? Tell about it.","How do you invite obedience without demanding it? Tell about a moment your child chose to listen.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry an unusual kind of peace? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w2-n1":{"story":["Morning by morning, Heavenly Father wakened Isaiah’s ear and taught him what to say. Then Isaiah could speak one gentle word to anyone who was weary.","Isaiah was not afraid. He set his face steady as flint and said, ‘Heavenly Father will help me.’","Isaiah asked a question. Who among you walks in darkness and has no light at all?","Let that one trust in the name of the Lord, he said, and lean on Heavenly Father. The dark did not last.","When your room goes dark tonight, you do not need a lamp to be safe. You can whisper Heavenly Father’s name, and He is already right there beside you."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because trusting in the dark takes quiet courage. In Beau’s world today: the hallway light burned out right at bedtime. Beau’s chest went tight, but he did not call out. He whispered to Heavenly Father and held his blanket until his breathing slowed. Just like Isaiah, who walked without light and trusted anyway, Beau learned that Heavenly Father stays close when we cannot see.","feel":"Set your face brave and still, the way Isaiah did. Now let your shoulders melt down soft into the pillow. Can you show me your brave face, and then your soft face?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah walked in the darkness with no light, and he did not run away."],["NAME","That not-knowing-what-comes-next feeling is called uncertain."],["FIND","When did you feel unsure because you could not see what was coming?"],["FORK","Isaiah could panic in the dark, or trust the name of the Lord. He trusted."],["CALM","Press one hand flat on your chest and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please help me.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father gives a gentle word to tired hearts right when they need it most!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father teach Isaiah to say to people who were tired?”","“Was Isaiah afraid when he walked in the dark?”","“What did Beau do when the hallway light went out?”"],"share":["When have you had to keep walking without seeing the end of the path? Share that time with your child.","How do you steady a child when you have no answers yourself? Tell about a moment you offered comfort instead of an explanation.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep faith through a long dark season? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w2-n2":{"story":["Isaiah told the people to look at the rock they were cut from. Look back to Abraham your father, he said, and to Sarah who bore you.","Heavenly Father called Abraham when he was all alone. Then He blessed him, and increased him.","Heavenly Father promised to comfort Zion. He would comfort all her waste places too.","He said her wilderness would become like Eden, and her desert like a garden. Joy and gladness would be found there, with singing.","You came from a family too, with grandmas and grandpas standing behind you. Heavenly Father knows every one of their names, and He knows yours."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because looking back gently teaches us who we are. In Wren’s world today: Wren found an old photograph of her great-grandma inside a box in the closet. She sat down on the rug and asked questions until dinner was ready. Then she carried the picture to the table to show everyone. Just like the people who looked back to Abraham and Sarah, Wren learned that families are a rock we can stand on.","feel":"Sit up tall and steady like a big rock that will not wobble. Now open your arms wide like a garden growing. Can you show me your strong rock, and then your growing garden?","stones":[["SPOT","The people felt small and dried up, like a wilderness where nothing grows."],["NAME","That empty, nothing-is-growing feeling is called discouraged."],["FIND","When have you felt like nothing you tried was working out?"],["FORK","They could forget where they came from, or look back and remember. They looked back."],["CALM","Trace a slow circle on your palm and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, You know my family.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father can turn a dry, empty place into a garden full of singing!","ask":["“Whose names did Isaiah tell the people to look back and remember?”","“What did Heavenly Father promise the wilderness would become?”","“What did Wren find inside the box in the closet?”"],"share":["What dry season of your life later bloomed into something good? Share that story with your child.","How do you hand family memory down to little ones? Tell about a moment a family story changed how your child saw themselves.","Did a grandparent or ancestor plant something you are still harvesting today? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w2-n3":{"story":["Isaiah saw someone running over the mountains. How beautiful are his feet, Isaiah said, because of what he carries.","The runner was bringing good tidings. He was publishing peace, and telling everyone that Heavenly Father reigns.","The watchmen on the walls saw him coming. They lifted up their voices, and together they sang.","Even the broken waste places of Jerusalem broke forth into joy and singing, because Heavenly Father had comforted His people.","You can be a runner too. When you carry something kind and true to someone, your little feet are beautiful to Heavenly Father."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because good news is meant to be carried, not kept. In Scout’s world today: Scout found out the classroom hamster had babies. He could not hold it in for one second. He ran to every table in the room, and then straight out to the parking lot to tell his mom. Just like the runner on the mountains, Scout learned that happy news grows bigger when we share it.","feel":"March your feet under the blanket like you are running up a mountain. Then cup your hands around your mouth and call out the happy news. Can you show me your running feet?","stones":[["SPOT","The watchmen saw the good news coming and could not keep quiet."],["NAME","That bursting, I-must-tell-somebody feeling is called joyful."],["FIND","What good news made you want to run and tell somebody right away?"],["FORK","The watchmen could stay silent, or lift their voices and sing. They sang."],["CALM","Take one big breath in, then hum it back out. Whisper, ‘Thank You, Heavenly Father.’"]],"remember":"Feet that carry kind news are beautiful to Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What was the runner carrying over the mountains?”","“What did the watchmen do when they saw the good news coming?”","“Who did Scout run outside to tell about the baby hamsters?”"],"share":["When did somebody else’s good news lift a heavy day for you? Share that memory with your child.","How do you make your home a place where news gets shared gladly? Tell about a moment your family celebrated together.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry peace into every room they entered? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w2-n4":{"story":["Isaiah saw the Savior long before He came. Isaiah called Him a man of sorrows, One who knew exactly what sadness feels like.","Jesus was not afraid of sad things. Surely, Isaiah said, He has borne our griefs and carried our sorrows.","Isaiah said we are all like sheep who wander off, each one turning to his own way.","So Jesus gathered up the whole heavy load in His own gentle arms, because He loves us. He made everything right again.","When your heart feels heavy tonight, you do not have to carry it by yourself. Jesus already knows how it feels, and He is holding you close."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because carrying someone else’s sadness is the tenderest kind of love. In Willow’s world today: her little brother dropped his ice cream on the sidewalk and could not stop crying. Willow sat down beside him on the warm cement. She did not fix it or hurry him along; she just held his hand. Just like Jesus, who carried our sorrows, Willow learned that love sits with people in the sad.","feel":"Hug your stuffed animal close against your chest, like you are carrying it somewhere safe. Rock it slowly side to side. Can you show me how gently you can carry something?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus knew grief and sorrow, and He carried ours so we would not be alone."],["NAME","That heavy, hurting-inside feeling is called sorrow."],["FIND","When did your heart feel heavy, and who helped you carry it?"],["FORK","We can hide our sad away, or hand it to Jesus. Jesus gladly took ours."],["CALM","Lay both hands open on the blanket and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please hold my sad.’"]],"remember":"Jesus loved you so much that He carried your sadness before you were even born!","ask":["“What did Jesus carry for us?”","“What did Isaiah say we are like when we wander away?”","“What did Willow do when her brother dropped his ice cream?”"],"share":["When did someone carry a sorrow with you instead of trying to solve it? Share that with your child.","How do you sit with your child’s sadness without rushing it away? Tell about a moment you simply stayed beside them.","Did a grandparent or ancestor quietly bear burdens for other people? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w2-n5":{"story":["For a small moment, Heavenly Father said, I let you go. But with great mercies I will gather you back to Me.","With everlasting kindness I will have mercy on you, He said. His kindness had no ending at all.","Then Heavenly Father made the biggest promise of all. He said the mountains could pack up and depart.","The hills could be removed too. But My kindness will not depart from you, He said, and the covenant of My peace will stay.","Think of the biggest hill you have ever seen. Heavenly Father’s kindness for you will outlast every single one of them."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because some truths are best held quietly and turned over slowly. In Wren’s world today: Wren spilled juice across her mom’s clean floor and froze, waiting. Mom only handed her a towel and hummed while they wiped it up together. Wren thought about it all afternoon. Just like the people who heard that Heavenly Father’s kindness would never depart, Wren learned that real love stays when we make a mess.","feel":"Push your hands out wide like mountains, then let them float slowly away. Now wrap yourself in a hug, because kindness stays. Can you show me the mountains going, and the hug staying?","stones":[["SPOT","The people worried that Heavenly Father had left them behind for good."],["NAME","That worried, are-You-still-there feeling is called afraid."],["FIND","When were you scared that someone might stop loving you?"],["FORK","They could believe the fear, or believe the promise. They chose the promise."],["CALM","Squeeze both hands tight, then let them go slow. Whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, You stay.’"]],"remember":"Mountains can pack up and leave, but Heavenly Father’s kindness for you never will!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father promise to do with great mercies?”","“Which one can go away, the mountains or Heavenly Father’s kindness?”","“What did Mom hand to Wren after the juice spilled?”"],"share":["When did you feel far from Heavenly Father and then gathered back? Share that turning point with your child.","How do you show a child that love holds steady after a mistake? Tell about a moment you chose gentleness over correction.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep loving people who were hard to love? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w2-n6":{"story":["Seek the Lord while He may be found, Isaiah said. Call upon Him while He is near.","My thoughts are not your thoughts, Heavenly Father said. They are higher, the way the heavens are higher than the earth.","Rain and snow come down from heaven, and they do not return empty. They water the earth until it buds and gives seed and bread.","So shall My word be, Heavenly Father said. And He promised His people would go out with joy and be led forth with peace.","The mountains would break forth into singing, and every tree would clap its hands. Tonight the whole world is glad you are here, safe in your bed."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because seeking is how joy gets found. In Scout’s world today: Scout hunted all morning for his missing green boot. He looked under the beds, behind the doors, and inside the laundry basket. When it finally turned up in the coat closet, he stomped both boots down the hallway singing. Just like the people promised they would go out with joy, Scout learned that seeking ends in gladness.","feel":"Wiggle your fingers down like rain falling onto your blanket. Then clap your hands soft like the happy trees. Can you show me the rain, and then the trees clapping?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were promised they would go out with joy and be led with peace."],["NAME","That light, something-good-is-coming feeling is called hopeful."],["FIND","What are you looking forward to so much that it makes you wiggle?"],["FORK","They could wait quietly forever, or seek the Lord now. They sought Him."],["CALM","Wiggle your toes ten times, then hold them still. Whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, I seek You.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father sends His word down like rain, and it always grows something good!","ask":["“What did Isaiah say to do while Heavenly Father is near?”","“What did the trees do in Heavenly Father’s happy promise?”","“Where did Scout finally find his missing green boot?”"],"share":["When did an answer come only after you kept on seeking? Share that search with your child.","How do you help a child keep looking when nothing has turned up yet? Tell about a moment you searched for something together.","Did a grandparent or ancestor find real joy in ordinary days? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w2-n7":{"story":["Isaiah said Heavenly Father is the high and lofty One who lives forever. I dwell in the high and holy place, He said.","But Heavenly Father said He lives somewhere else too. He dwells with anyone who has a humble, honest heart.","He comes near that quiet heart to revive it. He lifts the humble spirit up again when it feels low and small.","Then Heavenly Father says, peace, peace to him that is far off, and to him that is near. And I will heal him.","Your heart is small and honest and just exactly right. Heavenly Father wants to live close beside it, tonight and always."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because telling the truth about yourself is its own kind of bravery. In Beau’s world today: Beau knocked a plant off the windowsill and swept the dirt under the couch. His stomach ached all through lunch. Finally he tugged his dad’s sleeve and told him the whole thing. Just like the humble heart Heavenly Father chose to dwell with, Beau learned that honesty makes room for peace.","feel":"Curl up small and quiet like a little seed. Then let your heart lift as you take one big breath in. Can you show me small and quiet, and then lifted up?","stones":[["SPOT","A humble heart feels low and small, and Heavenly Father draws near to it."],["NAME","That small, I-need-some-help feeling is called humble."],["FIND","When did you feel little inside and wish someone would come close?"],["FORK","We can puff up big, or be honest and small. Heavenly Father chose the humble."],["CALM","Fold your hands under your chin and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, here is my whole heart.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father lives in the highest place and also right beside your quiet heart!","ask":["“Where does Heavenly Father dwell besides the high and holy place?”","“What word did Heavenly Father say twice, to those far off and near?”","“What did Beau finally tell his dad about?”"],"share":["When did humility bring you nearer to Heavenly Father than strength ever did? Share that with your child.","How do you make honesty feel safe in your home? Tell about a moment your child told you something hard to say.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a quiet, unshowy humility? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n1":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people went without food and called it a fast. But their hearts stayed hard, and they wondered why the day never felt holy.","So Heavenly Father gave Isaiah a better fast to carry to them. Share your bread with the hungry. Open your door to the poor with nowhere to sleep.","Cover the one who is cold, Heavenly Father said. Do not hide away from your own family.","Then your light will break out like the morning, and your dark places will glow like noon. You will be a watered garden, a spring that never runs dry.","You have bread, and you have arms for hugging. Tomorrow you can give a little of both away, and that quiet light will come into your house too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because tenderness is the fast Heavenly Father chose. In Willow’s world today: Willow opens her lunch and finds two clementines. The girl beside her has none. Willow rolls one across the table without saying a word. Just like the people who shared their bread and watched their light rise, Willow learned that kindness is how a heart turns bright.","feel":"Hold your hands together like a little bowl, then open them slowly, as if you were handing warm bread to someone hungry. Can you show me how gently you would give it away?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah’s people felt empty inside even after their fasting day was over."],["NAME","That hollow, nothing-filled-me feeling is called emptiness."],["FIND","When has your tummy or your heart felt empty and wanted something warm?"],["FORK","Isaiah could soften his message or say it plainly. Isaiah said it plainly: share your bread."],["CALM","Rest a hand on your tummy, breathe out slow, and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, make my heart soft.’"]],"remember":"When you share what you have, your own light breaks out like the morning!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father ask the people to give to the hungry?”","“What happens to your light when you share?”","“What did Willow roll across the table to her friend?”"],"share":["When has giving something away lit something up inside you? Share that memory with your little one.","How do you teach small hands to give without turning it into a rule? Tell about a moment your child gave on their own.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep a table where hungry people were always welcome? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n2":{"story":["Isaiah’s people had a holy day, but they filled it with their own errands and their own loud words. It felt like every other day.","Heavenly Father asked Isaiah to teach them something quieter. Turn your feet away from your busy plans on My holy day, and call the Sabbath a delight.","Honor that day, Heavenly Father said. Do not spend it chasing your own ways, your own wants, your own words.","Then you will delight yourself in the Lord, He promised, and He will carry you high, like someone riding on the tops of the hills.","One day each week can feel different in your house too: slower songs, closer people, quieter hands. It is a gift with your name on it."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because a quiet day teaches what a loud day cannot. In Wren’s world today: Sunday morning, Wren’s shoes are on and the toy bin is calling. Wren sits down by the window instead and watches the light move across the floor. By the time the family sings, Wren is already still inside. Just like the people who learned to call the Sabbath a delight, Wren learned that quiet is not empty, it is full.","feel":"Let your arms go soft and heavy on the blanket, like a day with nothing at all to hurry for. Can you show me your quietest, restingest body?","stones":[["SPOT","The people’s holy day felt rushed and ordinary, crowded with their own errands."],["NAME","That too-full, no-room-left feeling is called hurry."],["FIND","When does your day get so busy that your heart cannot catch up?"],["FORK","They could fill the holy day with their own plans, or rest in it. Isaiah taught them to rest and delight."],["CALM","Let your shoulders drop all the way down and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, help my heart be still.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father saved one whole day just for being near Him, and He called it a delight!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father ask the people to call the Sabbath?”","“What did Heavenly Father promise to those who honored His holy day?”","“Where did Wren sit instead of playing on Sunday morning?”"],"share":["What does Sabbath delight, rather than Sabbath duty, feel like for you? Share a Sunday that felt that way.","How do you protect stillness for a busy little one? Tell about a moment your child chose quiet all on their own.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep the Sabbath in a way you can still feel? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n3":{"story":["The people were afraid that Heavenly Father had stopped listening. Their prayers seemed to rise and disappear like smoke.","So Isaiah told them the truth, plain and strong. Heavenly Father’s hand is not too short to save, and His ear is not too tired to hear.","It was the people who had turned away, Isaiah said, and the wrong things they had done stood between them like a cloud.","But clouds move. Isaiah promised something wonderful: the Redeemer would come to Zion, and to everyone who turned back to Heavenly Father.","If you ever feel far away tonight, you can simply turn around. His reach is long enough to find the corner where you are."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because turning back takes more courage than running. In Beau’s world today: Beau knocks a cup off the counter and hides behind the couch. His chest thumps for a long, long minute. Then he walks out, small and honest, and says, ‘That was me.’ Just like the people Isaiah asked to turn back to Heavenly Father, Beau learned that coming back is always the brave way home.","feel":"Stretch one arm out as far as it will go, then reach a little farther still. Heavenly Father’s reach never runs out. Can you show me your longest, farthest reach?","stones":[["SPOT","The people felt unheard, as though their prayers went nowhere at all."],["NAME","That far-away, is-anybody-listening feeling is called lonely."],["FIND","When have you called for someone and had to wait a long time to be heard?"],["FORK","They could stay turned away, or turn back. Isaiah asked them to turn back, and promised the Redeemer would come."],["CALM","Press both hands over your heart and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, I am turning back to You.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s hand is never too short to reach you!","ask":["“Was Heavenly Father’s hand too short to save His people?”","“Who did Isaiah promise would come to Zion?”","“What did Beau say when he came out from behind the couch?”"],"share":["When did prayer feel like it went nowhere, and what finally brought you back? Share that stretch of the road.","How do you make turning back easy for a child who has hidden? Tell about a moment your child owned something small.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep reaching for someone who had wandered off? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n4":{"story":["Isaiah called to his people like someone throwing open the curtains at dawn. ‘Arise, shine,’ he said, ‘for thy light is come.’","Darkness lay heavy over the whole earth. But the Lord would rise upon His people, Isaiah promised, and His glory would be seen upon them.","Others would come walking toward that brightness, Isaiah said, from far countries and even from thrones.","One day the sun will not be needed for daylight, nor the moon for night. The Lord Himself will be an everlasting light, and the days of mourning will be over.","Your little nightlight will go dark someday, but that light never will. Sleep now; morning is already on its way to you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because a seeker is first up to meet the light. In Scout’s world today: Scout wakes before anyone and finds the hallway grey and still. Instead of calling out, Scout tiptoes to the window to watch the sky turn pink at the bottom. When Mama comes, Scout whispers, ‘It came back!’ Just like the people told to arise and shine because their light had come, Scout learned that morning always keeps its promise.","feel":"Curl up small like the night, then rise slowly with your arms opening wide like the sun coming over a hill. Can you show me how a morning wakes up?","stones":[["SPOT","The people sat in darkness while a great light was already rising for them."],["NAME","That waiting-in-the-dark, watching feeling is called hoping."],["FIND","When have you waited in the dark for morning, or for someone to turn a light on?"],["FORK","They could keep sitting in the dark, or arise and shine. Isaiah called them up into the light."],["CALM","Open your hands like a sunrise, breathe in slow, and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank You for morning.’"]],"remember":"The light Heavenly Father sends never goes out, and it is already on its way to you!","ask":["“What did Isaiah tell the people to do when their light came?”","“Who will be the everlasting light, so the sun is not needed?”","“What did Scout see out the window before anyone else woke up?”"],"share":["When did light finally arrive after a long dark season for you? Share what that first morning felt like.","How do you help a child rise toward good instead of being pushed? Tell about a moment they got up on their own.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry light into hard rooms? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n5":{"story":["The Spirit of the Lord was upon Isaiah, and Heavenly Father sent him with good news for gentle, tired people.","He was sent to bind up hearts that were broken, and to tell everyone shut away that the doors would open.","He was sent to comfort all who mourned, and he carried three quiet gifts from Heavenly Father.","Beauty instead of ashes, oil of joy instead of mourning, a song of praise instead of a heavy spirit. Then the sad ones would stand like strong trees Heavenly Father had planted Himself.","If your heart feels heavy tonight, Heavenly Father knows the trade He loves best. Hand Him the ashes; He has been holding your beauty all along."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because comfort is her gift and her calling. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s block tower falls and her little brother sits down in the wreckage and cries. Willow does not rebuild it yet. She sits down in the pile beside him and pats his knee until his breathing slows. Just like Isaiah, sent to comfort everyone who mourned, Willow learned that sitting close is the first gift a sad heart needs.","feel":"Cup your hands as if you were holding grey ashes, then turn them over and shake them out into flower petals. Can you show me the trade Heavenly Father makes?","stones":[["SPOT","The people of Zion were mourning, with hearts that felt broken and heavy."],["NAME","That heavy, achy, hard-to-carry feeling is called sadness."],["FIND","When did your heart feel heavy, and who came and sat beside you?"],["FORK","They could stay sitting in the ashes, or take the gifts. Isaiah brought them beauty, joy, and praise."],["CALM","Cross your arms into a hug, rock gently, and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please carry my heavy.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father takes the ashes and hands back something beautiful!","ask":["“What was Isaiah sent to do for hearts that were broken?”","“What did Heavenly Father give the people instead of ashes?”","“What did Willow do when her brother cried in the fallen blocks?”"],"share":["Where has Heavenly Father traded you beauty for ashes? Share that exchange with your child.","How do you sit with a child’s sadness before fixing it? Tell about a moment you chose presence over repair.","Did a grandparent or ancestor turn hard years into something beautiful? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n6":{"story":["Isaiah prayed for his people, and he found gentle words for who they were. ‘O Lord, thou art our father,’ he said.","‘We are the clay, and thou our potter. We all are the work of thy hand.’","Clay cannot shape itself. It rests in the hands that hold it, soft and willing, until it becomes something useful and lovely.","Isaiah was saying that Heavenly Father works like that: patient, unhurried, never rough. His people belong to Him the way a bowl belongs to the hands that made it.","You are the work of His hands too, every finger and eyelash and idea. You are not finished yet, and you are already loved."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because a wonderer notices whose hands are holding her. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s playdough snake keeps breaking, and her hands go still with frustration. She watches Grandpa roll his own slowly instead of fast. Wren tries again, gentler this time, and it holds together. Just like Isaiah, who called Heavenly Father the potter and His people the clay, Wren learned that good things are made by patient hands.","feel":"Squeeze your hands into one soft ball, then let them open slowly, like clay resting in kind hands. Can you show me your softest, most restful hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah felt small before Heavenly Father, and safe in His hands anyway."],["NAME","That small-but-held, someone-has-me feeling is called trust."],["FIND","When has someone bigger carried something you could not carry alone?"],["FORK","Isaiah could argue with Heavenly Father or rest in Him. Isaiah prayed, ‘We are the clay, and Thou our potter.’"],["CALM","Cup your hands together, breathe out, and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, I am the work of Your hands.’"]],"remember":"You are the work of Heavenly Father’s hands, and He is not finished with you yet!","ask":["“In Isaiah’s prayer, who was the potter and who was the clay?”","“Whose hands did Isaiah say we are the work of?”","“Who showed Wren how to roll the playdough slowly?”"],"share":["Where is Heavenly Father still shaping you? Share the place you can feel His thumbprint.","How do you stay patient with a child mid- shaping? Tell about a moment you slowed down instead of pressing harder.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have patient, making hands? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n7":{"story":["Heavenly Father showed Isaiah something brand new: new heavens and a new earth, so good that the old sorrows would not even come to mind.","There would be no more sound of weeping there, He said, and no more crying. Only gladness, and a people made of joy.","People would build houses and live in them, and plant vineyards and eat the fruit themselves. Nothing good would be carried away.","The wolf and the lamb would eat side by side, and the lion would eat straw like a gentle ox. Nothing would hurt, and nothing would destroy, in all Heavenly Father’s holy mountain.","Before you finish asking, He said, I will answer. Picture the lamb leaning warm against the wolf, both safe, both sleepy, just like you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because wondering what comes next is how a seeker walks. In Scout’s world today: The neighbor’s big dog barks and Scout’s feet freeze on the sidewalk. Scout crouches down slowly, holds out one careful hand, and waits. The dog leans in, and Scout’s whole face opens up. Just like the lamb who will one day rest beside the wolf, Scout learned that peace can grow where you thought it never could.","feel":"Lay your hands side by side on the blanket, one the wolf and one the lamb, resting quietly together. Can you show me two hands that are not afraid of each other?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah heard a promise of a world where no one is hurt and no one is afraid."],["NAME","That safe-at-last, nothing-can-harm- me feeling is called peace."],["FIND","Where in your house do you feel the very safest?"],["FORK","Isaiah could stare at today’s troubles or at the promise. Isaiah carried the joyful promise to everyone."],["CALM","Lay one hand on your heart, breathe out slow, and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank You for peace.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is making a world where nothing hurts and nobody cries!","ask":["“Which two animals will eat side by side in the new earth?”","“What will the lion eat there instead?”","“What did Scout do when the big dog barked?”"],"share":["What would you gladly set down in a world with no more weeping? Share what you long to put away.","How do you speak hope to a child who is afraid? Tell about a moment your child chose courage over fear.","Did a grandparent or ancestor live as though that peace were already coming? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w4-n1":{"story":["Long ago a young man named Jeremiah heard a quiet voice. It was Heavenly Father, and He said, ‘Before I made you, I knew you.’","Jeremiah looked down at his own small hands. ‘But I am only a child,’ he whispered. ‘I do not know how to speak.’","Heavenly Father did not say, ‘Yes, you are too little.’ He said, ‘Do not say you are a child. Go where I send you.’","‘Do not be afraid,’ He told Jeremiah, ‘for I am with thee.’ The quiet inside Jeremiah’s chest grew warm and steady.","Heavenly Father knew you before you were born, too. He knew your laugh and your sleepy eyes. You are little tonight, and you are already His."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because it takes courage to trust you are enough when you feel very small. In Beau’s world today: Beau is the smallest one in his class, and today it was his turn to hold the heavy door. His hands shook a little. He held it anyway, and every friend walked through. Just like Jeremiah, who was young and went anyway, Beau learned that Heavenly Father sends small people to do good things.","feel":"Hold up both hands and look at them in the dark. Heavenly Father knew those hands before you were born. Now press them softly over your heart, where His warm words go. Can you show me your quiet, brave hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jeremiah felt far too small for something very big."],["NAME","That small-and-not-ready feeling is called unsure."],["FIND","When have you felt too little for something someone asked you to do?"],["FORK","He could hide and say nothing. Or he could go anyway. Jeremiah went anyway."],["CALM","Press one hand on your heart and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please walk with me.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father knew you and loved you before you ever took your first breath!","ask":["“What did Jeremiah say when Heavenly Father called him?”","“What did Heavenly Father promise when Jeremiah felt afraid?”","“What did Beau hold open for all his friends today?”"],"share":["When did you feel far too small for what you were asked to do, and Heavenly Father carried you through? Tell your child about that time.","How do you answer your child when they say ‘I can’t’? Tell about a moment you watched courage grow in them.","Did a grandparent or ancestor step forward before they felt ready? Describe that quiet courage to your child."]},"b10-w4-n2":{"story":["Jeremiah still felt small. He did not think he had one single good word tucked inside him.","Then a gentle touch came to Jeremiah’s mouth. ‘I have put my words in thy mouth,’ Heavenly Father said, soft as morning.","Jeremiah did not have to make the words himself. They were given to him, like warm bread placed into open hands.","Then Heavenly Father sent him out to build and to plant. It was like a gardener setting little trees into good ground.","When you do not know what to say, you can ask Heavenly Father. He is close to your mouth and your ear tonight. You will never have to find kind words all alone."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because tender words are a gift we are given, not something we must make alone. In Willow’s world today: a friend at the sand table was crying, and Willow had no idea what to say. She sat down close and stayed. Then the words came: ‘You can build with me.’ Just like Jeremiah, whose words were placed in his mouth, Willow learned that Heavenly Father sends kind words right when they are needed.","feel":"Touch your own lips with one fingertip, softly, the way morning light touches a window. Now whisper one kind word out into the dark room. Can you show me your very gentlest whisper?","stones":[["SPOT","Jeremiah felt empty, with no words to give at all."],["NAME","That nothing-to-say feeling is called speechless."],["FIND","When did you want to help somebody but not know what to say?"],["FORK","He could stay silent and turn away. Or open his mouth and trust. Jeremiah opened it."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please put kind words in me.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father hands us the words we need at the very moment we need them!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father touch when Jeremiah had no words?”","“What did Heavenly Father send Jeremiah out to do?”","“What did Willow finally say to her friend at the sand table?”"],"share":["When did the right words come to you that you know were not your own? Share that moment with your child.","How do you help your child find words for big feelings? Tell about a time their kindness surprised you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a gift for saying the right thing? Describe that gentleness to your child."]},"b10-w4-n3":{"story":["One quiet day Heavenly Father asked Jeremiah a question. ‘Jeremiah, what do you see?’","Jeremiah looked, and then he looked again. ‘I see a branch from an almond tree,’ he said.","The almond tree is the very first tree to wake up in spring. While the other trees are still sleeping, it opens white blossoms like little stars.","‘Thou hast well seen,’ Heavenly Father told him. ‘I will hurry to do the thing that I have promised.’","Heavenly Father never forgets a promise. When He says a thing, He is already watching over it, the way He is watching over you tonight."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because looking closely is how we find what Heavenly Father is quietly showing us. In Scout’s world today: Scout kept a bare twig in a jar on the windowsill and checked it every morning. Most mornings, nothing. Then one morning, one tiny green bud, and he ran to show everybody. Just like Jeremiah, who saw the waking branch, Scout learned that Heavenly Father keeps every promise, even slow-looking ones.","feel":"Peek out from under your blanket with just your eyes, like a bud peeking out at spring. Now open your hands slowly, slowly, like a blossom in the morning sun. Can you show me how a flower wakes up?","stones":[["SPOT","Jeremiah felt a small bright spark when he looked closely."],["NAME","That wide-awake, look-again feeling is called wonder."],["FIND","What is something you love to look at closely, over and over again?"],["FORK","He could glance away and see nothing. Or look carefully. Jeremiah looked, and he saw."],["CALM","Blink slowly three times and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, help me see good things.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father watches over every promise He makes, and He has never once forgotten one!","ask":["“What kind of branch did Jeremiah see?”","“Which tree wakes up first when spring comes?”","“What did Scout finally find on his twig one morning?”"],"share":["When did a small sign remind you Heavenly Father was still working on something? Share that sign with your child.","How do you teach a small child to wait on a promise? Tell about a time your family waited together and it came.","Did a grandparent or ancestor notice small beauties others walked past? Describe that noticing to your child."]},"b10-w4-n4":{"story":["Heavenly Father had something to show Jeremiah. ‘Go down to the potter’s house,’ He said, ‘and there I will speak to you.’","So Jeremiah went. The potter’s wheel turned round and round, and wet clay rose up under two steady hands.","One pot did not come out right. It leaned and slumped and lost its shape right there in the potter’s hands.","The potter did not throw the clay away. He gathered it up and made it again into another good vessel, just as it seemed good to him.","‘As the clay is in the potter’s hand, so are ye in mine hand,’ said Heavenly Father. You are in kind, patient hands tonight, being made into something lovely."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because quiet truths often hide inside ordinary work, waiting for someone to sit still and watch. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s playdough snowman flopped sideways, and her lip began to wobble. Her grandma only smiled and rolled it back into a smooth round ball. They started again, and it turned out better. Just like the potter who made the clay again, Wren learned that Heavenly Father remakes what bends.","feel":"Hold both hands together like a bowl and turn them round and round, slow as a potter’s wheel. Now let them settle open and soft on your tummy. Can you show me your gentle potter hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The clay went crooked, and nobody got angry about it."],["NAME","That safe, not-thrown-away feeling is called hopeful."],["FIND","When have you made a mistake, and someone helped you begin again?"],["FORK","The potter could toss the clay out. Or shape it again. He shaped it again."],["CALM","Squeeze your fists tight, open them slowly, and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, shape me.’"]],"remember":"You are never a mistake to throw away, but clay resting in the kindest hands there are!","ask":["“Where did Heavenly Father send Jeremiah to watch and learn?”","“What did the potter do when the clay went crooked?”","“What did Wren’s grandma do with the floppy playdough snowman?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father reshape something in you instead of setting you aside? Share that remaking with your child.","How do you respond when your child’s work collapses? Tell about a moment you helped them start over.","Did a grandparent or ancestor rebuild after something fell apart? Describe that patience to your child."]},"b10-w4-n5":{"story":["Heavenly Father gave Jeremiah words to call out toward the north. His children had wandered a long way from home.","‘Come back,’ the words said. ‘I am merciful. I will not keep my anger forever.’","He promised to gather them, one from a city and two from a family. He would carry them home again to Zion.","And He would give them shepherds after His own heart. They would feed His children with knowing and with understanding.","Heavenly Father counts His children one by one, and He knows exactly where you are tonight. If you ever wander, the door stays open. ‘Come home’ is His softest word."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because mercy is the tenderness that leaves the door standing open. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s little brother knocked her block tower flat and ran to hide behind the couch. Willow looked at the mess for a long, quiet minute. Then she called out softly, ‘You can come out. We can build it again.’ Just like Heavenly Father calling His children home, Willow learned that love does not stay angry forever.","feel":"Cup your hands around your mouth and call very softly across the dark room: ‘Come home.’ Now open both arms as wide as they will go for a welcoming hug. Can you show me your come- home arms?","stones":[["SPOT","The children were far away, and Heavenly Father wanted them back."],["NAME","That far-from-home feeling is called homesick."],["FIND","When have you felt far from someone you love and wanted to go back?"],["FORK","He could stay angry forever. Or keep calling them home. Heavenly Father kept calling."],["CALM","Open your arms wide, then hug yourself and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, I am coming home.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father will not stay angry forever, and He keeps a light burning for every single one of us!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father ask His children to do?”","“Who did He promise to send to feed and care for them?”","“What did Willow say to her brother hiding behind the couch?”"],"share":["When did someone welcome you back without a lecture? Share how that mercy felt with your child.","How quickly do you set anger down with your children? Tell about a moment you chose to reopen the door.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep loving someone who wandered? Describe that steady welcome to your child."]},"b10-w4-n6":{"story":["Heavenly Father gave Jeremiah a picture to share with everybody. ‘Blessed is the one who trusts in the Lord,’ the words said.","‘He shall be as a tree planted by the waters.’ Jeremiah said it out loud, so that everyone could hear.","That tree spreads out her roots beside the river. Deep down where nobody can see, she is drinking all day long.","So the tree is not worried when the hot days come. Her leaves stay green, and she goes right on giving fruit.","Trusting Heavenly Father is just like that. Even on your hardest day, something quiet inside you keeps drinking. Your roots are in a river that never dries up."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because trusting quietly, when nothing looks easy, is its own kind of brave. In Beau’s world today: it was a hard day, and rain shut down the whole playground. His best friend stayed home sick. Beau still fed the class fish and still sang at circle time, doing the good small things anyway. Just like the tree with roots down in the river, Beau learned that trusting Heavenly Father keeps us green on dry days.","feel":"Stretch both arms up tall like branches, then wiggle your toes down deep like roots reaching for water. Hold still and breathe slow, the way a tree stands in the sun. Can you show me your strongest roots?","stones":[["SPOT","The tree stayed green even when the hot, dry days came."],["NAME","That steady, not-worried feeling is called trust."],["FIND","Where do you like to go when your day feels hard and dry?"],["FORK","We can trust only ourselves and grow thirsty, or trust Heavenly Father. Jeremiah trusted Him."],["CALM","Press your feet flat like roots and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, I am trusting you.’"]],"remember":"Roots that reach all the way down to the river are never afraid of a hot day!","ask":["“What is the person who trusts Heavenly Father like?”","“What did the tree’s roots find down under the ground?”","“What did Beau keep doing on his hard, rainy day?”"],"share":["What keeps your roots in the water during a dry season? Share one of those quiet practices with your child.","How do you stay steady for your children on hard days? Tell about a day you kept going anyway.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay green through a long dry season? Describe that endurance to your child."]},"b10-w4-n7":{"story":["Some days were tired days for Jeremiah. People did not want the words he carried, and he thought about staying quiet.","But the words would not sit still. They felt like a small warm fire tucked away inside his bones.","‘His word was in mine heart as a burning fire shut up in my bones,’ Jeremiah said. He tried and tried to hold it in, and he could not.","So he opened his mouth and sang instead. ‘Sing unto the Lord,’ he said. ‘Praise ye the Lord.’","When something true and good is tucked inside you, it wants to come out. Let it out, little one. Heavenly Father keeps a warm light burning in you, even on tired days."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because a true thing you have found is almost impossible to keep to yourself. In Scout’s world today: Scout learned a brand new song at preschool and decided to save it as a surprise. He lasted until the car door shut. Then out it came bubbling, all the verses at once, and then again. Just like Jeremiah with the fire in his bones, Scout learned that good news from Heavenly Father wants to be sung.","feel":"Put one hand flat on your chest and feel it go warm and up-and-down. Now hum one little tune, quiet as a candle flame in the dark. Can you show me the song hiding inside you?","stones":[["SPOT","Jeremiah felt tired and quiet, but something warm would not settle down."],["NAME","That warm, can’t-keep-it-in feeling is called eager."],["FIND","What good news made you want to run and tell somebody right away?"],["FORK","He could swallow the words and stay silent. Or he could sing. Jeremiah sang."],["CALM","Hum one soft note and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank you for the light in me.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father tucks a warm little light inside you that no tired day can ever put out!","ask":["“How did Heavenly Father’s words feel inside Jeremiah?”","“What did Jeremiah do instead of staying quiet?”","“Where did Scout’s brand new song finally burst out?”"],"share":["When did something true burn in you until you finally said it out loud? Share that moment with your child.","How do you make room for your child’s excitement at the end of a long day? Tell about a time you listened anyway.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a testimony they could not keep quiet? Describe that fire to your child."]},"b9-w1-n1":{"story":["Long ago, a man named David wanted to thank Heavenly Father with his whole heart. So he sang a happy song: “Bless Heavenly Father, O my soul! I will remember all the good things He does!”","David remembered how kind Heavenly Father is. He forgives us when we’re sorry. He helps us feel better when we’re sad. He fills our lives with good things, like a warm hug that never ends.","“Heavenly Father loves us like the very best daddy,” David sang. “As high as the sky is above the earth, that’s how big His love is for us!”","And when we say we’re sorry, Heavenly Father takes our mistakes far, far away — as far as the sunrise is from the sunset. He remembers them no more.","Heavenly Father’s love is so big and so gentle. No matter what, He loves you with His whole heart, all the way to the sky and back."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she feels how gentle and forgiving love can be. In Willow’s world today: Willow spilled her juice all over the floor and felt scared she was in trouble. But Mom just gave her a hug and said, “It’s okay — let’s clean it up together.” Willow’s worried heart melted into a happy one. Just like David sang about Heavenly Father’s kind, forgiving love, Willow felt how big and gentle that love can be.","feel":"Let’s bless Heavenly Father like David! Wrap your arms around yourself in a big hug, then stretch them wa-a-ay up high to the sky. His love is bigger than we can even reach! Can you show me how high His love goes?","stones":[["SPOT","David felt thankful and full of love for Heavenly Father."],["NAME","That warm, cared-for feeling is called loved."],["FIND","When do you feel loved and safe, like someone is taking good care of you?"],["FORK","We can forget the good things — or remember them and thank Heavenly Father. David chose to remember every one."],["CALM","Give yourself a hug and whisper, “Thank you for loving me, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loves you higher than the sky and takes your mistakes far away when you’re sorry! You are so loved, sweet one. What is one good thing Heavenly Father has done for you that you can thank Him for?","ask":["“What did David want to remember about Heavenly Father?”","“How big is Heavenly Father’s love for us?”","“What is one good thing Heavenly Father has done for you that you can thank Him for?”"],"share":["When have you felt Heavenly Father’s forgiveness lift a weight off your heart? Share how it felt to be free.","How do you remind yourself to “count the good things” when life feels heavy? Tell your child your practice.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a thankful, praising heart? Describe how their gratitude blessed the family."]},"b9-w1-n2":{"story":["There once was a person who felt sad and afraid. Big troubles were all around, and he didn’t know what to do. So he did the best thing of all — he called out to Heavenly Father.","“Please help me, Heavenly Father!” he prayed. And guess what? Heavenly Father heard every single word.","Heavenly Father listened close and helped him. His sadness turned to gladness, and his heart felt calm again. “I love Heavenly Father,” he sang, “because He hears me when I call!”","The person was so thankful. He decided he would keep praying and keep loving Heavenly Father for the rest of his life.","Whenever you talk to Heavenly Father, He hears you too — every whisper, every prayer. You are never, ever alone."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he trusts Heavenly Father to hear him when things feel hard. In Beau’s world today: Beau got lost from Dad for a scary minute at the busy store. His heart pounded, but he remembered to whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me,” and stay put. Soon Dad came around the corner! “Heavenly Father heard me,” Beau said. Just like the person who called out and was heard, Beau learned that Heavenly Father listens the moment we call.","feel":"Let’s call out to Heavenly Father! Cup your hands around your mouth and softly call, “I love You, Heavenly Father!” Now put your hand on your ear — He always hears. Can you show me your best listening ears?","stones":[["SPOT","The person felt sad and scared, then heard and helped."],["NAME","That someone-is-listening feeling is called heard."],["FIND","When has telling someone your worry helped you feel better?"],["FORK","A worried feeling can stay stuck inside — or we can call out to Heavenly Father, who hears. The person chose to pray, and help came."],["CALM","Take a slow breath and whisper, “Heavenly Father, You hear me.”"]],"remember":"Whenever you talk to Heavenly Father, He hears every word — even a tiny whisper! He loves to listen to you. What would you like to tell Heavenly Father tonight?","ask":["“What did the person do when he felt sad and afraid?”","“What did Heavenly Father do when he called?”","“What would you like to tell Heavenly Father tonight?”"],"share":["When did you cry out to Heavenly Father and feel truly heard? Share that answered prayer.","How do you teach your child that no prayer is too small for Heavenly Father? Tell about a time He heard you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a story of a prayer that was answered? Describe how Heavenly Father heard them."]},"b9-w1-n3":{"story":["One morning, a happy song-writer woke up and looked at the brand-new day. The sun was shining, the birds were singing, and his heart felt full of joy.","“This is the day that Heavenly Father made!” he sang. “I will be happy and glad in it!”","He knew that every single day is a gift from Heavenly Father. Some days are big and exciting; some days are quiet and small. But every day is made by Him.","“Thank You, Heavenly Father, for You are so good!” the person sang. “Your love lasts forever and ever!”","When you wake up tomorrow, you can remember: this new day is a gift, made just for you by Heavenly Father who loves you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout finds something wonderful in every new day. In Scout’s world today: it was a plain, rainy morning with no big plans. But Scout said, “I wonder what good things today will bring?” and went looking — and found a puddle to splash, a rainbow after lunch, and a cozy story with Grandma. Just like the song-writer who was glad for the day Heavenly Father made, Scout discovered that every day holds a gift when we look for it.","feel":"Let’s welcome the new day! Stretch your arms up in a big yawn, then open them wide like the morning sun coming up. “This is the day Heavenly Father made!” Can you show me your happiest good-morning stretch?","stones":[["SPOT","The song-writer felt happy and glad for the new day."],["NAME","That bright, morning-happy feeling is called glad."],["FIND","What makes you feel glad when you wake up in the morning?"],["FORK","We can grumble that a day is boring — or find the gift Heavenly Father put in it. The song- writer chose to be glad."],["CALM","Take a happy breath and whisper, “Thank You for today, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Every new day is a gift made just for you by Heavenly Father! You can wake up glad and look for the good in it. What is one thing you’re excited to do tomorrow?","ask":["“Who made this brand-new day?”","“What did the song-writer say he would be?”","“What is one thing you’re excited to do tomorrow?”"],"share":["How do you choose gladness on ordinary or hard days? Share a morning that turned around.","What small daily gift do you want your child to learn to notice? Tell why it matters to you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor greet each day with joy and gratitude? Describe their sunny spirit."]},"b9-w1-n4":{"story":["The longest song in all the Psalms is about something very special: Heavenly Father’s words, the scriptures. The song-writer loved them so much!","“Your words are like a lamp for my feet,” he sang, “and a bright light for my path.” A lamp helps us see where to walk in the dark.","When we don’t know what to do, Heavenly Father’s words show us the way — just like a little light helps us take the next safe step.","The song-writer even said, “I keep Your words safe inside my heart.” He learned them and remembered them, like hiding a treasure where it can never be lost.","You can keep Heavenly Father’s words in your heart too. When you listen to scripture stories, you’re filling your heart with a light that helps you always know the way."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she treasures Heavenly Father’s words in her heart. In Wren’s world today: Wren couldn’t decide whether to tattle or be kind when her friend made a little mistake. Then she remembered a scripture story about being gentle, and it lit up the right thing to do — like a little lamp. She chose kindness. Just like the song-writer whose path was lit by Heavenly Father’s words, Wren found that the scriptures show us the way.","feel":"Let’s light our little lamp! Hold your hands like you’re carrying a lantern out in front of you, and take slow, careful steps. The light shows the way! Can you walk your safe little path with your lamp?","stones":[["SPOT","The song-writer felt safe and sure, because Heavenly Father’s words showed the way."],["NAME","That I-know-the-way feeling is called guided."],["FIND","When has knowing what to do helped you feel safe and calm?"],["FORK","In the dark we can stumble and guess — or follow Heavenly Father’s light. The song-writer chose to let His words guide him."],["CALM","Hold your hands like a lamp, breathe slow, and think of a favorite scripture story."]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s words are like a little lamp that shows you the way — and you can keep them safe in your heart! What is one thing scripture stories have taught you to do?","ask":["“What did the song-writer say Heavenly Father’s words were like?”","“Where did he keep Heavenly Father’s words safe?”","“What is one thing scripture stories have taught you to do?”"],"share":["When did a scripture light your path in a moment of not-knowing? Share the verse and the choice.","How do you “hide His word in your heart” as a family? Tell about a passage you love.","Did a grandparent or ancestor treasure the scriptures? Describe how their love of Heavenly Father’s word guided them."]},"b9-w1-n5":{"story":["One song-writer thought about something amazing: Heavenly Father knows everything about him! He sang a wondering song about it.","“Heavenly Father, You know when I sit down and when I stand up. You know my thoughts and every word before I say it. You are always with me!”","He knew that wherever he went — up high or far away — Heavenly Father was right there, watching over him with love.","“You made me,” he sang, “and I am wonderfully made!” Every part of us — our fingers, our smiles, our giggles — was carefully made by Heavenly Father.","Isn’t that wonderful? Heavenly Father knows you all the way through, and He made you just right. You are precious and special to Him."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she loves to wonder at how amazing she’s made. In Wren’s world today: Wren looked at her own hand and wondered, “How did Heavenly Father make my fingers wiggle and my heart go thump?” She felt so special knowing He made every part of her on purpose. Just like the song-writer who marveled that he was wonderfully made, Wren discovered that Heavenly Father made her just right.","feel":"Let’s show how well Heavenly Father knows us! Wiggle your fingers (He made those!), pat your head (He knows your thoughts!), and point to your smile (He made that too!). Can you show me all the wonderful parts He made?","stones":[["SPOT","The song-writer felt amazed and special, knowing Heavenly Father made and knew him."],["NAME","That treasured, one-of-a-kind feeling is called special."],["FIND","When do you feel really special and loved just for being you?"],["FORK","We can think we’re just ordinary — or remember Heavenly Father made us wonderfully. The song-writer chose to feel how special he was."],["CALM","Put a hand on your heart, breathe slow, and whisper, “Heavenly Father made me special.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father knows everything about you and made you wonderfully — there is no one else exactly like you! How does it feel to know Heavenly Father made you just right?","ask":["“What does Heavenly Father know about us?”","“How did Heavenly Father make each of us?”","“How does it feel to know Heavenly Father made you just right?”"],"share":["When did you feel deeply known and loved by Heavenly Father? Share that assurance.","How do you help your child feel treasured exactly as they are? Tell about a moment you felt His notice.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a quiet confidence that Heavenly Father knew and loved them? Describe it."]},"b9-w1-n6":{"story":["Heavenly Father loves families very much. One of the Psalms is a happy song all about home and children.","“Unless Heavenly Father helps build the house, the builders work for nothing,” the song says. That means Heavenly Father is the one who helps our family and home grow strong and happy.","Then the song says something sweet: “Children are a gift from Heavenly Father!” Every child — including YOU — is a special present He gives to a family.","The song paints a happy picture: a mom and dad and their little ones gathered close, like young plants growing round a table full of love.","Your family is a gift, and you are a gift too! Heavenly Father helps your home be a place of love, where you are treasured every single day."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she cherishes the people in her home. In Willow’s world today: at dinner, Willow looked around at everyone laughing together and felt her heart go warm. “I’m so glad you’re my family,” she said, and gave her little brother a hug — even though he’d been silly all day. Just like the song about children being a gift from Heavenly Father, Willow felt how precious her family is.","feel":"Let’s make a cozy family circle! Reach your arms out wide like they’re wrapping all around your family, then pull them into a big squeeze-hug. Home is full of love! Can you give a great big family hug?","stones":[["SPOT","The family in the song felt happy and blessed to be together."],["NAME","That safe, glad-to-belong feeling is called treasured."],["FIND","When does your family make you feel happy and loved?"],["FORK","We can take our family for granted — or thank Heavenly Father for the gift of them. The song chose to see family as a blessing."],["CALM","Give a family member a hug and whisper, “Thank You for my family, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Your family is a gift from Heavenly Father — and so are you! He helps your home be full of love. Who in your family are you thankful for tonight?","ask":["“Who helps build a happy home?”","“What does the song say children are?”","“Who in your family are you thankful for tonight?”"],"share":["When have you felt your family was a sacred gift from Heavenly Father? Share that tender awareness.","How do you invite Heavenly Father to help “build your house”? Tell about a way He’s strengthened your home.","Did a grandparent or ancestor build a home of faith and love? Describe the family blessing they began."]},"b9-w1-n7":{"story":["The very last songs in the Psalms are the happiest of all! They shout, “Praise Heavenly Father!” over and over with joy.","The song calls to everyone and everything: “Sun and moon, praise Him! Shining stars, praise Him! Mountains and trees, praise Him!”","Even the animals join in! “Whales in the sea, birds in the sky, little bugs and big lions — praise Heavenly Father!” All of creation says thank You to the One who made them.","And then the very last line: “Let EVERYTHING that breathes praise Heavenly Father!” That means you too — with singing, clapping, and a happy, thankful heart.","The whole world is like one big, joyful song to Heavenly Father. And you get to be part of it! Isn’t that wonderful?"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to notice everything praising Heavenly Father. In Scout’s world today: on a walk, Scout listened to the birds chirping, the wind whooshing the trees, and a puppy’s happy bark. “It’s like the whole world is singing to Heavenly Father!” Scout said, and started humming a thank-You song too. Just like the psalm that calls everything to praise Him, Scout discovered that all creation — and Scout — can join the joyful song.","feel":"Let’s join the whole world’s song! Clap your hands, stomp your feet, and cheer, “Praise Heavenly Father!” Now take a big breath — you have breath, so you can praise Him too! Can you make your most joyful praising sound?","stones":[["SPOT","All of creation feels full of joy and praise for Heavenly Father."],["NAME","That bursting, happy-hearted feeling is called joyful."],["FIND","When do you feel so happy you just want to sing or shout?"],["FORK","Big joy can stay quiet inside — or bubble out in praise. All creation lets its joy sing out to Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Take a happy breath and hum one joyful note to Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"Everything that breathes can praise Heavenly Father — and that means YOU! You can praise Him with singing, clapping, and a thankful heart. What is your favorite happy way to praise Heavenly Father?","ask":["“Who does the song say should praise Heavenly Father?”","“Since you can breathe, what can you do?”","“What is your favorite happy way to praise Heavenly Father?”"],"share":["When has all creation lifted your heart to praise Heavenly Father? Share a moment of wonder.","How does your family make joyful praise a habit? Tell about a time of singing or celebrating together.","Did a grandparent or ancestor overflow with praise for Heavenly Father? Describe the joy of their worship."]},"b9-w2-n1":{"story":["A wise man named Solomon wrote down lots of good advice to help us live happy lives. We call his sayings Proverbs. One of the very best is about trusting Heavenly Father.","“Trust in Heavenly Father with ALL your heart,” Solomon wrote. “Don’t try to figure everything out by yourself.”","Sometimes we don’t know which way to go or what to do. That’s okay! Solomon said, “Ask Heavenly Father, and He will show you the right path.”","It’s like walking with someone who knows the way. We don’t have to see the whole road — we just hold His hand and take the next step.","When you trust Heavenly Father with your whole heart, He leads you on good paths, step by step. You can trust Him, even when you can’t see what’s ahead."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he trusts Heavenly Father even when he can’t see the whole way. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s family went on a hike, and the trail wound up a hill where Beau couldn’t see the top. His legs felt unsure. But Dad said, “Just take the next step — I know the way.” Beau trusted and kept going, and the view at the top was amazing! Just like Solomon said to trust Heavenly Father to direct our paths, Beau learned to trust the One who knows the way.","feel":"Let’s follow the leader! Hold out your hand like you’re holding Heavenly Father’s, and take slow, trusting steps forward. He knows the way! Can you walk your path, trusting your guide?","stones":[["SPOT","When we don’t know the way, we might feel unsure — but we can choose to trust."],["NAME","That I-don’t-know — but-I’ll-trust feeling is called trusting."],["FIND","When have you had to trust a grown-up who knew the way, even when you didn’t?"],["FORK","Not knowing can make us worry and freeze — or we can trust Heavenly Father and take the next step. Solomon taught us to trust Him."],["CALM","Take a slow breath and whisper, “I trust You to lead me, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"When you don’t know which way to go, you can trust Heavenly Father with your whole heart — He will lead you, step by step! What is something you can trust Heavenly Father to help you with?","ask":["“How much should we trust Heavenly Father?”","“What does Heavenly Father do when we trust Him?”","“What is something you can trust Heavenly Father to help you with?”"],"share":["When did trusting Heavenly Father — over your own understanding — lead you rightly? Share the story.","How do you model “leaning not on your own understanding” for your child? Tell about a step of faith.","Did a grandparent or ancestor follow Heavenly Father’s direction into the unknown? Describe their trusting heart."]},"b9-w2-n2":{"story":["Wise Solomon wanted everyone to have something wonderful called wisdom. Wisdom means knowing the good and right thing to do.","“Look for wisdom like you’re searching for hidden treasure!” Solomon said. “Dig for it, hunt for it — it’s more precious than gold and shiny jewels!”","How do we find this treasure? Solomon said to listen to Heavenly Father, and to listen to the wise grown-ups who love us, like our mom and dad.","“Pay attention, my child,” he wrote. “Keep my good words close, and you will grow wise and happy.”","Every time you listen and learn, you’re finding a little more treasure. Heavenly Father loves to help you grow wise, one good lesson at a time."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to search out good and wonderful things. In Scout’s world today: Scout wanted to build the tallest sandcastle but didn’t know how. Instead of giving up, Scout asked Grandpa, who knew a clever trick — wet sand sticks better! Scout listened, tried it, and built a mighty castle. Just like Solomon said to hunt for wisdom like treasure, Scout discovered that listening to wise helpers is like finding gold.","feel":"Let’s hunt for treasure! Put your hand above your eyes and “search” all around, then dig-dig-dig with your hands and — “I found it!” — hold up the sparkly treasure of wisdom. Can you show me your excited treasure-hunting face?","stones":[["SPOT","Looking for wisdom, we can feel curious and eager to learn."],["NAME","That want-to-know-more feeling is called curious."],["FIND","When have you felt curious and excited to learn something new?"],["FORK","We can shrug and not bother — or hunt for wisdom like treasure. Solomon taught us to search for it eagerly."],["CALM","Take a slow breath and whisper, “Help me grow wise, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Wisdom is a treasure worth hunting for — and you find it by listening to Heavenly Father and the wise people who love you! What is something wise you learned from a grown-up you love?","ask":["“What did Solomon say to look for like treasure?”","“How do we find the treasure of wisdom?”","“What is something wise you learned from a grown-up you love?”"],"share":["When did seeking wisdom — over quick or easy answers — bless your life? Share it.","How do you nurture a love of learning and listening in your child? Tell about a treasure you’ve found.","Did a grandparent or ancestor pass down wisdom you still treasure? Describe the gift of their counsel."]},"b9-w2-n3":{"story":["Wise Solomon knew that the words we say are very powerful. They can make a heart happy or a heart hurt.","“A soft, kind answer makes angry feelings go away,” Solomon taught. “But mean, grumpy words just make things worse.”","Imagine two friends. One shouts, “You broke it!” — and everyone gets upset. But if that friend says softly, “It’s okay, let’s fix it together,” the mad feelings melt like ice in the sun.","Soft words are gentle words. Kind words are healing words. They help everyone feel calm and loved.","Heavenly Father can help you choose soft, kind words — even when you feel a little grumpy. Gentle words make the whole world happier."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because gentle words are her superpower. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s brother knocked over her block tower and she felt her face go hot and mad. She almost yelled — but instead she took a breath and said softly, “That’s okay, will you help me build it again?” Her brother smiled and helped, and the mad feeling floated away. Just like Solomon taught that a soft answer turns anger away, Willow found that gentle words make peace.","feel":"Let’s practice soft words! First, make a loud grumpy face and shake your fists — grr! Now take a slow breath, soften your face, and whisper, “It’s okay.” Can you feel how a soft answer calms us down?","stones":[["SPOT","When something goes wrong, we might feel angry — but a soft answer helps us feel calm."],["NAME","That grumpy, hot-inside feeling is called angry."],["FIND","When have you felt angry and wanted to say a not-nice word?"],["FORK","An angry feeling can burst out in mean words — or we can breathe and choose a soft, kind answer. Solomon taught us to choose gentle words."],["CALM","Take a slow breath, soften your voice, and say, “I can use kind words.”"]],"remember":"When you feel grumpy, you can choose soft, kind words — and watch the angry feelings melt away! Gentle words make hearts happy. When could you use a soft answer this week?","ask":["“What does a soft, kind answer do to angry feelings?”","“What do mean, grumpy words do?”","“When could you use a soft answer this week?”"],"share":["When did a soft answer defuse anger and bless a relationship? Share that moment.","How do you coach your child (and yourself) toward gentle words in hot moments? Tell what helps.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a gift for peacemaking words? Describe their gentle way."]},"b9-w2-n4":{"story":["Near the end of Proverbs is a beautiful song about a wonderful woman — a mom who loved her family with all her heart.","She worked hard with happy hands. She made yummy food, cared for her home, and helped people who needed her.","She was kind and wise. When she spoke, gentle and good words came out, and she wasn’t afraid of hard work or new days.","The best part? Her children loved her so much. “Her little ones stand up and call her blessed,” the song says. That means they were so thankful for her!","Heavenly Father gives us loving people — moms, dads, grandmas, grandpas — who work hard to care for us. We can thank them, and love them, and help with our own two hands too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she loves to care for others like the kind woman in the song. In Willow’s world today: Willow noticed Mom was tired after a long day. So Willow used her helping hands — she put her toys away without being asked and brought Mom a pretend cup of tea. Mom’s face lit up. “Thank you, my helper!” Just like the loving woman whose family called her blessed, Willow learned that caring hands make a home warm.","feel":"Let’s use loving, helping hands! Pretend to stir a pot, fold a blanket, and give a pat on the back. Now clap for the people who care for you! Can you show me how YOU could help with your hands tomorrow?","stones":[["SPOT","The family in the song felt thankful and full of love for the kind woman."],["NAME","That warm, thank-you feeling is called grateful."],["FIND","Who works hard to take care of you, and how do they help?"],["FORK","We can forget to say thank you — or notice and love the people who care for us. The children chose to call her blessed."],["CALM","Put a hand on your heart and whisper, “Thank You for the people who love me, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father gives you loving people who work hard to care for you — and you can thank them AND help with your own hands! Who could you thank and help tomorrow?","ask":["“How did the woman use her hands?”","“What did her children call her?”","“Who could you thank and help tomorrow?”"],"share":["Who has been a “Proverbs 31” presence of loving strength in your life? Share their example.","How do you help your child notice and honor those who serve them? Tell about a way you say thanks.","Did a grandparent or ancestor pour out love through hard, quiet work? Describe their blessed hands."]},"b9-w2-n5":{"story":["A wise teacher once wrote a thoughtful book called Ecclesiastes. In it, he shared a gentle truth: there is a right time for everything.","“There’s a time to plant seeds and a time to pick the fruit,” he said. “A time to laugh and a time to cry. A time to hug and a time to rest.”","Heavenly Father made the world with seasons and times. Winter comes, then spring. Morning comes, then night. Everything has its own special time.","Some days are for playing and giggling. Some days are for resting and being quiet. Some feelings come and go, and that’s okay — each one has its time.","Heavenly Father is with us in every season and every time. We can trust Him whether we’re laughing or crying, busy or still. He holds all our times in His loving hands."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she thinks about the right time for things. In Wren’s world today: Wren wanted to play outside, but it was pouring rain. Instead of fussing, she wondered, “Maybe this is a time to stay cozy inside.” So she read books and built a fort, and when the sun came out later, it was the perfect time to splash in puddles. Just like the wise teacher said there’s a time for everything, Wren learned to trust each season’s time.","feel":"Let’s move through the times! Pretend to plant a tiny seed, then reach up like it’s growing tall. Now give a big laugh — “ha ha!” — then a slow, calm breath. Can you show me a time to jump and a time to be still?","stones":[["SPOT","The wise teacher felt calm, knowing everything has its right time."],["NAME","That settled, it’s-all-okay feeling is called peaceful."],["FIND","When did waiting for the right time make something even better?"],["FORK","We can fuss when it’s not the time we want — or trust that Heavenly Father knows the right time. The teacher chose peace with the seasons."],["CALM","Take three slow breaths and whisper, “Heavenly Father holds all my times.”"]],"remember":"There’s a right time for everything — a time to play and a time to rest — and Heavenly Father is with you in every one! What is something this is the right time for tonight?","ask":["“What are some of the times the teacher talked about?”","“Who is with us in every season and time?”","“What is something this is the right time for tonight?”"],"share":["When did trusting Heavenly Father’s timing bring you peace? Share a season you’re grateful you waited through.","How do you help your child accept that feelings and seasons pass? Tell what steadies you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor live with patient trust in Heavenly Father’s timing? Describe their steadiness."]},"b9-w2-n6":{"story":["The wise teacher of Ecclesiastes had one very special piece of advice, just for young people like you.","“Remember Heavenly Father now, while you are young!” he said. Don’t wait until you’re big and grown up — start loving Him today.","When we’re little, our hearts are soft and ready to learn, like fresh soil ready for seeds. It’s the perfect time to plant love for Heavenly Father deep inside.","Every prayer you say, every story you hear, every kind thing you do helps you know Heavenly Father better — right now, while you’re small.","You don’t have to wait to be a friend of Heavenly Father. You can start today, and grow closer to Him every single day of your happy life."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to start the adventure of knowing Heavenly Father early. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s big cousin said, “You’re too little to say prayers by yourself.” But Scout said, “No I’m not — I can start now!” and folded little arms and prayed a sweet, simple prayer all alone. Just like the wise teacher said to remember Heavenly Father while young, Scout discovered you’re never too little to know Him.","feel":"Let’s start now! Point to yourself and say, “I can love Heavenly Father today!” Now plant a pretend seed in your heart and watch it grow tall. Can you show me your heart growing love for Him, starting right now?","stones":[["SPOT","Learning to love Heavenly Father young, we can feel eager and close to Him."],["NAME","That excited-to-begin feeling is called eager."],["FIND","What is something you’re eager to do because you’re growing up?"],["FORK","We can think “I’ll wait till I’m big” — or start knowing Heavenly Father right now. The teacher said to start while young."],["CALM","Fold your arms, breathe slow, and whisper, “I love You now, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"You don’t have to wait until you’re grown up to know Heavenly Father — you can start today, while you’re little! What is one way you can grow closer to Heavenly Father right now?","ask":["“When did the wise teacher say to remember Heavenly Father?”","“Are you ever too little to love Heavenly Father?”","“What is one way you can grow closer to Heavenly Father right now?”"],"share":["What helped you begin to know Heavenly Father, and when did it take root? Share your beginning.","What early habit of faith do you most hope your child plants now? Tell why it matters.","Did a grandparent or ancestor come to Heavenly Father young and stay close a lifetime? Describe their early faith."]},"b9-w2-n7":{"story":["Wise Solomon shared one more treasure: he said a good name is better than piles of gold and silver.","What is a “good name”? It means being someone people can trust — someone honest, kind, and true. When you do good, your name becomes a good name.","Solomon knew that money and toys come and go. But being a good, honest person is a treasure that lasts forever.","Every time you tell the truth, keep a promise, or do the right thing, you’re building your good name — like adding a shiny brick to a strong, tall wall.","Heavenly Father is so happy when we choose to be honest and good. A good name is one of the best treasures of all, and you can build yours every day."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he chooses to be honest, even when it’s hard. In Beau’s world today: Beau accidentally broke a little toy while playing. No one saw. He could have stayed quiet — but his tummy felt yucky. So he took a brave breath and told the truth: “I broke it, and I’m sorry.” His mom hugged him and said, “Thank you for being honest.” Just like Solomon said a good name is a treasure, Beau built his good name by telling the truth.","feel":"Let’s build a good name! Stand up tall and proud, put your hand on your heart, and say, “I will be honest and good!” Now stack pretend bricks, one on top of another. Can you build your strong, good name nice and tall?","stones":[["SPOT","Choosing to be truthful, Beau felt a little nervous but good inside."],["NAME","That did-the-right-thing feeling is called honest."],["FIND","When have you told the truth, even though it felt a little hard?"],["FORK","We can fib to feel safe — or tell the truth and build a good name. Solomon taught that being honest is worth more than gold."],["CALM","Hand on your heart, take a strong breath, and say, “I will be honest and good.”"]],"remember":"A good name — being honest, kind, and true — is a treasure worth more than gold, and you build it every time you do right! When could it be brave to tell the truth this week?","ask":["“What did Solomon say is better than gold and silver?”","“How do we build a good name?”","“When could it be brave to tell the truth this week?”"],"share":["When did integrity cost you something but prove more valuable than gold? Share it.","How do you help your child treasure honesty over convenience? Tell about a moment truth mattered.","Did a grandparent or ancestor leave a legacy of a “good name”? Describe the integrity they handed down."]},"b9-w3-n1":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father sent a prophet named Isaiah to share His messages. One of Isaiah’s most beautiful messages was full of love and hope.","Sometimes people made wrong choices, and their hearts felt dark and heavy, like a shirt with big red stains.","But Heavenly Father said something wonderful through Isaiah: “Come, let’s talk it over. Even if your mistakes are as red as scarlet, I can make you as white and clean as fresh snow!”","Isn’t that amazing? When we’re truly sorry and come to Heavenly Father, He washes our hearts pure and clean — whiter than the whitest snow.","No mistake is too big for Heavenly Father’s love. He always welcomes us back and makes us clean and new. That is His happy promise to you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she knows the gift of being made clean and new. In Willow’s world today: Willow said an unkind word to her friend and felt a heavy, yucky feeling inside. She went back, gave a hug, and said, “I’m sorry — that wasn’t kind.” Right away her heart felt light and clean, like fresh snow. Just like Heavenly Father’s promise to make our hearts white as snow, Willow found that saying sorry brings a clean, happy heart.","feel":"Let’s think of clean, white snow! Wiggle your fingers down slowly like gentle snowflakes falling, then brush your hands over your heart — all clean! Can you show me your fresh, snowy-clean heart?","stones":[["SPOT","Before, the heart felt heavy — after Heavenly Father’s washing, it feels clean."],["NAME","That fresh, all-better feeling is called clean."],["FIND","When have you felt fresh and happy after making something right?"],["FORK","A heavy heart can stay stuck and hide — or come to Heavenly Father to be made clean. Isaiah shared His promise to wash us white as snow."],["CALM","Brush your hands over your heart, breathe out, and whisper, “Make me clean, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"No mistake is too big — when you’re sorry, Heavenly Father washes your heart white as snow, clean and new! What helps you feel better after you’ve made a mistake?","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father promise to make our hearts like?”","“Is any mistake too big for Heavenly Father’s love?”","“What helps you feel better after you’ve made a mistake?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father’s cleansing love give you a true fresh start? Share that relief.","How do you teach your child that repentance is gentle and hopeful, not scary? Tell of His mercy to you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor find peace in being made clean by Heavenly Father? Describe their “white as snow” story."]},"b9-w3-n2":{"story":["One day, the prophet Isaiah had an amazing experience. In a beautiful vision, he saw Heavenly Father’s shining throne, high and glorious, filling the temple with light.","Bright angels flew all around, singing, “Holy, holy, holy!” Everything felt so wonderful and full of Heavenly Father’s love.","Isaiah felt very small, but an angel gently touched his lips with something warm and bright from the altar. Right away, Isaiah felt clean and ready, and his worries floated away.","Then Isaiah heard Heavenly Father ask, “Who will go and share My message with My people?” Isaiah didn’t wait even a moment.","“Here am I! Send me!” Isaiah said bravely. He was ready and willing to help Heavenly Father. When Heavenly Father needs a helper, we can be brave and say, “Send me!” too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"","feel":"Let’s be a willing helper like Isaiah! Raise your hand up high like you’re volunteering, and say big and brave, “Here am I — send me!” Can you show me how ready you are to help?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah felt small at first, then brave and willing to help."],["NAME","That ready-to-help feeling is called willing."],["FIND","When have you felt happy and ready to be a helper?"],["FORK","We can feel too shy and stay quiet — or bravely say, “Send me!” Isaiah chose to be a willing helper for Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Take a strong breath and whisper, “I’m ready to help, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"When there’s good work to do, you can be brave and willing like Isaiah and say, “Here am I — send me!” What is a good way you could be a helper tomorrow?","ask":["“What did the angels sing in Isaiah’s vision?”","“What did Isaiah say when Heavenly Father needed a helper?”","“What is a good way you could be a helper tomorrow?”"],"share":["When did you say “here am I” to Heavenly Father, even feeling unready? Share what happened.","How do you encourage your child to volunteer and serve willingly? Tell about a time you stepped up.","Did a grandparent or ancestor answer a call to serve Heavenly Father? Describe their willing heart."]},"b9-w3-n3":{"story":["Isaiah shared a happy message about a special place: Heavenly Father’s holy house, the temple.","“In the last days,” Isaiah said, “Heavenly Father’s house will stand up high on a mountain, and people from everywhere will want to go there!”","“Come,” the people will say to each other. “Let’s go up to Heavenly Father’s house! He will teach us His good ways, and we will walk in His paths.”","The temple is a peaceful, holy place where we feel very close to Heavenly Father. It’s like His special home, full of light and quiet love.","We can love going to Heavenly Father’s house too. Whether it’s the temple or church, it’s a wonderful place to feel Him near and learn His gentle ways."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves the journey to special places. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s family drove to see the temple, all lit up and beautiful. Scout pressed against the window and gasped, “It’s like a castle of light!” On the peaceful grounds, Scout felt happy and calm and close to Heavenly Father. Just like Isaiah’s message about going up to Heavenly Father’s house, Scout discovered how special it feels to be near His holy home.","feel":"Let’s go up to Heavenly Father’s house! March your feet up a pretend hill — up, up, up — then fold your arms reverently and whisper, “It’s so peaceful here.” Can you show me your quiet, reverent face inside His house?","stones":[["SPOT","The people in Isaiah’s message felt eager and glad to go to Heavenly Father’s house."],["NAME","That excited — and-peaceful feeling is called eager."],["FIND","When have you felt excited to go somewhere special and good?"],["FORK","We can drag our feet about holy places — or be eager to go and feel Heavenly Father near. The people chose, “Let’s go up!”"],["CALM","Fold your arms, breathe slow, and whisper, “I love being near You, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s house is a special, peaceful place where you can feel Him close — and you can be eager to go! How do you feel when you’re in a place that feels close to Heavenly Father?","ask":["“Where did the people want to go up to?”","“What did they say Heavenly Father would teach them?”","“How do you feel when you’re in a place that feels close to Heavenly Father?”"],"share":["When has the temple or a house of worship drawn your heart to Heavenly Father? Share it.","How do you help your child love sacred places and reverence? Tell about a meaningful visit.","Did a grandparent or ancestor cherish the temple or Heavenly Father’s house? Describe their devotion."]},"b9-w3-n4":{"story":["Isaiah shared the happiest secret of all — a promise about a very special baby who would come someday.","“Unto us a child will be born!” Isaiah said. “And He will be wonderful. He will be called the Prince of Peace!”","This special baby was Jesus, Heavenly Father’s own Son. Isaiah told about Him long, long before He was born, because Heavenly Father wanted His people to have hope.","Jesus would be a mighty helper, full of love and peace. He would show everyone the way back to Heavenly Father.","Even a long time ago, Heavenly Father was planning something wonderful for His children. He always keeps His promises — and the promise of Jesus was the most wonderful of all."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she loves to wonder about Heavenly Father’s beautiful plans. In Wren’s world today: Wren saw her aunt getting ready for a new baby and wondered, “Isn’t it amazing how Heavenly Father plans good things before they even happen?” She thought about how He promised baby Jesus long ago too. Just like Isaiah wondered at the promise of the Prince of Peace, Wren marveled at Heavenly Father’s wonderful, hope-filled plans.","feel":"Let’s think about the happy promise! Rock your arms gently like you’re holding a sweet baby, and whisper softly, “The Prince of Peace is coming.” Can you show me how gently we hold something precious?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah felt full of hope about the special baby who was promised."],["NAME","That good-things-are-coming feeling is called hopeful."],["FIND","When have you felt hopeful and excited about something good coming?"],["FORK","We can forget Heavenly Father’s promises — or hold onto them with hope. Isaiah held onto the hope of the Prince of Peace."],["CALM","Rock your arms gently, breathe slow, and whisper, “Thank You for Jesus, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Long ago, Heavenly Father promised a special baby — Jesus, the Prince of Peace — because He loves you and wanted you to have hope! What good thing are you feeling hopeful about?","ask":["“What did Isaiah say the special baby would be called?”","“Who was the special baby that was promised?”","“What good thing are you feeling hopeful about?”"],"share":["When has the hope of the Savior carried you through a hard time? Share that hope.","How do you help your child anticipate and treasure Jesus? Tell about a way you point to Him.","Did a grandparent or ancestor testify of Jesus with great hope? Describe the faith they shared."]},"b9-w3-n5":{"story":["Isaiah shared a dream of a beautiful, peaceful world that Heavenly Father is preparing — a world where nothing is scary and everyone is safe.","In this happy place, the wolf and the little lamb lie down together as friends. The lion and the calf rest side by side. No one is afraid anymore!","And guess who leads them all? A little child! “A little child shall lead them,” Isaiah said. Even the smallest one is safe and gentle there.","In Heavenly Father’s peaceful world, no one gets hurt and no one is mean. Everything is filled with kindness, safety, and love.","Heavenly Father wants us to feel that peace even now. When we’re kind and gentle, we bring a little bit of His peaceful world right into our homes today."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she loves to make peace and help everyone feel safe. In Willow’s world today: at the park, a big kid and a little kid were both grabbing the same swing and starting to fuss. Willow gently said, “You can both have a turn — I’ll help count!” Soon they were laughing together. Just like Isaiah’s peaceful world where the lamb and lion are friends, Willow helped make a little pocket of peace.","feel":"Let’s make a peaceful world! Pretend to pet a soft, gentle lamb, then a big friendly lion, right next to each other. Now give a calm, happy sigh — “aahh.” Can you show me your most peaceful, gentle face?","stones":[["SPOT","In Isaiah’s peaceful world, everyone feels safe and calm."],["NAME","That safe, gentle, nothing-scary feeling is called peaceful."],["FIND","When do you feel most peaceful and safe?"],["FORK","We can be rough and grabby — or gentle and kind like Heavenly Father’s peaceful world. Isaiah showed us the way of peace."],["CALM","Take a slow, gentle breath and whisper, “Help me be peaceful, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is preparing a peaceful world where everyone is safe — and you can bring a little of that peace today by being kind and gentle! How could you help someone feel peaceful and safe tomorrow?","ask":["“Who lies down together as friends in Isaiah’s peaceful world?”","“Who leads them all?”","“How could you help someone feel peaceful and safe tomorrow?”"],"share":["When have you glimpsed Heavenly Father’s peace in the middle of an unpeaceful world? Share it.","How do you cultivate a home of gentleness and safety? Tell about a peacemaking moment.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a deep, calming peace? Describe the gentleness they modeled."]},"b9-w3-n6":{"story":["After all his wonderful messages, Isaiah sang a happy song of trust. It’s the song this whole week is named after!","“Heavenly Father saves me!” Isaiah sang. “I will trust in Him, and I will not be afraid.”","Isaiah knew that when Heavenly Father is with us, we don’t have to be scared. He is our strength when we feel weak, and He is our song when we feel sad.","“Heavenly Father is my strength and my song,” Isaiah sang joyfully. Even in hard times, Isaiah chose to trust instead of being afraid.","You can sing Isaiah’s song too! When something feels scary, you can remember: Heavenly Father is with me, so I will trust Him and not be afraid."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he trusts Heavenly Father instead of being afraid. In Beau’s world today: there was a big thunderstorm, and the loud booms made Beau’s heart jump. But he took a deep breath and remembered Isaiah’s song. “Heavenly Father is with me — I will trust and not be afraid,” he whispered, and he even hummed a little tune until the storm passed. Just like Isaiah sang about trusting and not fearing, Beau turned his scared feeling into a brave, trusting one.","feel":"Let’s sing a brave, trusting song! Stand up tall, put your hands on your heart, and say, “I will trust and not be afraid!” Now hum a happy, brave little tune. Can you show me your bravest, most trusting face?","stones":[["SPOT","When something felt scary, Isaiah chose to trust instead of being afraid."],["NAME","That Heavenly-Father-is-with-me feeling is called trusting."],["FIND","When has something felt scary, and you wanted to feel brave?"],["FORK","A scary feeling can grow bigger and bigger — or we can trust Heavenly Father and feel brave. Isaiah chose to trust and not be afraid."],["CALM","Hand on your heart, take a brave breath, and say, “I will trust and not be afraid.”"]],"remember":"When something feels scary, you can sing Isaiah’s song — “Heavenly Father is with me, so I will trust and not be afraid!” What could you remember the next time you feel scared?","ask":["“What did Isaiah say he would NOT be?”","“What did Isaiah say Heavenly Father was to him?”","“What could you remember the next time you feel scared?”"],"share":["When did trusting Heavenly Father dissolve your fear? Share the before and after.","How do you help your child move from fear to trust? Tell about a fear He helped you face.","Did a grandparent or ancestor face frightening times with trust in Heavenly Father? Describe their courage."]},"b9-w3-n7":{"story":["Isaiah’s happy song had one more joyful part. He sang about drawing up joy like cool water from a deep, wonderful well.","“With joy you will draw water from the wells of Heavenly Father’s saving love!” Isaiah sang. Imagine a bucket coming up full — not of water, but of happiness!","When we remember all the good things Heavenly Father does, our hearts fill up with joy, splashing over like a fresh, cool drink on a hot day.","And Isaiah didn’t want to keep the joy to himself. “Tell everyone!” he sang. “Sing about the wonderful things Heavenly Father has done!”","You can draw up joy too, just by remembering Heavenly Father’s love — and then share it with everyone, so the happiness spreads and spreads."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to find joy and share it with everyone. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s grandma got better after being sick, and Scout was so happy and grateful. Scout couldn’t hold the joy in — running to tell everyone, “Heavenly Father helped Grandma feel better!” Soon the whole family was smiling and thankful. Just like Isaiah sang about drawing up joy and telling everyone, Scout discovered that shared joy grows even bigger.","feel":"Let’s draw up joy from the well! Pretend to pull a rope hand over hand — up, up — then lift the happy bucket high and cheer, “Joy!” Now splash the joy to someone you love. Can you draw up your happiest bucket of joy?","stones":[["SPOT","Remembering Heavenly Father’s love, Isaiah felt his heart fill up with joy."],["NAME","That overflowing, happy-hearted feeling is called joyful."],["FIND","When has remembering something good filled you up with happiness?"],["FORK","Joy can stay small and quiet inside — or we can draw it up and share it. Isaiah chose to fill up with joy and tell everyone."],["CALM","Take a happy breath and think of one good thing Heavenly Father has done for you."]],"remember":"You can fill your heart with joy just by remembering Heavenly Father’s love — and then share it so the happiness grows! What good thing about Heavenly Father could you share with someone tomorrow?","ask":["“What did Isaiah say we would draw up with joy?”","“What did Isaiah want everyone to do with the good news?”","“What good thing about Heavenly Father could you share with someone tomorrow?”"],"share":["When did drawing on Heavenly Father’s goodness fill an empty place with joy? Share it.","How do you help your child both feel and share joy in Heavenly Father? Tell about a time you testified.","Did a grandparent or ancestor overflow with joyful gratitude to Heavenly Father? Describe their well of joy."]},"b9-w4-n1":{"story":["Isaiah shared a message so tender and kind that it feels like a warm hug from Heavenly Father.","“Someday,” Isaiah said, “Heavenly Father will wipe away the tears from every face. There will be no more crying, and everyone will be happy!”","Imagine Heavenly Father gently drying your tears with the softest, most loving touch, whispering, “It’s okay now, My child. Everything is going to be all right.”","Heavenly Father sees every tear we cry. He cares about every sad feeling, and He loves to comfort us and turn our sadness into joy.","Even when we feel sad today, we can remember Heavenly Father’s promise: He wipes away tears, and He fills our hearts with comfort and love."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she loves to comfort and dry tears. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s little cousin was crying because she missed her mommy. Willow didn’t have a way to fix it, but she gently wiped her cousin’s tears and gave her a hug and sat close until she felt better. Just like Heavenly Father’s promise to wipe away every tear, Willow brought comfort with her gentle, loving care.","feel":"Let’s feel Heavenly Father’s comfort! Pretend to gently wipe a tear from your cheek, then give yourself a soft, warm hug. “It’s going to be okay.” Can you show me a sad face turning into a comforted, peaceful one?","stones":[["SPOT","When we’re sad, Heavenly Father promises to comfort us and dry our tears."],["NAME","That better-now, cared-for feeling is called comforted."],["FIND","When has a gentle hug or kind touch helped you feel better after crying?"],["FORK","A sad feeling can stay heavy — or we can let Heavenly Father comfort us and dry our tears. Isaiah shared His promise of comfort."],["CALM","Wipe a pretend tear, breathe out slow, and whisper, “Thank You for comforting me, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father sees every tear and promises to wipe them all away — He loves to comfort you! Who can give you a comforting hug when you feel sad?","ask":["“What did Isaiah say Heavenly Father would wipe away?”","“What does Heavenly Father do when we feel sad?”","“Who can give you a comforting hug when you feel sad?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father comfort you in a season of tears? Share how He met you.","How do you help your child bring their sadness to Heavenly Father? Tell about a comfort He gave you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor find deep comfort in Heavenly Father through sorrow? Describe their solace."]},"b9-w4-n2":{"story":["Isaiah shared a lovely secret about how to feel calm and peaceful inside, even when the world is busy.","“Heavenly Father will keep you in perfect peace,” Isaiah said, “when you keep your mind on Him.”","That means when we fill our thoughts with Heavenly Father — thinking about His love, remembering His stories — our hearts grow calm and quiet and peaceful.","It’s like when your mind is buzzing with worries, and then you think one gentle thought about Heavenly Father, and the buzzing settles into soft, warm peace.","Heavenly Father wants to give you peace. When your thoughts turn to Him, He fills you up with a calm, safe, wonderful peace — the perfect kind."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she knows that thinking about Heavenly Father brings peace. In Wren’s world today: Wren felt worried about her first day at a new class — her thoughts were spinning fast. So she closed her eyes and thought about how Heavenly Father loved her and was with her. Slowly, her spinning thoughts grew calm and peaceful. Just like Isaiah said keeping our minds on Heavenly Father brings perfect peace, Wren found calm by thinking of Him.","feel":"Let’s find perfect peace! Put your hands on the sides of your head, then slowly move them down as you take a deep breath, thinking one calm thought about Heavenly Father. Can you show me your peaceful, settled face?","stones":[["SPOT","When Isaiah kept his mind on Heavenly Father, he felt perfect peace."],["NAME","That calm, settled, quiet feeling is called peaceful."],["FIND","When do your thoughts feel busy or worried, and you need to feel calm?"],["FORK","Worried thoughts can spin faster and faster — or we can think of Heavenly Father and find peace. Isaiah kept his mind on Him."],["CALM","Take three slow breaths and think one gentle thought about Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"When your thoughts feel busy or worried, you can think about Heavenly Father — and He fills you with perfect peace! What happy thought about Heavenly Father could you think when you feel worried?","ask":["“How do we get Heavenly Father’s perfect peace?”","“What happens when we keep our minds on Heavenly Father?”","“What happy thought about Heavenly Father could you think when you feel worried?”"],"share":["When did fixing your mind on Heavenly Father bring peace amid chaos? Share your practice.","How do you teach your child to redirect anxious thoughts toward Heavenly Father? Tell what helps you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a settled, peaceful mind rooted in Heavenly Father? Describe their calm."]},"b9-w4-n3":{"story":["Isaiah taught that Heavenly Father is like the strongest, most reliable rock you could ever build on.","“Trust in Heavenly Father forever,” Isaiah said, “for He is like a Rock that lasts always and always.”","He also said Heavenly Father is like a precious, sure cornerstone — the special, strong stone builders use to hold a whole house together, so it never falls.","When we build our lives on Heavenly Father, we’re standing on something that will never wobble, crack, or crumble. He is steady and strong forever.","No matter what happens around us, Heavenly Father holds us firm. When we trust in Him, we are safe and steady, standing on the strongest Rock of all."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he stands strong on Heavenly Father. In Beau’s world today: Beau was learning to balance on the low wall at the park, and his legs felt wobbly and unsure. He remembered that Heavenly Father was his strong Rock, holding him steady. He took a brave breath, steadied himself, and walked the whole way! Just like Isaiah said to trust Heavenly Father, the Rock that lasts forever, Beau found his steady footing by leaning on Him.","feel":"Let’s stand on the strong Rock! Plant your feet wide, make your body big and firm, and don’t wobble — steady as a rock! Now say, “Heavenly Father is my strong Rock!” Can you stand as steady and sure as stone?","stones":[["SPOT","Trusting Heavenly Father, the Rock, we can feel steady and safe."],["NAME","That firm, won’t-wobble feeling is called steady."],["FIND","When have you felt wobbly and needed something strong to hold onto?"],["FORK","A wobbly feeling can tip us over — or we can stand firm on Heavenly Father, our Rock. Isaiah taught us to trust Him forever."],["CALM","Plant your feet firm, breathe in, and stand steady like a strong rock."]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is your sure, strong Rock — when you trust in Him, you can stand steady and safe forever! When could you lean on Heavenly Father to feel steady?","ask":["“What strong, steady thing did Isaiah say Heavenly Father is like?”","“What happens when we build our lives on Heavenly Father?”","“When could you lean on Heavenly Father to feel steady?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father prove a steady “Rock” while everything shook? Share it.","How do you help your child find footing on Heavenly Father in unsteady moments? Tell about a time you did.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stand firm on Heavenly Father as their rock? Describe their steadiness."]},"b9-w4-n4":{"story":["This week’s book is named after something Isaiah said Heavenly Father would do: “a marvellous work and a wonder!”","That means Heavenly Father does amazing, surprising, wonderful things — things so good and clever that they make us say, “Wow!”","Isaiah promised that Heavenly Father would do a wonder that would help all His children — teaching and blessing even those who thought they couldn’t learn.","Heavenly Father is full of surprises! He can make wonderful things happen in ways we never even imagined. His works are truly marvelous.","When you see something amazing — a rainbow, a new baby, a kind miracle — you can remember: Heavenly Father is still doing marvelous works and wonders, today and always."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to discover Heavenly Father’s marvelous wonders. In Scout’s world today: after a rainstorm, Scout spotted a huge, bright rainbow arching across the whole sky. “How does Heavenly Father DO that?” Scout gasped, staring in wonder. It felt like a marvelous surprise made just for that moment. Just like Isaiah said Heavenly Father does a marvellous work and a wonder, Scout discovered that His amazing works are everywhere.","feel":"Let’s be amazed at Heavenly Father’s wonders! Open your eyes and mouth w-i-d-e in a big “WOW!” and throw your hands up in surprise. Heavenly Father does marvelous things! Can you show me your most amazed, wonder-filled face?","stones":[["SPOT","Seeing Heavenly Father’s marvelous works, we feel amazed and full of wonder."],["NAME","That big-eyed, “wow!” feeling is called amazed."],["FIND","What is something amazing that made you say “wow”?"],["FORK","We can rush past amazing things — or stop and wonder at Heavenly Father’s marvelous works. Isaiah invites us to be amazed by Him."],["CALM","Take a slow breath, look up, and whisper, “You do marvelous things, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father does marvelous works and wonders — amazing, surprising, wonderful things — today and always! What marvelous thing that Heavenly Father made amazes you most?","ask":["“What did Isaiah say Heavenly Father would do?”","“What do marvelous works make us want to say?”","“What marvelous thing that Heavenly Father made amazes you most?”"],"share":["When did you witness a “marvellous work and a wonder” from Heavenly Father? Share the awe.","How do you help your child stay wonder-filled at Heavenly Father’s works? Tell about a favorite marvel.","Did a grandparent or ancestor witness a wonder or miracle from Heavenly Father? Describe their amazement."]},"b9-w4-n5":{"story":["Isaiah shared a comforting message about how Heavenly Father helps us know which way to go.","“When you’re not sure which way to turn,” Isaiah said, “you will hear a gentle voice behind you saying, ‘This is the way — walk in it.’”","Heavenly Father helps guide us, like a kind whisper in our hearts showing us the right path — the good and safe way to go.","Isaiah also said that being quiet and calm helps us hear Him. “In quietness and trust is your strength,” he taught. When we get still, we can feel Heavenly Father’s gentle guidance.","Whenever you don’t know what’s right, you can get quiet, listen with your heart, and trust Heavenly Father to whisper, “This is the way.” He always helps you find the good path."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she gets quiet and listens with her heart. In Wren’s world today: Wren wasn’t sure whether to share her new crayons or keep them all to herself. Instead of rushing, she got quiet and thought about what was kind. In the calm, she felt a gentle nudge in her heart: sharing is the good way. She shared, and it felt right. Just like Isaiah said we hear “this is the way” when we’re quiet and trusting, Wren listened and found the good path.","feel":"Let’s listen for the way! Cup your hand to your ear and get very quiet… then point ahead and whisper, “This is the way!” Now take a peaceful step forward. Can you show me your best quiet- listening ears?","stones":[["SPOT","Listening quietly, we can feel guided and sure of the right way."],["NAME","That I-know-the-good-way feeling is called guided."],["FIND","When have you gotten quiet and figured out the right thing to do?"],["FORK","We can rush ahead and guess — or get quiet and listen for Heavenly Father’s gentle guidance. Isaiah taught us to listen in quietness."],["CALM","Get very still, take a slow breath, and listen with your heart for the good way."]],"remember":"When you’re not sure what’s right, you can get quiet and listen — Heavenly Father will gently guide you, whispering, “This is the way!” When could you get quiet and listen for the good way?","ask":["“What gentle words does Heavenly Father help us hear?”","“What helps us hear Heavenly Father’s guidance?”","“When could you get quiet and listen for the good way?”"],"share":["When did a quiet prompting from Heavenly Father show you the way? Share how you heard it.","How do you help your child slow down to sense Heavenly Father’s guidance? Tell about a time you did.","Did a grandparent or ancestor follow Heavenly Father’s quiet guidance? Describe their listening faith."]},"b9-w4-n6":{"story":["Isaiah painted the most beautiful picture of hope. He told about a dry, empty desert — and then something wonderful happened!","“The desert will be glad,” Isaiah said, “and the dry, thirsty ground will burst into bloom with flowers! It will blossom like a beautiful rose.”","Imagine a bare, brown place suddenly filling up with soft green grass, splashing streams, and bright, happy flowers everywhere. That’s what Heavenly Father can do!","Heavenly Father can bring beautiful life to even the driest, saddest places. He turns empty into full, and sad into glad, and brown into bloom.","When your heart feels dry or sad, remember: Heavenly Father can make good things bloom again. With Him, there is always hope for something beautiful."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she believes good things can bloom again. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s favorite little plant looked droopy and brown, and she felt sad. But Mom showed her how to water it and set it in the sun. Day by day, it grew green and even sprouted a tiny flower! Willow clapped with joy. Just like Isaiah’s desert that blossomed like a rose, Willow learned that with gentle care and hope, beautiful things can bloom.","feel":"Let’s watch the desert bloom! Crouch down small like dry, cracked ground, then slowly grow up and open your arms wide like a flower bursting into bloom. Can you show me the empty desert blossoming into a beautiful garden?","stones":[["SPOT","Watching the desert bloom, we feel hopeful that good things can grow."],["NAME","That good-is-coming feeling is called hopeful."],["FIND","When has something sad or empty gotten better and happy again?"],["FORK","We can believe dry places stay dry forever — or hope in Heavenly Father, who makes things bloom. Isaiah showed us the desert blossoming."],["CALM","Take a slow breath, open your arms like a flower, and whisper, “You make things bloom, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father can make good things bloom, even in the driest, saddest places — so there’s always hope for something beautiful! What is something you’re hopeful will get better and bloom?","ask":["“What did Isaiah say the dry desert would do?”","“What can Heavenly Father do to dry, sad places?”","“What is something you’re hopeful will get better and bloom?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father bring “bloom” to a dry season of your life? Share that renewal.","How do you nurture hope in your child when things feel bleak or bare? Tell about a bloom you’ve seen.","Did a grandparent or ancestor see Heavenly Father bring new life after a barren time? Describe their hope."]},"b9-w4-n7":{"story":["Isaiah’s beautiful message ended with words of courage and joy — a wonderful way to finish the whole month!","“Be strong, and don’t be afraid,” Isaiah said. “Heavenly Father is coming to help you and save you!”","In Isaiah’s happy picture, wonderful things happen: streams splash through the desert, and everyone who was sad leaps and sings for joy. “Sorrow and sighing fly away!”","Isaiah even told of a special, safe road — a happy path that leads home to Heavenly Father, where His people walk and sing with joy forever.","So be strong and brave, little one! Heavenly Father is always helping you and leading you home. And on the way, He fills our hearts with singing, and turns all our sorrow into joy."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because his courage carries him all the way to joy. In Beau’s world today: Beau felt nervous about riding his bike without training wheels — it seemed so hard and a little scary. But he remembered to be strong and not afraid, took a deep breath, and pushed off. Wobble, wobble — then he was riding! He leaped off, cheering with pure joy. Just like Isaiah said, “Be strong, don’t be afraid,” Beau’s brave heart turned into a happy, joyful one.","feel":"Let’s be strong and joyful! Flex your muscles strong — “I am brave!” — then leap up high and cheer, “Joy!” Sorrow flies away! Can you show me strong and brave, then leaping with joy?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah said to be strong and brave, and then joy comes leaping in."],["NAME","That strong, I-can-do-it feeling is called brave."],["FIND","When have you felt brave and strong doing something hard?"],["FORK","Fear can make us give up — or we can be strong, trust Heavenly Father, and find joy. Isaiah said, “Be strong, don’t be afraid.”"],["CALM","Take a strong, brave breath and whisper, “Heavenly Father makes me strong.”"]],"remember":"Be strong and don’t be afraid — Heavenly Father is always helping you and leading you home, and He turns sorrow into joy! What is something brave and strong Heavenly Father can help you do?","ask":["“What did Isaiah say to be, instead of afraid?”","“What happens to sorrow and sighing in Isaiah’s happy message?”","“What is something brave and strong Heavenly Father can help you do?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father give you strength and turn sorrow into joy? Share that journey.","How do you help your child be strong and unafraid with Heavenly Father’s help? Tell about a brave moment.","Did a grandparent or ancestor walk a hard road with courage and find joy? Describe their strong, singing faith."]}};
